window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2017-26", year: 2017, no: 26, subject: "modern", topic: "Butler Committee",
  q: `The object of the Butler Committee of 1927 was to`,
  opts: ["Define the jurisdiction of the Central and Provincial Governments.", "Define the powers of the Secretary of State for India.", "Impose censorship on national press.", "Improve the relationship between the Government of India and the Indian States."], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Improve the relationship between the Government of India and the Indian States.</h3>
<p>The <b>Indian States Committee</b> (1927–29), chaired by <b>Sir Harcourt Butler</b>, examined relations between the <b>Paramount Power</b> (British Crown) and the <b>princely states</b>, including financial and economic relations. Its report (1929) said <b>paramountcy must remain paramount</b>, that the princes' relationship was with the Crown directly (not with the Government of India), and that states should not be handed over to a responsible Indian government without their consent.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Centre-province jurisdiction was dealt with by the GoI Acts (1919, 1935), not the Butler Committee.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Secretary of State's powers were defined by the 1858 Act and later Acts.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Press censorship came through laws like the Vernacular Press Act (1878), not this committee.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: British policy towards princely states</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Phase</th><th>Policy</th></tr>
<tr><td>1757–1813</td><td>Ring fence (Warren Hastings)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1813–1857</td><td>Subordinate isolation; Subsidiary Alliance (Wellesley); Doctrine of Lapse (Dalhousie)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1858–1935</td><td>Subordinate union; Queen's Proclamation (1858) ended annexation; Chamber of Princes (1921, Montford reforms)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1935–1947</td><td>Equal federation proposed (GoI Act 1935)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>About 565 princely states covered two-fifths of India; integrated after 1947 by Sardar Patel and V.P. Menon through Instruments of Accession and merger agreements. AISPC (All India States People's Conference, 1927) led popular movements in states.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-27", year: 2017, no: 27, subject: "economy", topic: "Domestic Content Requirement (solar)",
  q: `The term 'Domestic Content Requirement' is sometimes seen in the news with reference to`,
  opts: ["Developing solar power production in our country", "Granting licences to foreign T.V. channels in our country", "Exporting our food products to other countries", "Permitting foreign educational institutions to set up their campuses in our country"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Developing solar power production in our country</h3>
<p>Under the <b>Jawaharlal Nehru National Solar Mission</b> (2010), some solar projects had to use Indian-made solar <b>cells and modules</b> (Domestic Content Requirement, DCR). The <b>USA challenged</b> this at the WTO (2013); the WTO panel (2016) and Appellate Body ruled against India, holding it violated <b>GATT national treatment</b> (Art III:4) and the <b>TRIMs Agreement</b>. India then limited DCR to government-owned projects (permitted under the government procurement exception).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> TV channel licences are governed by uplinking/downlinking guidelines (MIB).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Food exports follow APEDA standards, not DCR.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Foreign campuses follow UGC regulations (2023).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Current DCR use: CPSU Scheme Phase II, PM-KUSUM, PM Surya Ghar (rooftop) require domestically made cells and modules.</li>
<li>Other support: <b>ALMM</b> (approved models list), Basic Customs Duty (40% modules, 25% cells from 2022), <b>PLI</b> for high-efficiency modules.</li>
<li>India's own WTO cases against US local content rules in State renewable programmes.</li>
<li>Related TRIMs concepts: local content, trade balancing, export performance requirements (asked 2020 Q56).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-28", year: 2017, no: 28, subject: "ir", topic: "Nuclear security bodies",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Nuclear Security Summits are periodically held under the aegis of the United Nations.</li><li>The International Panel on Fissile Materials is an organ of International Atomic Energy Agency.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the <b>Nuclear Security Summits</b> were a US initiative (President Obama's 2009 Prague speech), not UN-led. Four summits: Washington (2010), Seoul (2012), The Hague (2014), Washington (2016). India attended all.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> the <b>International Panel on Fissile Materials</b> (2006) is an independent group of arms-control experts from many countries, based at Princeton University; it publishes the Global Fissile Material Report. It is not an IAEA organ.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: nuclear security regimes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Convention on the Physical Protection of Nuclear Material</b> (CPPNM, 1980; 2005 amendment in force 2016; India ratified).</li>
<li><b>International Convention for the Suppression of Acts of Nuclear Terrorism</b> (2005, UN; India among the first signatories).</li>
<li><b>UNSC Resolution 1540</b> (2004): stop non-state actors acquiring WMDs.</li>
<li><b>Global Initiative to Combat Nuclear Terrorism</b> (2006, US-Russia).</li>
<li>CTBT (1996, not in force; India not a signatory); proposed FMCT (Fissile Material Cut-off Treaty) stalled at the Conference on Disarmament.</li>
<li>India's Global Centre for Nuclear Energy Partnership (GCNEP), Bahadurgarh, Haryana.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-29", year: 2017, no: 29, subject: "economy", topic: "National Pension System",
  q: `Who among the following can join the National Pension System (NPS)?`,
  opts: ["Resident Indian citizens only", "Persons of age from 21 to 55 only", "All State Government employees joining the services after the date of notification by the respective State Governments", "All Central Government employees including those of Armed Forces joining the services on or after 1st April, 2004"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) All State Government employees joining the services after the date of notification by the respective State Governments</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> wrong: <b>NRIs</b> can also join (and OCIs since 2019).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> wrong: entry age is <b>18 to 70 years</b> (was 18–60, then 65).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> correct: States adopt NPS by notification for employees joining after that date (most States did; some like Rajasthan, Chhattisgarh, Punjab, Himachal reverted to the Old Pension Scheme in 2022–23).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> wrong: NPS is mandatory for Central Government employees joining on or after 1 January 2004, <b>except the Armed Forces</b>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Regulated by <b>PFRDA</b> (PFRDA Act, 2013). Defined contribution scheme: Tier I (pension, restricted withdrawal), Tier II (voluntary savings).</li>
<li>At exit (60): at least 40% must buy an annuity; up to 60% lump sum tax-free.</li>
<li><b>Unified Pension Scheme</b> (UPS, approved August 2024, from April 2025): assured pension of 50% of average basic pay of the last 12 months for 25+ years' service; an option for central employees under NPS.</li>
<li><b>Atal Pension Yojana</b> (2015): for unorganised sector, ages 18–40, guaranteed pension of ₹1,000–5,000. <b>NPS Vatsalya</b> (2024) for minors.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-30", year: 2017, no: 30, subject: "geography", topic: "Teesta river",
  q: `With reference to river Teesta, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The source of river Teesta is the same as that of Brahmaputra but it flows through Sikkim.</li><li>River Rangeet originates in Sikkim and it is a tributary of river Teesta.</li><li>River Teesta flows into Bay of Bengal on the border of India and Bangladesh.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the Teesta rises from <b>Tso Lhamo (Cho Lhamu) lake</b> and the <b>Zemu glacier / Khangse glacier</b> area in North Sikkim. The Brahmaputra (Yarlung Tsangpo) rises from the <b>Chemayungdung glacier</b> near Mansarovar in Tibet.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the <b>Rangeet</b> rises from the Rathong glacier in West Sikkim and joins the Teesta near the Sikkim–West Bengal border (Melli / Triveni).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> the Teesta flows through Sikkim, West Bengal (Jalpaiguri) and into <b>Bangladesh</b>, where it joins the <b>Brahmaputra (Jamuna)</b>; it does not flow into the Bay of Bengal.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Teesta water sharing</b>: India–Bangladesh agreement drafted in 2011 but not signed (West Bengal objected). China offered Bangladesh a Teesta restoration project.</li>
<li>Hydro projects: Teesta III (Chungthang) dam was destroyed by the <b>October 2023 South Lhonak GLOF</b> in North Sikkim.</li>
<li>Other Sikkim rivers: Lachen and Lachung (form the Teesta at Chungthang), Rangpo.</li>
<li>Brahmaputra tributaries: Subansiri, Jia Bharali (Kameng), Dhansiri, Manas, Teesta (right bank, in Bangladesh), Lohit, Dibang, Burhi Dihing.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-31", year: 2017, no: 31, subject: "science", topic: "Zika virus",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>In tropical regions, Zika virus disease is transmitted by the same mosquito that transmits dengue.</li><li>Sexual transmission of Zika virus disease is possible.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1:</b> Zika is spread mainly by <b>Aedes aegypti</b> (and Aedes albopictus), the day-biting mosquito that also spreads <b>dengue, chikungunya and yellow fever</b>.</li>
<li><b>2:</b> it can also spread through <b>sexual contact</b>, from mother to foetus, and through blood transfusion.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Flavivirus (RNA), first found in monkeys in Uganda's Zika forest (1947). WHO declared a Public Health Emergency of International Concern in February 2016 (Brazil outbreak).</li>
<li>Infection in pregnancy causes <b>microcephaly</b> in babies; in adults, linked to Guillain-Barré syndrome. No vaccine or specific treatment.</li>
<li>India's first cases: Gujarat (2017); outbreaks in Rajasthan (2018), Kerala (2021), Maharashtra (2024).</li>
</ul>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Mosquito</th><th>Diseases</th></tr>
<tr><td>Aedes</td><td>Dengue, chikungunya, Zika, yellow fever</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anopheles (female)</td><td>Malaria</td></tr>
<tr><td>Culex</td><td>Japanese encephalitis, West Nile, filariasis</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2017-32", year: 2017, no: 32, subject: "economy", topic: "BIS mark and AGMARK",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Standard Mark of Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) is mandatory for automotive tyres and tubes.</li><li>AGMARK is a quality Certification Mark issued by the Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO).</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> under mandatory certification (Quality Control Orders), the <b>ISI mark</b> is compulsory for automotive tyres and tubes, along with items like cement, LPG cylinders, electrical appliances, packaged drinking water, helmets, toys (2021).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> <b>AGMARK</b> is issued by the <b>Directorate of Marketing and Inspection</b> (Ministry of Agriculture) under the Agricultural Produce (Grading and Marking) Act, 1937, not the FAO.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: certification marks in India</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Mark</th><th>For</th><th>Body</th></tr>
<tr><td>ISI</td><td>Industrial products</td><td>BIS (BIS Act 2016)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hallmark</td><td>Gold and silver jewellery (mandatory for gold since 2021, HUID)</td><td>BIS</td></tr>
<tr><td>AGMARK</td><td>Agricultural produce</td><td>DMI</td></tr>
<tr><td>FPO mark</td><td>Processed fruit products (now under FSSAI)</td><td>FSSAI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ecomark</td><td>Environment-friendly products (revamped 2024)</td><td>CPCB/BIS</td></tr>
<tr><td>BEE star label</td><td>Energy efficiency</td><td>Bureau of Energy Efficiency</td></tr>
<tr><td>India Organic, Jaivik Bharat</td><td>Organic food</td><td>APEDA, FSSAI</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2017-33", year: 2017, no: 33, subject: "economy", topic: "e-NAM",
  q: `What is/are the advantage/advantages of implementing the 'National Agriculture Market' scheme?
<ol><li>It is a pan-India electronic trading portal for agricultural commodities.</li><li>It provides the farmers access to nationwide market, with prices commensurate with the quality of their produce.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<p><b>e-NAM</b> (launched 14 April 2016; implemented by <b>SFAC</b>, Ministry of Agriculture) is a pan-India <b>electronic trading portal</b> networking existing APMC mandis into a unified national market. Farmers get transparent price discovery through online bidding, quality-based prices (assaying labs), and online payment.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> e-NAM is a pan-India electronic trading portal linking APMC mandis.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> It gives farmers access to a wider market with quality-based (assayed) prices.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>About 1,400+ mandis in 23 States and 4 UTs integrated (2024–25); traders, FPOs and warehouses (e-NWR) linked; platform of platforms.</li>
<li>Reform prerequisites for States: single trading licence valid across the State, single-point levy of market fee, e-auction.</li>
<li>Agricultural marketing is a <b>State subject</b>. <b>Model APLM Act, 2017</b>. The three farm laws (2020) were repealed in November 2021.</li>
<li>Related: <b>ONDC</b> for farm produce, FPOs (10,000 FPO scheme), Gramin Agricultural Markets (GrAMs), Agri Infrastructure Fund.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-34", year: 2017, no: 34, subject: "economy", topic: "National IPR Policy",
  q: `With reference to the 'National Intellectual Property Rights Policy', consider the following statements:
<ol><li>It reiterates India's commitment to the Doha Development Agenda and the TRIPS Agreement.</li><li>Department of Industrial Policy and Promotion is the nodal agency for regulating intellectual property rights in India.</li></ol>
Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the <b>National IPR Policy (May 2016)</b>, "Creative India; Innovative India", reaffirms India's commitment to the WTO's <b>Doha Development Agenda</b> and the <b>TRIPS Agreement</b>, while using TRIPS flexibilities (public health).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>DIPP</b> (now <b>DPIIT</b>) was made the <b>nodal department</b> for all IPR matters; copyright (from Ministry of Culture) and semiconductor layout designs (from MeitY) were moved to it in 2016.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: seven objectives of the policy</h3>
<ol>
<li>IPR awareness, outreach and promotion.</li>
<li>Generation of IPRs.</li>
<li>Legal and legislative framework.</li>
<li>Administration and management (modernise IP offices).</li>
<li>Commercialisation of IPRs.</li>
<li>Enforcement and adjudication.</li>
<li>Human capital development.</li>
</ol>
<p>Cell for IPR Promotion and Management (CIPAM) under DPIIT. Doha Declaration on TRIPS and Public Health (2001) affirms compulsory licensing (India's first compulsory licence: Natco for Bayer's Nexavar, 2012).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-35", year: 2017, no: 35, subject: "environment", topic: "Schedule I animals",
  q: `According to the Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972, which of the following animals cannot be hunted by any person except under some provisions provided by law?
<ol><li>Gharial</li><li>Indian wild ass</li><li>Wild buffalo</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<p>All three were in <b>Schedule I</b> (highest protection). Under <b>Section 9</b>, hunting of any wild animal in Schedules I–IV is prohibited, except as allowed under Sections 11 (danger to human life, with Chief Wildlife Warden's permission) and 12 (education, scientific research, scientific management, collection for zoos).</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: the three species</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Species</th><th>IUCN</th><th>Where</th></tr>
<tr><td>Gharial</td><td>Critically Endangered</td><td><b>National Chambal Sanctuary</b> (MP, Rajasthan, UP), Katarniaghat, Son Gharial Sanctuary; Gandak (Bihar)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indian wild ass (khur)</td><td>Near Threatened</td><td><b>Little Rann of Kutch</b> (Wild Ass Sanctuary, Gujarat)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wild water buffalo</td><td>Endangered</td><td>Kaziranga, Manas, Dibru-Saikhowa; Chhattisgarh (State animal; Udanti-Sitanadi)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>After the 2022 amendment, Schedule I lists the most protected animals; Schedule II lesser protected; Schedule III plants; Schedule IV CITES species.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-36", year: 2017, no: 36, subject: "polity", topic: "Fundamental Duties",
  q: `Which of the following statements is/are true of the Fundamental Duties of an Indian citizen?
<ol><li>A legislative process has been provided to enforce these duties.</li><li>They are correlative to legal duties.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the Constitution provides <b>no legislative process or mechanism</b> to enforce Fundamental Duties; they are non-justiciable. Parliament may enforce particular duties through ordinary laws (e.g. Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act, 1971; Wild Life Act; Environment Protection Act), but this is not a constitutional enforcement process.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> they are moral and civic duties, not correlative to legal duties.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Added by the <b>42nd Amendment (1976)</b> on the <b>Swaran Singh Committee</b> recommendation; <b>Article 51A</b>, Part IVA; inspired by the USSR Constitution.</li>
<li>Originally <b>10</b> duties; the 11th (51A(k): parents to provide education to children aged 6–14) added by the <b>86th Amendment (2002)</b>.</li>
<li>Apply only to citizens (unlike some FRs).</li>
<li><b>Verma Committee (1999)</b> listed laws that give effect to some duties.</li>
<li>Courts use them to uphold the reasonableness of laws (AIIMS Students' Union, 2001).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-37", year: 2017, no: 37, subject: "modern", topic: "Pre-Congress associations",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<ol><li>Radhakanta Deb — First President of the British Indian Association</li><li>Gazulu Lakshminarasu Chetty — Founder of the Madras Mahajana Sabha</li><li>Surendranath Banerjee — Founder of the Indian Association</li></ol>
Which of the above pairs is/are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> <b>Raja Radhakanta Deb</b> was the first president of the <b>British Indian Association</b> (1851, Calcutta; formed by merging the Landholders' Society and the Bengal British India Society). Debendranath Tagore was secretary.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> <b>Gazulu Lakshminarasu Chetty</b> founded the <b>Madras Native Association</b> (1852) and the newspaper Crescent. The <b>Madras Mahajana Sabha</b> (1884) was founded by <b>M. Veeraraghavachariar, G. Subramania Iyer and P. Anandacharlu</b>.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> <b>Surendranath Banerjea</b> and Anandamohan Bose founded the <b>Indian Association</b> (1876, Calcutta), which held the Indian National Conference (1883, 1885).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: pre-Congress political bodies</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Organisation</th><th>Founder, year</th></tr>
<tr><td>Landholders' Society</td><td>Dwarkanath Tagore, 1838</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bombay Association</td><td>Jagannath Shankarseth, 1852</td></tr>
<tr><td>East India Association (London)</td><td>Dadabhai Naoroji, 1866</td></tr>
<tr><td>Poona Sarvajanik Sabha</td><td>M.G. Ranade and others, 1870</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indian League</td><td>Sisir Kumar Ghosh, 1875</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bombay Presidency Association</td><td>Pherozeshah Mehta, K.T. Telang, Badruddin Tyabji, 1885</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2017-38", year: 2017, no: 38, subject: "polity", topic: "Preamble: liberty",
  q: `Which one of the following objectives is not embodied in the Preamble to the Constitution of India?`,
  opts: ["Liberty of thought", "Economic liberty", "Liberty of expression", "Liberty of belief"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Economic liberty</h3>
<p>The Preamble promises <b>Liberty of thought, expression, belief, faith and worship</b>. It does not mention "economic liberty". (It promises <b>economic justice</b>, along with social and political justice.)</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(c)</b> Liberty of expression is in the Preamble.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Liberty of belief is in the Preamble.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Preamble text</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Justice</b>: social, economic and political (from the Russian Revolution).</li>
<li><b>Liberty</b>: of thought, expression, belief, faith and worship (French Revolution ideal; US influence).</li>
<li><b>Equality</b>: of status and of opportunity.</li>
<li><b>Fraternity</b>: assuring the dignity of the individual and the unity and integrity of the Nation ("integrity" added in 1976).</li>
</ul>
<p>Liberty is protected through Articles 19–22 and 25–28. The Preamble's "liberty" is positive liberty: conditions for full personality development (2019 Q85).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-39", year: 2017, no: 39, subject: "economy", topic: "Quality Council of India",
  q: `With reference to 'Quality Council of India (QCI)', consider the following statements:
<ol><li>QCI was set up jointly by the Government of India and the Indian Industry.</li><li>Chairman of QCI is appointed by the Prime Minister on the recommendations of the industry to the Government.</li></ol>
Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> QCI was set up in <b>1997</b> as a public-private partnership between the Government of India and Indian industry represented by <b>ASSOCHAM, CII and FICCI</b>; registered as a non-profit society. Nodal ministry: DPIIT (Commerce and Industry).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> its Chairman is appointed by the <b>Prime Minister</b> on the recommendation of industry to the government.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: QCI's boards</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Board</th><th>Role</th></tr>
<tr><td>NABCB</td><td>Accreditation of certification and inspection bodies</td></tr>
<tr><td>NABL</td><td>Accreditation of testing and calibration labs</td></tr>
<tr><td>NABH</td><td>Accreditation of hospitals and healthcare providers</td></tr>
<tr><td>NABET</td><td>Accreditation of education and training (EIA consultants)</td></tr>
<tr><td>NBQP</td><td>National Board for Quality Promotion</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>QCI also runs <b>Swachh Survekshan</b> assessments (with MoHUA), ZED certification for MSMEs, and quality checks for many schemes.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-40", year: 2017, no: 40, subject: "economy", topic: "Small Finance Banks",
  q: `What is the purpose of setting up of Small Finance Banks (SFBs) in India?
<ol><li>To supply credit to small business units</li><li>To supply credit to small and marginal farmers</li><li>To encourage young entrepreneurs to set up business particularly in rural areas.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<p>RBI's guidelines (2014, based on the <b>Nachiket Mor Committee</b> on financial inclusion) say SFBs aim at <b>financial inclusion</b> by (i) providing savings vehicles and (ii) supplying credit to <b>small business units, small and marginal farmers, micro and small industries</b> and other unorganised sector entities, through high-technology, low-cost operations. Encouraging young entrepreneurs in rural areas is not a stated purpose.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: differentiated banks</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th></th><th>Small Finance Bank</th><th>Payments Bank</th></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum capital</td><td>₹200 crore (on-tap licensing guidelines, 2019)</td><td>₹100 crore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lending</td><td>Yes; 75% priority sector; 50% of loans up to ₹25 lakh</td><td>No lending</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deposits</td><td>All deposits</td><td>Demand deposits up to ₹2 lakh per customer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples</td><td>AU, Equitas, Ujjivan, Jana, ESAF, Suryoday</td><td>Airtel, India Post, Fino, Paytm (barred from new deposits, 2024)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>SFBs can convert to universal banks after five years with good track record (RBI guidelines, April 2024).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-41", year: 2017, no: 41, subject: "ir", topic: "APMCHUD",
  q: `With reference to 'Asia Pacific Ministerial Conference on Housing and Urban Development (APMCHUD)', consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The first APMCHUD was held in India in 2006 on the theme 'Emerging Urban Forms — Policy Responses and Governance Structure'.</li><li>India hosts all the Annual Ministerial Conferences in partnership with ADB, APEC and ASEAN.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong per key):</b> the first APMCHUD was held in <b>New Delhi in December 2006</b>, but its theme was different. The theme "<b>Emerging Urban Forms – Policy Responses and Governance Structure</b>" was used for the <b>6th APMCHUD</b>, held in New Delhi in December 2016.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> the conference is held about every two years, <b>hosted by member countries in rotation</b> (Tehran 2008, Solo/Indonesia 2010, Amman 2012, Seoul 2014, New Delhi 2016), supported by <b>UN-Habitat</b>; India does not host all of them.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Unverified: the exact theme of the 2006 conference. The key's logic rests on the theme belonging to the 2016 conference.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>APMCHUD is a consultative mechanism of housing and urban development ministers of Asia-Pacific countries to promote sustainable urbanisation.</li>
<li>India's urban schemes: PMAY-Urban (2015; PMAY-U 2.0, 2024), AMRUT (2015; 2.0, 2021), Smart Cities Mission (2015), SBM-Urban 2.0, HRIDAY (heritage cities), PM e-Bus Sewa (2023).</li>
<li>New Urban Agenda (Habitat III, Quito, 2016). UN-Habitat HQ Nairobi.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-42", year: 2017, no: 42, subject: "polity", topic: "Virtue of democracy",
  q: `Democracy's superior virtue lies in the fact that it calls into activity`,
  opts: ["the intelligence and character of ordinary men and women.", "the methods for strengthening executive leadership.", "a superior individual with dynamism and vision.", "a band of dedicated party workers."], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) the intelligence and character of ordinary men and women.</h3>
<p>Democracy's distinctive value is that it involves <b>ordinary citizens</b> in deciding public affairs, developing their intelligence, judgement and moral character through participation (J.S. Mill's educative argument for representative government). It rests on faith in the common person, not on a superior leader (c), strong executives (b) or party cadres (d), which elitist or authoritarian systems also use.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Strengthening executive leadership is not democracy's distinctive virtue; authoritarian systems do that too.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Relying on a superior individual is the opposite of democratic participation.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Party workers matter in politics, but the virtue lies in engaging all citizens.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: arguments for democracy</h3>
<ul>
<li>Accountable, responsive government; peaceful change of rulers.</li>
<li>Equality and dignity of citizens; protection of rights.</li>
<li>Better decision quality through debate; room to correct mistakes.</li>
<li>Educative effect on citizens (Mill, Rousseau).</li>
<li>Abraham Lincoln: "government of the people, by the people, for the people".</li>
<li>Criticisms: slow decisions, majority tyranny, populism, money and muscle power in elections.</li>
<li>Types: direct (Swiss cantons, referendum), representative, deliberative, participatory.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-43", year: 2017, no: 43, subject: "economy", topic: "UPI and wallets",
  q: `Which of the following is a most likely consequence of implementing the 'Unified Payments Interface (UPI)'?`,
  opts: ["Mobile wallets will not be necessary for online payments.", "Digital currency will totally replace the physical currency in about two decades.", "FDI inflows will drastically increase.", "Direct transfer of subsidies to poor people will become very effective."], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Mobile wallets will not be necessary for online payments.</h3>
<p>UPI lets users pay directly from their <b>bank accounts</b> using a virtual address (VPA) and PIN, 24×7, without first loading money into a <b>prepaid wallet</b>. So wallets (Paytm, MobiKwik etc.) become less necessary; indeed UPI overtook wallets quickly after 2017.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> cash continues; currency in circulation still grew after 2016.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> UPI has no direct link with FDI.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> subsidy transfers use <b>DBT</b> via Aadhaar Payment Bridge and bank accounts (JAM trinity), not UPI.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>UPI (NPCI, April 2016) handles about 80% of India's digital payment volume; India accounts for nearly half of global real-time payments.</li>
<li>UPI international: UAE, Singapore (PayNow link, 2023), France, Sri Lanka, Mauritius, Nepal, Bhutan, Qatar, Trinidad and Tobago.</li>
<li>e-RUPI (2021): prepaid voucher for targeted benefits. e-Rupee: RBI's CBDC (2022).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-44", year: 2017, no: 44, subject: "science", topic: "Cosmology terms",
  q: `The terms 'Event Horizon', 'Singularity', 'String Theory' and 'Standard Model' are sometimes seen in the news in the context of`,
  opts: ["Observation and understanding of the Universe", "Study of the solar and the lunar eclipses", "Placing satellites in the orbit of the Earth", "Origin and evolution of living organisms on the Earth"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Observation and understanding of the Universe</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Event horizon</b>: the boundary around a <b>black hole</b> from which not even light can escape.</li>
<li><b>Singularity</b>: the point of infinite density at the centre of a black hole (or at the Big Bang), where known physics breaks down.</li>
<li><b>String theory</b>: a theoretical framework in which fundamental particles are tiny vibrating strings; an attempt to unify gravity with quantum mechanics (needs extra dimensions).</li>
<li><b>Standard Model</b>: the theory of fundamental particles (quarks, leptons, gauge bosons, Higgs boson) and three forces (electromagnetic, weak, strong); it does not include gravity.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Eclipses are explained by Sun-Earth-Moon alignment, not these terms.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Satellite placement uses orbital mechanics (escape velocity, GTO), not string theory.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Origin of life is biology (evolution, abiogenesis), not these physics terms.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stephen Hawking: <b>Hawking radiation</b> (black holes slowly evaporate); died March 2018.</li>
<li>Higgs boson discovered at CERN (2012); Nobel 2013 to Peter Higgs and François Englert.</li>
<li>Four fundamental forces: gravity, electromagnetism, weak nuclear, strong nuclear.</li>
<li>Dark matter (about 27%) and dark energy (about 68%) make up most of the universe; ordinary matter about 5%.</li>
<li>Event Horizon Telescope: first black hole image (M87*, April 2019).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-45", year: 2017, no: 45, subject: "science", topic: "Genome sequencing in agriculture",
  q: `With reference to agriculture in India, how can the technique of 'genome sequencing', often seen in the news, be used in the immediate future?
<ol><li>Genome sequencing can be used to identify genetic markers for disease resistance and drought tolerance in various crop plants.</li><li>This technique helps in reducing the time required to develop new varieties of crop plants.</li><li>It can be used to decipher the host-pathogen relationships in crops.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1:</b> sequencing finds genes and <b>molecular markers</b> linked to traits such as disease resistance, drought or salinity tolerance.</li>
<li><b>2:</b> <b>marker-assisted selection</b> lets breeders screen seedlings by DNA instead of waiting for plants to mature, cutting breeding time (e.g. from 10–12 years to a few years).</li>
<li><b>3:</b> sequencing both crop and pathogen genomes reveals how pathogens infect and how plants resist them (host-pathogen interaction), helping design resistant varieties.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Indian successes: <b>Improved Samba Mahsuri</b> (bacterial blight resistant rice, ICAR-IIRR and CCMB), <b>Swarna Sub1</b> (flood-tolerant rice, Sub1 gene), Pusa Basmati 1509 with blast resistance; chickpea and pigeonpea genomes sequenced (ICRISAT-led).</li>
<li>India took part in sequencing rice (IRGSP, 2005), tomato, wheat genomes.</li>
<li>Genome editing (CRISPR) builds on sequencing: India released two genome-edited rice varieties (2025).</li>
<li>Human genome: Genome India Project (10,000 genomes); Human Genome Project completed 2003.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-46", year: 2017, no: 46, subject: "polity", topic: "Parliamentary government: advantage",
  q: `The main advantage of the parliamentary form of government is that`,
  opts: ["the executive and legislature work independently.", "it provides continuity of policy and is more efficient.", "the executive remains responsible to the legislature.", "the head of the government cannot be changed without election."], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the executive remains responsible to the legislature.</h3>
<p>The chief merit of the parliamentary system is <b>responsible government</b>: the executive (Council of Ministers) is collectively responsible to the elected legislature (Art 75(3)) and can be questioned and removed by it. This makes the government accountable to the people's representatives at all times.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> independence of executive and legislature is a feature of the <b>presidential</b> system.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> stability and continuity are claimed merits of the presidential system.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> in parliamentary systems the PM can be changed without a general election (e.g. party change of leader, no-confidence).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other merits: harmony between executive and legislature, prevents despotism, wide representation, alternative government available.</li>
<li>Demerits: instability (coalitions), cabinet dictatorship with large majorities, ministers may lack expertise.</li>
<li>Tools of accountability: question hour, zero hour, adjournment and no-confidence motions, cut motions, committees (2017 Q50).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-47", year: 2017, no: 47, subject: "polity", topic: "Rights and duties",
  q: `In the context of India, which one of the following is the correct relationship between Rights and Duties?`,
  opts: ["Rights are correlative with Duties.", "Rights are personal and hence independent of society and Duties.", "Rights, not Duties, are important for the advancement of the personality of the citizen.", "Duties, not Rights, are important for the stability of the State."], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Rights are correlative with Duties.</h3>
<p>Rights and duties are two sides of the same coin: one person's right is another's duty to respect it, and a citizen who claims rights must also perform duties to society and the State. Gandhi: "The true source of rights is duty."</p>
<p>(Fundamental Duties themselves are not legally correlative to Fundamental Rights, as asked in 2017 Q36, but in political theory rights and duties are correlative.)</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Rights are social; they are recognised by society and the State, not independent of them.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Both rights and duties matter for personality development.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Both rights and duties matter for stability.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Laski: "My rights are built upon my duties."</li>
<li>Rights are social, recognised and protected by society and the State, so (b) is wrong.</li>
<li>Both rights and duties are needed for personality development and State stability.</li>
<li>Article 51A (Fundamental Duties) was added in 1976; Swaran Singh Committee said rights and duties go together.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-48", year: 2017, no: 48, subject: "polity", topic: "Preamble as the mind of the makers",
  q: `The mind of the makers of the Constitution of India is reflected in which of the following?`,
  opts: ["The Preamble", "The Fundamental Rights", "The Directive Principles of State Policy", "The Fundamental Duties"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) The Preamble</h3>
<p>The Supreme Court in <b>Berubari Union (1960)</b> called the Preamble "<b>a key to open the mind of the makers</b>" of the Constitution. It sets out the source of authority (the people), the nature of the State, the objectives (justice, liberty, equality, fraternity) and the date of adoption.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Fundamental Rights protect citizens but are not described as the key to the makers' mind.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> DPSPs guide policy but the Preamble is called the key (Berubari, 1960).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Fundamental Duties were added only in 1976, so they cannot reflect the original makers' mind.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: descriptions of the Preamble</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Who</th><th>Called it</th></tr>
<tr><td>K.M. Munshi</td><td>"Horoscope of our sovereign democratic republic"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ernest Barker</td><td>"Key-note to the Constitution"</td></tr>
<tr><td>N.A. Palkhivala</td><td>"Identity card of the Constitution"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thakurdas Bhargava</td><td>"Soul of the Constitution"</td></tr>
<tr><td>M. Hidayatullah</td><td>Resembles the US Declaration of Independence but more than a declaration</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Based on the <b>Objectives Resolution</b> moved by Nehru (13 December 1946). Legal status: part of the Constitution (Kesavananda 1973), amendable but not its basic features; not enforceable by itself (2020 Q16).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-49", year: 2017, no: 49, subject: "geography", topic: "Kohima to Kottayam by road",
  q: `If you travel by road from Kohima to Kottayam, what is the minimum number of States within India through which you can travel, including the origin and the destination?`,
  opts: ["6", "7", "8", "9"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 7</h3>
<p>Shortest route through States: <b>Nagaland</b> (Kohima) → <b>Assam</b> → <b>West Bengal</b> (Siliguri corridor) → <b>Odisha</b> → <b>Andhra Pradesh</b> → <b>Tamil Nadu</b> → <b>Kerala</b> (Kottayam) = <b>7</b>.</p>
<p>Going via Jharkhand, Telangana or Karnataka would add States. The Siliguri corridor ("Chicken's Neck") is the only land link between the northeast and mainland India. Bihar can be avoided because the corridor passes through West Bengal.</p>
<h3>Holistic notes: State borders</h3>
<ul>
<li>States bordering the most other States: <b>Uttar Pradesh</b> (borders 8 States + Delhi UT); Assam borders 7 NE States plus WB.</li>
<li>Siliguri corridor: about 20–22 km wide; borders Nepal, Bangladesh, near Bhutan and China (Doklam).</li>
<li>Kerala borders only Tamil Nadu and Karnataka (and Puducherry's Mahe).</li>
<li>Bihar borders: UP, Jharkhand, West Bengal; Nepal internationally.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2017-50", year: 2017, no: 50, subject: "polity", topic: "Parliamentary control over executive",
  q: `The Parliament of India exercises control over the functions of the Council of Ministers through
<ol><li>Adjournment motion</li><li>Question hour</li><li>Supplementary questions</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Adjournment motion</b>: draws attention to a definite matter of urgent public importance; needs 50 members' support; Lok Sabha only; involves censure of the government.</li>
<li><b>Question Hour</b>: first hour of a sitting; starred (oral answer, supplementaries allowed), unstarred (written answer), short notice questions.</li>
<li><b>Supplementary questions</b>: follow-ups to starred questions; test the minister on the spot.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: other devices</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Device</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Zero Hour</td><td>Informal; Indian innovation (1962); after Question Hour</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calling attention motion</td><td>Indian innovation (1954); not in Rules originally</td></tr>
<tr><td>No-confidence motion</td><td>Lok Sabha only; 50 members' support; no reason needed (Rule 198)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Censure motion</td><td>Must state reasons; against minister or council</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cut motions</td><td>Policy cut (₹1), economy cut, token cut (₹100)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Half-an-hour discussion, short duration discussion (Rule 193)</td><td>Debate without vote</td></tr>
<tr><td>Committees</td><td>PAC, Estimates, DRSCs, Government Assurances, Subordinate Legislation</td></tr>
</table></div>` }

);
