{ id: "2015-51", year: 2015, no: 51, subject: "modern", topic: "Cabinet Mission",
  q: `With reference to the Cabinet Mission, which of the following statements is/are correct?
<ol><li>It recommended a federal government.</li><li>It enlarged the powers of the Indian courts.</li><li>It provided for more Indians in the ICS.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3", "1 and 3", "None"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<p>The <b>Cabinet Mission</b> (March–June 1946: Pethick-Lawrence, Stafford Cripps, A.V. Alexander) came to plan the transfer of power.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> it proposed a loose <b>federal Union of India</b> (British provinces and princely states) with the Centre controlling only defence, foreign affairs and communications; provinces grouped into sections A, B, C; residuary powers with provinces.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> it said nothing about enlarging the powers of Indian courts.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> it did not deal with Indianisation of the ICS (that was a demand from the 19th century; Lee Commission 1924 dealt with it).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Rejected the demand for a full Pakistan. Proposed a <b>Constituent Assembly</b> elected by provincial assemblies (one member per about 10 lakh population) and an Interim Government.</li>
<li>Elections July 1946: Congress 208, League 73 of 296 provincial seats.</li>
<li>The League withdrew acceptance and called <b>Direct Action Day</b> (16 August 1946), leading to Calcutta killings.</li>
<li>Interim Government formed 2 September 1946 (Nehru as Vice-President of the Executive Council); League joined in October.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-52", year: 2015, no: 52, subject: "environment", topic: "Namdapha National Park",
  q: `Which one of the following National Parks has a climate that varies from tropical to subtropical, temperate and arctic?`,
  opts: ["Khangchendzonga National Park", "Nandadevi National Park", "Neora Valley National Park", "Namdapha National Park"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Namdapha National Park</h3>
<p><b>Namdapha</b> (Changlang district, Arunachal Pradesh) rises from about <b>200 m to 4,571 m</b> (Dapha Bum), so its climate ranges from tropical through subtropical and temperate to arctic. It is the only park in the world with four big cats: <b>tiger, leopard, clouded leopard and snow leopard</b>. Also home to the hoolock gibbon (India's only ape) and the Namdapha flying squirrel.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Khangchendzonga (Sikkim; WHS 2016) ranges from about 1,800 m to 8,586 m, so mostly temperate to arctic; no tropical zone.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Nanda Devi (Uttarakhand; WHS 1988 with Valley of Flowers) is high-altitude, subalpine to arctic.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Neora Valley (Kalimpong, West Bengal) ranges about 180 m to 3,200 m; subtropical to temperate, no arctic zone.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Namdapha: Tiger Reserve (1983), lies between the Mishmi hills and Patkai range; Noa-Dihing river.</li>
<li>Arunachal's other tiger reserves include Pakke and Kamlang.</li>
<li>Neora Valley: red panda habitat.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-53", year: 2015, no: 53, subject: "ir", topic: "Amnesty International",
  q: `Amnesty International is`,
  opts: ["an agency of the United Nations to help refugees of civil wars", "a global Human Rights Movement", "a non-governmental voluntary organization to help very poor people", "an inter-governmental agency to cater to medical emergencies in war-ravaged regions"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) a global Human Rights Movement</h3>
<p><b>Amnesty International</b> (founded 1961 in London by lawyer <b>Peter Benenson</b>) is a global movement of over 10 million people campaigning for human rights: freedom of expression, against torture and the death penalty, for prisoners of conscience. It won the <b>Nobel Peace Prize in 1977</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The UN refugee agency is <b>UNHCR</b> (Geneva, 1950; Nobel 1954, 1981).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> It does not work mainly on poverty relief (organisations like Oxfam do).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Medical emergencies in war zones: ICRC (not inter-governmental either) and MSF (NGO).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Amnesty India halted operations in 2020 after its bank accounts were frozen (FCRA and ED action).</li>
<li>Publishes an annual "State of the World's Human Rights" report and a death penalty report.</li>
<li>Other human rights bodies: Human Rights Watch (NGO), UN Human Rights Council (47 members, Geneva; India a member several times), OHCHR.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-54", year: 2015, no: 54, subject: "culture", topic: "Chronology of monuments",
  q: `With reference to the art and archaeological history of India, which one among the following was made earliest?`,
  opts: ["Lingaraja Temple at Bhubaneswar", "Rock-cut Elephant at Dhauli", "Rock-cut Monuments at Mahabalipuram", "Varaha Image at Udayagiri"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Rock-cut Elephant at Dhauli</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Option</th><th>Monument</th><th>Date</th></tr>
<tr><td>(b)</td><td><b>Dhauli elephant</b> (near Bhubaneswar, above Ashoka's Major Rock Edicts incl. Separate Kalinga Edicts)</td><td>3rd century BCE (Mauryan, c. 260 BCE)</td></tr>
<tr><td>(d)</td><td>Varaha image, Udayagiri caves (Vidisha, MP)</td><td>Early 5th century CE (Chandragupta II, Gupta)</td></tr>
<tr><td>(c)</td><td>Rock-cut monuments, Mahabalipuram</td><td>7th–8th century CE (Pallava)</td></tr>
<tr><td>(a)</td><td>Lingaraja temple, Bhubaneswar</td><td>11th century CE (Somavamshi / Ganga)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Lingaraja temple (Kalinga style, deul) is about 1,300 years later than Dhauli.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Mahabalipuram is about 900 years later.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Udayagiri Varaha (Vishnu rescuing Earth) is about 650 years later.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dhauli: the forepart of an elephant carved from a rock (symbol of the Buddha); Japan-built Shanti Stupa (1972) nearby. Kalinga war site on the Daya river.</li>
<li>Udayagiri (Odisha) is different: Jain caves of Kharavela (Hathigumpha inscription), 1st century BCE.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-55", year: 2015, no: 55, subject: "medieval", topic: "Feudalism",
  q: `With reference to Indian history, which of the following is/are the essential element/elements of the feudal system?
<ol><li>A very strong centralized political authority and a very weak provincial or local political authority</li><li>Emergence of administrative structure based on control and possession of land</li><li>Creation of lord-vassal relationship between the feudal lord and his overlord</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> feudalism means the <b>opposite</b>: a weak centre and strong local power holders (samantas), with political authority fragmented.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> administration rested on control and possession of land; land grants to Brahmins, officials and military chiefs carried fiscal and administrative rights.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> a <b>lord-vassal</b> (overlord-samanta) hierarchy, with the vassal owing loyalty, tribute and military service.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Indian feudalism debate</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>R.S. Sharma</b> ("Indian Feudalism", 1965): land grants from the Gupta period onwards led to decentralisation, decline of trade and towns, peasant subjection, forced labour (vishti).</li>
<li>Critics: <b>D.C. Sircar</b>, <b>Harbans Mukhia</b> ("Was there feudalism in Indian history?", 1981), <b>B.D. Chattopadhyaya</b> (integrative, state-formation model).</li>
<li>Terms: samanta, mahasamanta, bhoga, brahmadeya, agrahara.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-56", year: 2015, no: 56, subject: "environment", topic: "BioCarbon Fund",
  q: `'BioCarbon Fund Initiative for Sustainable Forest Landscapes' is managed by the`,
  opts: ["Asian Development Bank", "International Monetary Fund", "United Nations Environment Programme", "World Bank"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) World Bank</h3>
<p>The <b>BioCarbon Fund Initiative for Sustainable Forest Landscapes</b> (ISFL, 2013) is a multilateral fund managed by the <b>World Bank</b>, supported by donor governments (Norway, UK, USA, Germany, Switzerland). It promotes reduced greenhouse gas emissions from the land sector (REDD+, climate-smart agriculture, land-use planning) in jurisdictions like Colombia, Ethiopia, Indonesia, Mexico, Zambia.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> ADB runs its own climate funds (Asia Pacific Climate Finance Fund), not ISFL.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The IMF runs the Resilience and Sustainability Trust (2022) for climate-related balance of payments support; not ISFL.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> UNEP co-runs UN-REDD with FAO and UNDP; not ISFL.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: World Bank carbon funds</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Forest Carbon Partnership Facility</b> (2008): Readiness Fund and Carbon Fund for REDD+.</li>
<li>BioCarbon Fund (2004): first carbon fund for land use and forestry.</li>
<li>Prototype Carbon Fund (1999), Partnership for Market Readiness, Climate Investment Funds.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-57", year: 2015, no: 57, subject: "ir", topic: "India's memberships",
  q: `India is a member of which among the following?
<ol><li>Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation</li><li>Association of South-East Asian Nations</li><li>East Asia Summit</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3", "India is a member of none of them"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> India is <b>not</b> in <b>APEC</b> (21 Pacific Rim economies, 1989); a membership moratorium (1997–2010) kept it out and India's bid is pending.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> India is not an <b>ASEAN</b> member; it is a <b>dialogue partner</b> (sectoral 1992, full 1995, summit-level 2002; Comprehensive Strategic Partnership 2022).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> India is a founding member of the <b>East Asia Summit</b> (2005, Kuala Lumpur): ASEAN 10 + India, China, Japan, South Korea, Australia, New Zealand, USA, Russia (joined 2011).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Wrong: India is in neither APEC nor ASEAN.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Wrong for the same reason; only the EAS applies.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong: India is an EAS member.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: India and Asia-Pacific groupings</h3>
<ul>
<li>Member: EAS, ASEAN Regional Forum (1996), ADMM-Plus, BIMSTEC, Mekong-Ganga Cooperation, IORA, Quad, IPEF (three pillars), SCO.</li>
<li>Not a member: APEC, RCEP (withdrew 2019), CPTPP.</li>
<li><b>Act East Policy</b> (2014), successor of Look East (1991).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-58", year: 2015, no: 58, subject: "geography", topic: "Steel industry imports",
  q: `In India, the steel production industry requires the import of`,
  opts: ["saltpetre", "rock phosphate", "coking coal", "All of the above"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) coking coal</h3>
<p>Steel making in blast furnaces needs <b>metallurgical (coking) coal</b> with low ash. Indian coal is high in ash (30–45%), so India imports about <b>85%</b> of its coking coal, mainly from <b>Australia</b>, plus Russia, USA, Mozambique, Indonesia.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Saltpetre (potassium nitrate) is used in gunpowder, fertilisers and food preservation; not in steel making.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Rock phosphate is imported for fertilisers (DAP), not steel.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong, since only coking coal applies.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Indian steel</h3>
<ul>
<li>India is the <b>second-largest</b> crude steel producer (after China) since 2018.</li>
<li>National Steel Policy 2017: 300 million tonnes capacity by 2030–31.</li>
<li>Raw materials: iron ore (Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Karnataka, Jharkhand), limestone, dolomite, manganese; domestic coking coal in Jharia (Jharkhand).</li>
<li>Green steel taxonomy (2024) and Mission Coking Coal; hydrogen-based DRI to cut emissions.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-59", year: 2015, no: 59, subject: "polity", topic: "Fifth and Sixth Schedules",
  q: `The provisions in Fifth Schedule and Sixth Schedule in the Constitution of India are made in order to`,
  opts: ["protect the interests of Scheduled Tribes", "determine the boundaries between States", "determine the powers, authority and responsibilities of Panchayats", "protect the interests of all the border States"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) protect the interests of Scheduled Tribes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Fifth Schedule</b> (Art 244(1)): administration and control of <b>Scheduled Areas</b> and STs in States other than the four NE States below. Tribes Advisory Council; Governor can modify laws for these areas and make regulations (land transfer, moneylending). Ten States, including Jharkhand, Odisha, Chhattisgarh, MP.</li>
<li><b>Sixth Schedule</b> (Art 244(2), 275(1)): <b>Autonomous District Councils</b> in tribal areas of <b>Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram</b>, with legislative, judicial and executive powers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> State boundaries are changed by Parliament under Articles 3 and 4; no Schedule determines them.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Panchayat powers are listed in the <b>Eleventh Schedule</b> (Art 243G).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> No Schedule protects border States as such; special provisions for some States are in Articles 371–371J.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>PESA Act 1996 extends Panchayats to Fifth Schedule areas (gram sabha powers over minor forest produce, land acquisition consultation).</li>
<li>Ten Autonomous District Councils under the Sixth Schedule (Bodoland, Karbi Anglong, Dima Hasao in Assam; Garo, Khasi, Jaintia hills in Meghalaya; Tripura TTAADC; Chakma, Lai, Mara in Mizoram).</li>
<li>Demands for Sixth Schedule status: Ladakh (2023–25 protests; four killed in Leh violence on 24 September 2025; Sonam Wangchuk detained under the NSA and released in March 2026; talks with the MHA continue, with Article 371-type safeguards discussed).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-60", year: 2015, no: 60, subject: "polity", topic: "Budget and public funds",
  q: `With reference to the Union Government, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Department of Revenue is responsible for the preparation of Union Budget that is presented to the Parliament.</li><li>No amount can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund of India without the authorization from the Parliament of India.</li><li>All the disbursements made from Public Account also need the authorization from the Parliament of India.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the Budget is prepared by the <b>Budget Division of the Department of Economic Affairs</b>, Ministry of Finance, not the Department of Revenue.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>Article 114(3)</b>: no money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund except under an appropriation made by law (Appropriation Act).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> money in the <b>Public Account</b> (Art 266(2)), such as provident funds, small savings and deposits, belongs to others; payments from it are made by executive action without parliamentary appropriation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: three funds</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Fund</th><th>Article</th><th>Withdrawal</th></tr>
<tr><td>Consolidated Fund</td><td>266(1)</td><td>Only by Parliament's appropriation law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public Account</td><td>266(2)</td><td>Executive action (banker role)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contingency Fund</td><td>267</td><td>Held by the President (Finance Secretary operates); advances recouped later by Parliament. Corpus ₹30,000 crore</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Department of Revenue handles direct and indirect taxes (CBDT, CBIC).</p>` },

{ id: "2015-61", year: 2015, no: 61, subject: "polity", topic: "Custodian of the Constitution",
  q: `Who/Which of the following is the custodian of the Constitution of India?`,
  opts: ["The President of India", "The Prime Minister of India", "The Lok Sabha Secretariat", "The Supreme Court of India"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) The Supreme Court of India</h3>
<p>The <b>Supreme Court</b> is the guardian and final interpreter of the Constitution. Through <b>judicial review</b> (Arts 13, 32, 131–136, 226 for High Courts), it can strike down laws and executive actions that violate the Constitution, and protects the <b>basic structure</b> (Kesavananda Bharati, 1973).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The President takes an oath to "preserve, protect and defend" the Constitution (Art 60), but acts on ministerial advice and does not interpret it authoritatively.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The PM heads the executive, which is itself subject to judicial review.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Lok Sabha Secretariat is an administrative office of the House.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Supreme Court is also called the guardian of Fundamental Rights (Art 32 is the "heart and soul" of the Constitution, per Ambedkar).</li>
<li>Advisory jurisdiction: Art 143 (Presidential references, e.g. on Governors' timelines for assent, 2025).</li>
<li>Constitution Day: 26 November.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-62", year: 2015, no: 62, subject: "economy", topic: "Irrigation programmes",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme was launched during 1996-97 to provide loan assistance to poor farmers.</li><li>The Command Area Development Programme was launched in 1974-75 for the development of water-use efficiency.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> <b>AIBP</b> (1996–97) gave central loan assistance (later grants) to <b>State governments</b> to complete ongoing major and medium irrigation projects; it did not lend to farmers.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the <b>Command Area Development Programme</b> (1974–75) aimed to close the gap between irrigation potential created and used, improving water-use efficiency through field channels, land levelling, warabandi (rotational water supply).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Both are now part of <b>PM Krishi Sinchayee Yojana</b> (2015): AIBP, Har Khet Ko Pani (CAD and water management, surface minor irrigation, RRR of water bodies), Per Drop More Crop (micro-irrigation), watershed development.</li>
<li>Long Term Irrigation Fund with NABARD (2016–17) for 99 priority AIBP projects.</li>
<li>Gross irrigated area is about 55% of gross cropped area; groundwater supplies over 60% of irrigation.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-63", year: 2015, no: 63, subject: "environment", topic: "GEAC",
  q: `The Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee is constituted under the`,
  opts: ["Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006", "Geographical Indications of Goods (Registration and Protection) Act, 1999", "Environment (Protection) Act, 1986", "Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Environment (Protection) Act, 1986</h3>
<p>The <b>GEAC</b> (in MoEFCC) was set up under the <b>"Rules for the Manufacture, Use, Import, Export and Storage of Hazardous Micro-organisms, Genetically Engineered Organisms or Cells, 1989"</b>, framed under the <b>Environment (Protection) Act, 1986</b>. It approves large-scale use and environmental release of GM organisms, including field trials.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> FSSA 2006 governs food safety; GM food approval is meant to go through FSSAI, but GEAC was not created under it.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The GI Act protects place-linked product names.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Wild Life Act protects species and habitats.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: GM crops in India</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Bt cotton</b> (2002) is the only GM crop approved for commercial cultivation (illegal HTBt cotton is widespread).</li>
<li><b>Bt brinjal</b>: GEAC approved 2009; moratorium imposed 2010 (Bangladesh grows it).</li>
<li><b>GM mustard DMH-11</b>: GEAC approved environmental release (2022); case pending in the Supreme Court (split verdict 2024).</li>
<li><b>Genome-edited rice</b> varieties (DRR Dhan 100 Kamala, Pusa DST Rice 1) released in 2025; SDN-1 and SDN-2 edits exempted from GM rules (2022).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-64", year: 2015, no: 64, subject: "ir", topic: "Mekong-Ganga Cooperation",
  q: `In the Mekong-Ganga Cooperation, an initiative of six countries, which of the following is/are not a participant/participants?
<ol><li>Bangladesh</li><li>Cambodia</li><li>China</li><li>Myanmar</li><li>Thailand</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2, 3 and 4", "1 and 3", "1, 2 and 5"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3</h3>
<p>The <b>Mekong-Ganga Cooperation</b> (MGC, Vientiane, 2000) has six members: <b>India, Thailand, Myanmar, Cambodia, Laos and Vietnam</b>. It covers tourism, culture, education, transport and communications.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (not a participant):</b> Bangladesh is not in MGC (it is in BIMSTEC and BBIN).</li>
<li><b>2 (participant):</b> Cambodia is a member.</li>
<li><b>3 (not a participant):</b> China is not in MGC, though the Mekong rises in Tibet; China leads the separate <b>Lancang-Mekong Cooperation</b> (2016).</li>
<li><b>4 (participant):</b> Myanmar is a member.</li>
<li><b>5 (participant):</b> Thailand is a member.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mekong flows through China, Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, Vietnam.</li>
<li>Other Mekong frameworks: Mekong River Commission (1995; Laos, Thailand, Cambodia, Vietnam), Mekong-US Partnership, Mekong-Japan.</li>
<li>MGC Asian Traditional Textiles Museum, Siem Reap (Cambodia). Quick Impact Projects funded by India.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-65", year: 2015, no: 65, subject: "economy", topic: "Basel III",
  q: `'Basel III Accord' or simply 'Basel III', often seen in the news, seeks to`,
  opts: ["develop national strategies for the conservation and sustainable use of biological diversity", "improve banking sector's ability to deal with financial and economic stress and improve risk management", "reduce the greenhouse gas emissions but places a heavier burden on developed countries", "transfer technology from developed countries to poor countries to enable them to replace the use of chlorofluorocarbons in refrigeration with harmless chemicals"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) improve banking sector's ability to deal with financial and economic stress and improve risk management</h3>
<p><b>Basel III</b> (2010, Basel Committee on Banking Supervision at the BIS, after the 2008 crisis) raises the quality and quantity of bank capital, adds liquidity standards and limits leverage.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> National biodiversity strategies (NBSAPs) come under the CBD.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Heavier GHG burden on developed countries describes the Kyoto Protocol.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> CFC replacement through technology transfer comes under the Montreal Protocol's Multilateral Fund.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Basel III elements</h3>
<ul>
<li>Minimum CET1 4.5%, Tier 1 6%, total capital 8% of risk-weighted assets, plus <b>Capital Conservation Buffer</b> 2.5%; countercyclical buffer 0–2.5%. RBI requires total CRAR of 9% plus CCB (11.5%).</li>
<li><b>Liquidity Coverage Ratio</b> (30-day stress), <b>Net Stable Funding Ratio</b> (one year), <b>Leverage ratio</b> (3%; RBI 4% for D-SIBs).</li>
<li>Basel I (1988, credit risk), Basel II (2004, three pillars: capital, supervision, market discipline). "Basel III endgame" (2017 reforms) being implemented.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-66", year: 2015, no: 66, subject: "geography", topic: "Westerlies",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The winds which blow between 30° N and 60° S latitudes throughout the year are known as westerlies.</li><li>The moist air masses that cause winter rains in North-Western region of India are part of westerlies.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the westerlies blow between <b>30° and 60° in each hemisphere</b> (30–60° N and 30–60° S), from the subtropical highs to the subpolar lows. "30° N to 60° S" would include the tropics and the trade winds, so the statement is wrong.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>Western Disturbances</b>, low-pressure systems from the Mediterranean region carried by the westerlies (subtropical westerly jet), bring winter rain and snow to north-west India; good for rabi wheat.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: planetary winds</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Belt</th><th>Winds</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–30°</td><td>Trade winds (NE in north, SE in south)</td></tr>
<tr><td>30–60°</td><td>Westerlies (SW in north, NW in south); strongest in the south ("Roaring Forties, Furious Fifties, Shrieking Sixties")</td></tr>
<tr><td>60–90°</td><td>Polar easterlies</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Calm belts: doldrums (equator), horse latitudes (about 30°).</p>` },

{ id: "2015-67", year: 2015, no: 67, subject: "ir", topic: "IOR-ARC / IORA",
  q: `With reference to 'Indian Ocean Rim Association for Regional Cooperation (IOR-ARC)', consider the following statements:
<ol><li>It was established very recently in response to incidents of piracy and accidents of oil spills.</li><li>It is an alliance meant for maritime security only.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> it was set up in <b>March 1997</b> (Mauritius), after a 1995 initiative, to promote regional economic cooperation, not as a response to piracy or oil spills. Renamed <b>IORA</b> in 2013.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> it is not a security alliance; its priority areas include trade and investment, fisheries, disaster risk management, tourism, academic cooperation, <b>blue economy</b> and women's empowerment (maritime safety is one area among many).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: IORA</h3>
<ul>
<li>23 members (e.g., India, Australia, South Africa, Indonesia, Iran, UAE, Bangladesh, Sri Lanka, France via Réunion); 12 dialogue partners. Secretariat: <b>Ebene, Mauritius</b>.</li>
<li>Related: IONS (Indian Ocean Naval Symposium, 2008, navies), Colombo Security Conclave, India's <b>SAGAR</b> (2015) and <b>MAHASAGAR</b> (2025) visions.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-68", year: 2015, no: 68, subject: "modern", topic: "Moderates and Extremists",
  q: `Which one of the following movements has contributed to a split in the Indian National Congress resulting in the emergence of 'moderates' and 'extremists'?`,
  opts: ["Swadeshi Movement", "Quit India Movement", "Non-Cooperation Movement", "Civil Disobedience Movement"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Swadeshi Movement</h3>
<p>The <b>Swadeshi Movement</b> (1905–08), against the partition of Bengal, deepened the divide over methods and scope: Extremists (Lal-Bal-Pal, Aurobindo) wanted boycott extended across India and to all spheres; Moderates wanted it limited to Bengal and to foreign goods. This led to the <b>Surat split (1907)</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Quit India (1942) came long after the Moderate–Extremist divide, which ended at Lucknow (1916).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Non-Cooperation (1920–22) was followed by the No-changer vs Pro-changer (Swarajist) division, not Moderate–Extremist.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Civil Disobedience (1930–34) did not split Congress into these groups.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Partition of Bengal announced July 1905 (Curzon), effective 16 October 1905 (Raksha Bandhan, observed as a day of mourning; Tagore's "Amar Sonar Bangla").</li>
<li>Annulled in 1911 (Delhi Durbar); capital shifted to Delhi.</li>
<li>Later splits: Swarajist Party (1923, C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru), Congress Socialist Party (1934), Forward Bloc (1939, Bose).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-69", year: 2015, no: 69, subject: "geography", topic: "Living root bridges",
  q: `In a particular region in India, the local people train the roots of living trees into robust bridges across the streams. As the time passes, these bridges become stronger. These unique 'living root bridges' are found in`,
  opts: ["Meghalaya", "Himachal Pradesh", "Jharkhand", "Tamil Nadu"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Meghalaya</h3>
<p>The <b>Khasi and Jaintia</b> people of <b>Meghalaya</b> guide the aerial roots of the <b>Indian rubber fig</b> (<i>Ficus elastica</i>) across streams over 10–15 years, using bamboo or betel-nut scaffolding. The bridges grow stronger with age and last centuries. The <b>double-decker root bridge</b> at Nongriat (near Cherrapunji/Sohra) is the most famous.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Himachal Pradesh has wooden and suspension bridges; no living root bridges.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Jharkhand does not have this tradition.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Tamil Nadu does not have this tradition.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>"Jingkieng Jri: Living Root Bridge Cultural Landscapes" is on UNESCO's <b>Tentative List</b> (2022) and was nominated for World Heritage.</li>
<li>Meghalaya: Mawsynram and Sohra are among the wettest places on Earth; Mawlynnong "cleanest village in Asia"; Khasi society is matrilineal.</li>
<li>Some root bridges also exist in Nagaland and Indonesia.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-70", year: 2015, no: 70, subject: "geography", topic: "Tides",
  q: `Tides occur in the oceans and seas due to which among the following?
<ol><li>Gravitational force of the Sun</li><li>Gravitational force of the Moon</li><li>Centrifugal force of the Earth</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the <b>Sun's</b> gravity also causes tides, about 46% as strong as the Moon's (it is far more massive but much farther away).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the <b>Moon's</b> gravitational pull is the main cause; it pulls water towards it on the near side.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> the <b>centrifugal force</b> from the Earth-Moon system rotating around their common centre of mass creates the second bulge on the far side.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: types of tides</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Spring tides</b>: Sun, Moon and Earth in line (new moon and full moon, syzygy); higher high tides.</li>
<li><b>Neap tides</b>: Sun and Moon at right angles (first and last quarter); lowest range.</li>
<li><b>Perigean tides</b>: Moon closest to Earth. Two high and two low tides roughly every 24 hours 50 minutes (semi-diurnal).</li>
<li>Highest tidal range: Bay of Fundy (Canada). In India: Gulf of Khambhat and Gulf of Kutch (tidal energy potential).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-71", year: 2015, no: 71, subject: "science", topic: "IRS satellites",
  q: `In which of the following activities are Indian Remote Sensing (IRS) satellites used?
<ol><li>Assessment of crop productivity</li><li>Locating groundwater resources</li><li>Mineral exploration</li><li>Telecommunications</li><li>Traffic studies</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "4 and 5 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2, 3, 4 and 5"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3>
<div class="note">Some argue high-resolution IRS imagery (Cartosat) is used in urban and traffic planning. UPSC's key is (a).</div>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> crop acreage and production forecasting (FASAL, CHAMAN for horticulture), drought monitoring.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> groundwater prospect maps (Rajiv Gandhi National Drinking Water Mission) using hydrogeomorphology.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> mineral exploration through geological and structural mapping.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> telecommunications use <b>communication satellites</b> (INSAT/GSAT series) in geostationary orbit, not IRS satellites (in sun-synchronous orbit).</li>
<li><b>5 (wrong, per key):</b> real-time traffic studies need ground or aerial sensors and GPS; IRS is not designed for this.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>IRS-1A (1988) was India's first operational remote sensing satellite. Series: Resourcesat, Cartosat, Oceansat, RISAT (radar), EOS series.</li>
<li>NISAR (NASA-ISRO SAR, launched July 2025 by GSLV-F16).</li>
<li>Data from NRSC (Hyderabad) via Bhuvan portal.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-72", year: 2015, no: 72, subject: "geography", topic: "Tropical wet evergreen forests",
  q: `Consider the following States:
<ol><li>Arunachal Pradesh</li><li>Himachal Pradesh</li><li>Mizoram</li></ol>
In which of the above States do 'Tropical Wet Evergreen Forests' occur?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<p>Tropical wet evergreen forests need over 250 cm rainfall, a short dry season and warm temperatures. In India they occur on the Western Ghats' western slopes, the north-east and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Arunachal Pradesh has them in the foothills and valleys (e.g. Namdapha, Upper Assam-type dipterocarp forests).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Himachal Pradesh has subtropical pine, temperate (deodar, oak) and alpine forests; it is too cold and dry for tropical wet evergreen forests.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Mizoram has tropical wet evergreen and semi-evergreen forests in its lower areas.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Indian forest types (Champion and Seth, 16 groups)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tropical wet evergreen: multi-layered canopy, trees 45–60 m; ebony, mahogany, rosewood, rubber, cinchona, Dipterocarpus.</li>
<li>Tropical moist deciduous (100–200 cm): teak, sal, shisham, sandalwood; most widespread with dry deciduous.</li>
<li>Tropical thorn (below 70 cm): babul, kikar, khejri.</li>
<li>Montane: sub-tropical (chir pine), temperate (deodar, oak), alpine (birch, juniper).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-73", year: 2015, no: 73, subject: "science", topic: "IndARC",
  q: `The term 'IndARC', sometimes seen in the news, is the name of`,
  opts: ["an indigenously developed radar system inducted into Indian Defence", "India's satellite to provide services to the countries of Indian Ocean Rim", "a scientific establishment set up by India in Antarctic region", "India's underwater observatory to scientifically study the Arctic region"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) India's underwater observatory to scientifically study the Arctic region</h3>
<p><b>IndARC</b> (July 2014) is India's first <b>underwater moored observatory</b> in the <b>Kongsfjorden</b> fjord, Svalbard (Norway), at about 192 m depth. It records ocean temperature, salinity, currents and other data to study the Arctic's link with the Indian monsoon. Run by NCPOR (Goa) and ESSO-NIOT.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Indigenous defence radars include Rajendra (Akash), Swathi (weapon locating), Arudhra, Ashwini.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> India's satellite for neighbours is the <b>South Asia Satellite</b> (GSAT-9, 2017), for SAARC countries.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> India's Antarctic stations are Dakshin Gangotri (1983, now supply base), <b>Maitri</b> (1989) and <b>Bharati</b> (2012); Maitri II is planned.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: India in the polar regions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Arctic station: <b>Himadri</b> (2008, Ny-Ålesund, Svalbard). India is an <b>Arctic Council observer</b> (2013). <b>Arctic Policy</b> (2022).</li>
<li><b>Indian Antarctic Act 2022</b>. India is a consultative party to the Antarctic Treaty (1983); hosted ATCM 46 in Kochi (2024).</li>
<li>Himansh: high-altitude Himalayan research station (Spiti, 2016).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-74", year: 2015, no: 74, subject: "environment", topic: "Forest Carbon Partnership Facility",
  q: `With reference to 'Forest Carbon Partnership Facility', which of the following statements is/are correct?
<ol><li>It is a global partnership of governments, businesses, civil society and indigenous peoples.</li><li>It provides financial aid to universities, individual scientists and institutions involved in scientific forestry research to develop eco-friendly and climate adaptation technologies for sustainable forest management.</li><li>It assists the countries in their 'REDD+ (Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation+)' efforts by providing them with financial and technical assistance.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the FCPF (World Bank, 2008, launched at Bali COP13) is a global partnership of governments, businesses, civil society and indigenous peoples.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> it does not fund universities or individual scientists for forestry research; it funds countries.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> it helps developing countries with REDD+ through a <b>Readiness Fund</b> (strategies, reference levels, monitoring systems) and a <b>Carbon Fund</b> (results-based payments for verified emission reductions).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>About 47 developing countries (Africa, Asia-Pacific, Latin America) participate; India is not a REDD country participant.</li>
<li>Related: BioCarbon Fund ISFL (World Bank), UN-REDD (FAO, UNDP, UNEP), Green Climate Fund REDD+ results-based payments pilot.</li>
<li>REDD+ "plus": conservation, sustainable management of forests, enhancement of forest carbon stocks.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2015-75", year: 2015, no: 75, subject: "culture", topic: "Classical languages",
  q: `Which one of the following was given classical language status recently?`,
  opts: ["Odia", "Konkani", "Bhojpuri", "Assamese"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Odia</h3>
<p><b>Odia</b> was declared the sixth classical language in <b>2014</b>, the first Indo-Aryan language after Sanskrit to get the status.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Konkani is a Scheduled (Eighth Schedule) language since 1992 but not classical.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Bhojpuri is not in the Eighth Schedule and not classical.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Assamese did not have the status in 2015; it got it only in <b>October 2024</b>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: classical languages (11)</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Year</th><th>Languages</th></tr>
<tr><td>2004</td><td>Tamil (first)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2005</td><td>Sanskrit</td></tr>
<tr><td>2008</td><td>Telugu, Kannada</td></tr>
<tr><td>2013</td><td>Malayalam</td></tr>
<tr><td>2014</td><td>Odia</td></tr>
<tr><td>2024</td><td>Marathi, Pali, Prakrit, Assamese, Bengali</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Revised criteria (2024): high antiquity of early texts (1,500–2,000 years), ancient literature considered heritage, knowledge texts (prose, inscriptions), and the classical form may be distinct from its current form. Benefits: international awards, Centres of Excellence, UGC chairs.</p>` }

