// UPSC CSE Prelims 2014, GS Paper I, Series A, Q26–50.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2014-26", year: 2014, no: 26, subject: "culture", topic: "Tribal sapling festival",
  q: `Every year, a month-long ecologically important campaign/festival is held during which certain communities/tribes plant saplings of fruit-bearing trees. Which of the following are such communities/tribes?`,
  opts: ["Bhutia and Lepcha", "Gond and Korku", "Irula and Toda", "Sahariya and Agariya"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Gond and Korku</h3>
<p>The <b>Gond</b> and <b>Korku</b> tribes of central India (Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra: Satpura region, Betul, Chhindwara, Melghat) hold a month-long festival during the monsoon in which they plant saplings of fruit-bearing trees.</p>
<p>The festival is <b>Hari Jiroti</b>, a month-long monsoon festival of the Gond and Korku of <b>Betul and Harda districts</b> (Madhya Pradesh); villagers plant saplings of wild mango, jamun, bael, jackfruit, custard apple and mahua on forest land (Down To Earth).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Bhutia and Lepcha live in Sikkim and Darjeeling; known for Losar and Losoong festivals, not this campaign.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Irula (snake and rat catchers, Tamil Nadu) and Toda (buffalo herders, Nilgiris) are not linked with this.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Sahariya (a PVTG of MP and Rajasthan) and Agariya (traditional iron smelters) are not linked with this.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: tribes of central India</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Gonds</b>: largest tribal group in India; Gond kingdoms (Garha-Mandla, Rani Durgavati); Gond art (Jangarh Singh Shyam, Bhajju Shyam).</li>
<li><b>Korkus</b>: Munda (Austroasiatic) group of the Satpura hills (Melghat).</li>
<li>75 Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs); PM JANMAN (2023) for their development.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-27", year: 2014, no: 27, subject: "economy", topic: "Sales tax",
  q: `The sales tax you pay while purchasing a toothpaste is a`,
  opts: ["tax imposed by the Central Government", "tax imposed by the Central Government but collected by the State Government", "tax imposed by the State Government but collected by the Central Government", "tax imposed and collected by the State Government"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) tax imposed and collected by the State Government</h3>
<p>In 2014 (pre-GST), sales tax/VAT on goods sold within a State was levied and collected by the <b>State</b> under <b>Entry 54 of the State List</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Centre levied Central Sales Tax only on <b>inter-State</b> sales (collected and kept by the origin State); intra-State sales tax was a State tax.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Taxes levied by the Centre but collected by States were stamp duties on bills of exchange and excise on medicinal and toilet preparations (old Art 268).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Constitution has no tax imposed by States but collected by the Centre.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: after GST (1 July 2017)</h3>
<ul>
<li>101st Amendment (2016): Article 246A gives concurrent power to both to levy GST. Intra-State supply: CGST (Centre) + SGST (State); inter-State: IGST (Centre, shared).</li>
<li>GST Council (Art 279A): Union FM chairs; Centre has one-third vote weight, States two-thirds; decisions need three-fourths.</li>
<li>Toothpaste moved from 18% to 5% in the 2025 GST rate rationalisation (two main slabs of 5% and 18%, plus 40% for sin goods).</li>
<li>Outside GST: alcohol for human consumption, and five petroleum products (until the Council decides).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-28", year: 2014, no: 28, subject: "economy", topic: "Venture capital",
  q: `What does venture capital mean?`,
  opts: ["A short-term capital provided to industries", "A long-term start-up capital provided to new entrepreneurs", "Funds provided to industries at times of incurring losses", "Funds provided for replacement and renovation of industries"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) A long-term start-up capital provided to new entrepreneurs</h3>
<p><b>Venture capital</b> is long-term risk capital (usually equity) given to new, innovative, high-growth businesses that cannot get bank loans. Investors take a stake and exit later (IPO or sale) with high returns if the venture succeeds.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Short-term capital for industries is working capital (bank overdrafts, cash credit).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Funds to loss-making firms are rescue or turnaround finance (stressed-asset funds).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Replacement and renovation funding is modernisation finance (term loans).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stages: seed and angel → early (Series A) → growth → pre-IPO; private equity invests in mature firms.</li>
<li>Regulated by SEBI as <b>Alternative Investment Funds</b> (AIF Category I, 2012 regulations).</li>
<li><b>Fund of Funds for Startups</b> (₹10,000 crore, run by SIDBI, 2016; a second fund announced in Budget 2025).</li>
<li>India is the world's third-largest startup ecosystem; over 100 unicorns.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-29", year: 2014, no: 29, subject: "economy", topic: "Twelfth Five-Year Plan",
  q: `The main objective of the 12th Five-Year Plan is`,
  opts: ["inclusive growth and poverty reduction", "inclusive and sustainable growth", "sustainable and inclusive growth to reduce unemployment", "faster, sustainable and more inclusive growth"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) faster, sustainable and more inclusive growth</h3>
<p>The <b>12th Plan (2012–17)</b> theme was "<b>Faster, More Inclusive and Sustainable Growth</b>", with a target of 8% annual GDP growth (scaled down from 9%).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Inclusive growth with poverty reduction was part of the 11th Plan's thrust ("Towards Faster and More Inclusive Growth", 2007–12).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> This misses "faster", which is part of the 12th Plan's theme.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Reducing unemployment was not the stated main objective.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: plan themes</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Plan</th><th>Focus</th></tr>
<tr><td>1st (1951–56)</td><td>Agriculture, Harrod-Domar model</td></tr>
<tr><td>2nd (1956–61)</td><td>Heavy industry, Mahalanobis model</td></tr>
<tr><td>5th (1974–79)</td><td>Garibi Hatao</td></tr>
<tr><td>8th (1992–97)</td><td>Human development; after liberalisation</td></tr>
<tr><td>11th (2007–12)</td><td>Faster and more inclusive growth</td></tr>
<tr><td>12th (2012–17)</td><td>Faster, more inclusive and sustainable growth (last plan)</td></tr>
</table></div>` },

{ id: "2014-30", year: 2014, no: 30, subject: "economy", topic: "Current account",
  q: `With reference to Balance of Payments, which of the following constitutes/constitute the Current Account?
<ol><li>Balance of trade</li><li>Foreign assets</li><li>Balance of invisibles</li><li>Special Drawing Rights</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3", "1 and 3", "1, 2 and 4"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the <b>balance of trade</b> (merchandise exports minus imports) is part of the current account.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> foreign assets (FDI, portfolio investment, loans) come in the <b>capital/financial account</b>.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> the <b>balance of invisibles</b> (services, primary income such as interest and dividends, and transfers such as remittances) is part of the current account.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> SDRs are reserve assets, recorded under changes in reserves (financial account), not the current account.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: BoP structure</h3>
<ul>
<li>Current account = trade balance + net invisibles. India usually runs a large trade deficit, partly offset by services exports (IT) and remittances (India is the top remittance recipient, about $135 billion in 2024–25).</li>
<li>Capital account: FDI, FPI, external commercial borrowings, NRI deposits, external assistance.</li>
<li>CAD of about 2.5% of GDP is considered sustainable (Rangarajan).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-31", year: 2014, no: 31, subject: "economy", topic: "MSF and NDTL",
  q: `The terms 'Marginal Standing Facility Rate' and 'Net Demand and Time Liabilities', sometimes appearing in news, are used in relation to`,
  opts: ["banking operations", "communication networking", "military strategies", "supply and demand of agricultural products"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) banking operations</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Marginal Standing Facility</b> (MSF, 2011): banks can borrow overnight from RBI against government securities, even dipping into their SLR holdings (up to 2% of NDTL), at a penal rate (repo + 0.25%). It is the ceiling of the interest rate corridor.</li>
<li><b>Net Demand and Time Liabilities</b> (NDTL): a bank's demand deposits (current, savings) and time deposits (fixed) plus other liabilities, net of inter-bank claims. CRR and SLR are calculated as a share of NDTL.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Communication networking uses terms like bandwidth and latency.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Military strategy terms include deterrence, second strike.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Agricultural markets use MSP, buffer stocks.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: policy corridor</h3>
<ul>
<li>Floor: <b>Standing Deposit Facility</b> (SDF, 2022; repo − 0.25%), replacing the reverse repo as the floor.</li>
<li>Policy rate: <b>repo</b>. Ceiling: <b>MSF</b> = bank rate.</li>
<li>Liquidity Adjustment Facility (LAF): repo and reverse repo operations, plus variable rate repo/reverse repo auctions.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-32", year: 2014, no: 32, subject: "economy", topic: "Business Correspondents",
  q: `What is/are the facility/facilities the beneficiaries can get from the services of Business Correspondent (Bank Saathi) in branchless areas?
<ol><li>It enables the beneficiaries to draw their subsidies and social security benefits in their villages.</li><li>It enables the beneficiaries in the rural areas to make deposits and withdrawals.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> BCs disburse DBT subsidies, MGNREGA wages, pensions and other social security payments at the doorstep, often using Aadhaar-enabled payment systems (AePS).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> they handle small deposits and withdrawals, account opening, remittances and recovery of small loans.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>RBI allowed the <b>Business Correspondent / Business Facilitator</b> model in 2006 (H.R. Khan's recommendations) to extend banking to unbanked areas.</li>
<li>BCs can be retired bank employees, kirana shops, post offices, SHGs, NGOs, CSCs; "Bank Mitra" under PMJDY.</li>
<li>Business Facilitators only help (identify customers, collect papers) and cannot handle cash.</li>
<li>Over 15 lakh BC outlets; key to PMJDY's reach.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-33", year: 2014, no: 33, subject: "economy", topic: "Statutory reserve requirements",
  q: `In the context of Indian economy, which of the following is/are the purpose/purposes of 'Statutory Reserve Requirements'?
<ol><li>To enable the Central Bank to control the amount of advances the banks can create</li><li>To make the people's deposits with banks safe and liquid</li><li>To prevent the commercial banks from making excessive profits</li><li>To force the banks to have sufficient vault cash to meet their day-to-day requirements</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<div class="note">Debated key: many argue statement 2 is also a purpose (SLR keeps part of deposits in safe, liquid assets). UPSC's key is (a).</div>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> CRR and SLR are tools of credit control; raising them reduces the funds banks can lend (credit creation).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong, per key):</b> safety and liquidity of deposits is a side effect; UPSC treats monetary control as the purpose. Deposit safety is mainly the job of prudential regulation and deposit insurance (DICGC, ₹5 lakh cover).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> reserve requirements are not meant to limit bank profits.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> vault cash for daily needs is kept by banks on their own; CRR is held with the RBI, and cash in hand does not count towards CRR.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>CRR</b>: Section 42 of the RBI Act; held as balances with RBI; earns no interest; 3% (2025).</li>
<li><b>SLR</b>: Section 24 of the Banking Regulation Act; cash, gold, unencumbered government securities; 18%.</li>
<li>Raising them is contractionary (anti-inflation); lowering them is expansionary.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-34", year: 2014, no: 34, subject: "ir", topic: "Arab Spring",
  q: `Recently, a series of uprisings of people referred to as 'Arab Spring' originally started from`,
  opts: ["Egypt", "Lebanon", "Syria", "Tunisia"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Tunisia</h3>
<p>The <b>Arab Spring</b> began in <b>Tunisia</b> in December 2010 after street vendor <b>Mohamed Bouazizi</b> set himself on fire in Sidi Bouzid. The "Jasmine Revolution" toppled President Ben Ali (January 2011) and protests spread across the Arab world.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Egypt's Tahrir Square protests (January 2011) came after Tunisia and ousted Hosni Mubarak.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Lebanon saw only limited protests in 2011.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Syria's protests (March 2011) turned into a long civil war; Assad fell in December 2024.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Outcomes: Libya (Gaddafi killed, 2011; civil war), Yemen (Saleh ousted; civil war since 2014), Bahrain (protests crushed).</li>
<li>Tunisia was seen as the only democratic success; the Tunisian National Dialogue Quartet won the Nobel Peace Prize 2015. President Saied's 2021 power grab reversed much of it.</li>
<li>Indian evacuation: Operation Safe Homecoming (Libya, 2011).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-35", year: 2014, no: 35, subject: "ir", topic: "Arctic Council",
  q: `Consider the following countries:
<ol><li>Denmark</li><li>Japan</li><li>Russian Federation</li><li>United Kingdom</li><li>United States of America</li></ol>
Which of the above are the members of the 'Arctic Council'?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "2, 3 and 4", "1, 4 and 5", "1, 3 and 5"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 3 and 5</h3>
<p>The <b>Arctic Council</b> (Ottawa Declaration, 1996) has eight members, the countries with territory in the Arctic: <b>Canada, Denmark (Greenland), Finland, Iceland, Norway, Russia, Sweden, USA</b>.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Denmark is a member through Greenland and the Faroe Islands.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Japan is an <b>observer</b> (2013).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Russia is a member (it has the longest Arctic coastline).</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> the UK is an observer (1998).</li>
<li><b>5 (correct):</b> the USA is a member through Alaska.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Six indigenous organisations are Permanent Participants (e.g. Inuit Circumpolar Council, Saami Council).</li>
<li>13 observer States, including <b>India, China, Japan, South Korea, Singapore, Italy</b> (all 2013), UK, France, Germany.</li>
<li>Work paused in 2022 after Russia's invasion of Ukraine; resumed partly at working level. Chair: Norway (2023–25), then Denmark (2025–27).</li>
<li>Northern Sea Route: shorter Asia–Europe shipping as ice melts.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-36", year: 2014, no: 36, subject: "ir", topic: "Regions and countries in news",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<table><tr><th>Region often in news</th><th>Country</th></tr><tr><td>1. Chechnya</td><td>Russian Federation</td></tr><tr><td>2. Darfur</td><td>Mali</td></tr><tr><td>3. Swat Valley</td><td>Iraq</td></tr></table>
Which of the above pairs is/are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Pair by pair</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (correct):</b> <b>Chechnya</b> is a republic of the <b>Russian Federation</b> in the North Caucasus (capital Grozny); two Chechen wars (1994–96, 1999–2009).</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (wrong):</b> <b>Darfur</b> is in western <b>Sudan</b>; genocide from 2003 (Janjaweed militias); fresh atrocities by the RSF in the war since 2023 (El Fasher fell in 2025).</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (wrong):</b> the <b>Swat Valley</b> is in <b>Pakistan</b> (Khyber Pakhtunkhwa); Taliban control 2007–09; Malala Yousafzai is from Swat.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: other regions in the news</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nagorno-Karabakh (Azerbaijan; Armenian population left after Azerbaijan's 2023 offensive).</li>
<li>Donbas (Ukraine), Crimea (annexed by Russia 2014).</li>
<li>Balochistan (Pakistan), Xinjiang (China), Rakhine (Myanmar), Tigray (Ethiopia), Sahel (Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-37", year: 2014, no: 37, subject: "science", topic: "Agni-IV",
  q: `With reference to Agni-IV Missile, which of the following statements is/are correct?
<ol><li>It is a surface-to-surface missile.</li><li>It is fuelled by liquid propellant only.</li><li>It can deliver one-tonne nuclear warheads about 7500 km away.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Agni-IV is a <b>surface-to-surface</b> intermediate-range ballistic missile.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> it is a two-stage <b>solid-propellant</b> missile (solid fuel allows quick launch and road mobility).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> its range is about <b>4,000 km</b> with a one-tonne warhead, not 7,500 km.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Agni series (DRDO)</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Missile</th><th>Range</th></tr>
<tr><td>Agni-I</td><td>700–900 km</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agni-II</td><td>2,000+ km</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agni-III</td><td>3,000+ km</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agni-IV</td><td>about 4,000 km</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agni-V</td><td>5,000+ km (ICBM class, canisterised); <b>MIRV</b> test under Mission Divyastra (March 2024)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agni-Prime (Agni-P)</td><td>1,000–2,000 km, new generation; rail-based launcher test (2025)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Integrated Guided Missile Development Programme (1983, A.P.J. Abdul Kalam): Prithvi, Agni, Trishul, Akash, Nag. Strategic Forces Command operates the nuclear arsenal.</p>` },

{ id: "2014-38", year: 2014, no: 38, subject: "geography", topic: "Coalbed methane and shale gas",
  q: `With reference to two non-conventional energy sources called 'coalbed methane' and 'shale gas', consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Coalbed methane is the pure methane gas extracted from coal seams, while shale gas is a mixture of propane and butane only that can be extracted from fine-grained sedimentary rocks.</li><li>In India, abundant coalbed methane sources exist, but so far no shale gas sources have been found.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> CBM is mostly methane (not perfectly pure; some CO<sub>2</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>). <b>Shale gas</b> is also <b>mainly methane</b> (natural gas trapped in shale), not just propane and butane (those make LPG).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> India has identified shale gas resources in Cambay, KG, Cauvery, Damodar valley and Assam-Arakan basins (2016 Q74); ONGC drilled its first shale well in Durgapur (2013).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>CBM</b>: in India, about 2,600 billion cubic metres prospective resources; commercial production in Raniganj (West Bengal), Jharia and Bokaro (Jharkhand), Sohagpur (MP).</li>
<li>Extraction: CBM by dewatering coal seams; shale gas by horizontal drilling and fracking (guar gum is used, Q40).</li>
<li>Policy: HELP (2016) uniform licence for all hydrocarbons; Open Acreage Licensing; target to raise gas share in energy mix to 15% by 2030.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-39", year: 2014, no: 39, subject: "culture", topic: "Changpa community",
  q: `With reference to 'Changpa' community of India, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>They live mainly in the State of Uttarakhand.</li><li>They rear the Pashmina goats that yield a fine wool.</li><li>They are kept in the category of Scheduled Tribes.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the <b>Changpa</b> (Changthang-pa) are semi-nomadic pastoralists of the <b>Changthang plateau in Ladakh</b> (eastern Ladakh, near Pangong and Tso Moriri), not Uttarakhand.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> they rear <b>Changthangi (Pashmina) goats</b>, whose fine undercoat gives pashmina wool, along with yaks and sheep.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> they are a <b>Scheduled Tribe</b> (listed under J&amp;K's ST order of 1989; now in the Ladakh UT).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Changthangi goats survive at -40°C; the cold produces the fine fibre (12–15 microns).</li>
<li>Kashmir Pashmina has a GI tag. Shahtoosh (from the Tibetan antelope/chiru) is banned.</li>
<li>Threats: shrinking pastures (border tensions with China, infrastructure), climate change, younger generation leaving.</li>
<li>Other pastoral communities: Gaddis (HP), Bakarwals and Gujjars (J&amp;K), Bhotiyas (Uttarakhand), Raikas/Rebaris (Rajasthan), Kurumas (Deccan).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-40", year: 2014, no: 40, subject: "geography", topic: "Guar",
  q: `In India, cluster bean (Guar) is traditionally used as a vegetable or animal feed, but recently the cultivation of this has assumed significance. Which one of the following statements is correct in this context?`,
  opts: ["The oil extracted from seeds is used in the manufacture of biodegradable plastics", "The gum made from its seeds is used in the extraction of shale gas", "The leaf extract of this plant has the properties of anti-histamines", "It is a source of high quality biodiesel"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) The gum made from its seeds is used in the extraction of shale gas</h3>
<p><b>Guar gum</b>, from the endosperm of guar seeds, is used as a thickener in <b>hydraulic fracturing</b> fluid; it helps carry sand (proppant) into the cracks in shale rock. The US shale boom (2011–12) caused a huge spike in guar prices and exports.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Guar seeds are not an oil source for biodegradable plastics (those use starch, PLA, PHA).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Guar leaf extract is not known as an antihistamine.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Guar is not a biodiesel feedstock (jatropha, pongamia, used cooking oil are).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India produces about 80% of the world's guar; <b>Rajasthan</b> grows most of it (also Haryana, Gujarat). Drought-hardy legume; fixes nitrogen.</li>
<li>Other uses: food thickener (E412: ice cream, sauces), textiles, paper, cosmetics, pharmaceuticals.</li>
<li>Guar is traded on NCDEX (futures).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-41", year: 2014, no: 41, subject: "geography", topic: "Coral reefs in India",
  q: `Which of the following have coral reefs?
<ol><li>Andaman and Nicobar Islands</li><li>Gulf of Kachchh</li><li>Gulf of Mannar</li><li>Sunderbans</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the <b>Andaman and Nicobar Islands</b> have fringing and barrier reefs (Mahatma Gandhi Marine NP, Rani Jhansi Marine NP).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the <b>Gulf of Kachchh</b> has fringing reefs in the Marine National Park (Jamnagar), the northernmost reefs in India.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> the <b>Gulf of Mannar</b> has reefs around 21 islands (Gulf of Mannar Marine NP, Biosphere Reserve).</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> the <b>Sundarbans</b> has muddy, sediment-laden, low-salinity water from the Ganga-Brahmaputra delta, which blocks the sunlight corals need; it has mangroves, not coral reefs.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Lakshadweep has atolls (the only atolls in India). Also small patches at Malvan (Maharashtra), Netrani island (Karnataka), Gaveshani bank.</li>
<li>Coral needs: warm (20–30°C), clear, shallow, saline water. Corals and zooxanthellae algae are mutualists; heat causes bleaching (fourth global bleaching event, 2023–25).</li>
<li>Corals are protected under Schedule I of the Wild Life Act.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-42", year: 2014, no: 42, subject: "geography", topic: "Soil erosion",
  q: `In India, the problem of soil erosion is associated with which of the following?
<ol><li>Terrace cultivation</li><li>Deforestation</li><li>Tropical climate</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<div class="note">Debated key: heavy tropical monsoon rain also causes erosion, so some argued for (2 and 3). UPSC's key is (b).</div>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> <b>terrace cultivation</b> is a soil <b>conservation</b> method on slopes; it slows runoff and checks erosion.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>deforestation</b> removes the root cover that binds soil, exposing it to rain and wind; a leading cause of erosion (Himalayas, Chambal ravines).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong, per key):</b> climate type alone is not the cause; erosion arises from removing vegetation and poor land use.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Types: sheet, rill, gully (ravines of Chambal), wind (Rajasthan), stream-bank erosion.</li>
<li>Other causes: overgrazing, shifting cultivation (jhum), mining, faulty farming (up-down slope ploughing).</li>
<li>Conservation: contour ploughing, strip cropping, terracing, shelter belts, afforestation, check dams, mulching.</li>
<li>About 30% of India's land is degraded (ISRO desertification atlas); LDN target 26 million ha by 2030.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-43", year: 2014, no: 43, subject: "geography", topic: "Monsoon climate",
  q: `The seasonal reversal of winds is the typical characteristic of`,
  opts: ["Equatorial climate", "Mediterranean climate", "Monsoon climate", "All of the above climates"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Monsoon climate</h3>
<p>"Monsoon" comes from the Arabic <i>mausim</i> (season). Its defining feature is a <b>seasonal reversal of wind direction</b>: in India, south-west winds from the sea in summer (rainy) and north-east winds from the land in winter (mostly dry, except Tamil Nadu coast).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Equatorial climate has uniform conditions all year with convectional rain (2015 Q80); no wind reversal.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Mediterranean climate has dry summers and winter rain from shifting westerlies, but not a reversal of winds.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong, since only monsoon climate has the reversal.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: theories of the monsoon</h3>
<ul>
<li>Classical thermal concept (differential heating of land and sea, Halley).</li>
<li>Dynamic concept: seasonal shift of the <b>ITCZ</b> (Flohn).</li>
<li>Jet streams: the subtropical westerly jet shifts north of the Himalayas and the tropical easterly jet sets in.</li>
<li>Tibetan plateau heating, El Niño/La Niña, Indian Ocean Dipole, MJO influence rainfall.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-44", year: 2014, no: 44, subject: "culture", topic: "Panchayatana",
  q: `With reference to the cultural history of India, the term 'Panchayatan' refers to`,
  opts: ["an assembly of village elders", "a religious sect", "a style of temple construction", "an administrative functionary"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) a style of temple construction</h3>
<p>A <b>Panchayatana</b> temple has a main shrine at the centre of a platform with <b>four subsidiary shrines at the four corners</b> (five shrines in all). Examples: <b>Dashavatara temple, Deogarh</b> (Gupta, early form), Kandariya Mahadeva and Lakshmana temples (Khajuraho), Brahmeswara temple (Bhubaneswar).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> An assembly of village elders is a panchayat (sabha, ur), not panchayatana.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> It is not a sect; though Adi Shankara's <b>Panchayatana puja</b> (worship of five deities: Shiva, Vishnu, Devi, Surya, Ganesha) uses the same root.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> It was not an official's title.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: temple terms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Garbhagriha (sanctum), mandapa (hall), shikhara (Nagara spire), vimana (Dravida tower), gopuram (gateway), amalaka and kalasha (top of Nagara shikhara), antarala (vestibule), jagati (platform).</li>
<li>Styles: Nagara (north), Dravida (south), Vesara (mixed, Chalukya/Hoysala).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-45", year: 2014, no: 45, subject: "geography", topic: "Rivers of Arunachal Pradesh",
  q: `Consider the following rivers:
<ol><li>Barak</li><li>Lohit</li><li>Subansiri</li></ol>
Which of the above flows/flow through Arunachal Pradesh?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the <b>Barak</b> rises in Manipur's hills (Senapati) and flows through Manipur, Mizoram border and Assam (Barak valley, Silchar) into Bangladesh (Surma and Kushiyara); it does not enter Arunachal.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the <b>Lohit</b> enters India from Tibet into eastern Arunachal and joins the Brahmaputra system; the Dhola-Sadiya (Bhupen Hazarika) bridge spans it.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> the <b>Subansiri</b> flows from Tibet through Arunachal into Assam; the Subansiri Lower hydro project (2,000 MW) is on the Arunachal-Assam border.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Brahmaputra in Arunachal</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tsangpo enters Arunachal as the <b>Siang</b> (Dihang), joined by the <b>Dibang</b> and <b>Lohit</b> near Sadiya to form the Brahmaputra.</li>
<li>Other Arunachal rivers: Kameng, Tawang chu, Noa-Dihing, Tirap, Kamla.</li>
<li>China began a mega dam on the Yarlung Tsangpo at Medog (2025); India plans the Siang Upper Multipurpose Project as a buffer.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-46", year: 2014, no: 46, subject: "geography", topic: "Wetlands and river confluences",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<table><tr><th>Wetlands</th><th>Confluence of rivers</th></tr><tr><td>1. Harike Wetlands</td><td>Confluence of Beas and Satluj/Sutlej</td></tr><tr><td>2. Keoladeo Ghana National Park</td><td>Confluence of Banas and Chambal</td></tr><tr><td>3. Kolleru Lake</td><td>Confluence of Musi and Krishna</td></tr></table>
Which of the above pairs is/are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Pair by pair</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (correct):</b> <b>Harike</b> (Punjab), formed by the Harike headworks (1953) at the <b>Beas–Sutlej confluence</b>; Ramsar site (1990), source of the Indira Gandhi canal.</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (wrong):</b> <b>Keoladeo</b> (Bharatpur, Rajasthan) is fed by the <b>Gambhir and Banganga</b> rivers and the Ajan bund, not the Banas–Chambal confluence.</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (wrong):</b> <b>Kolleru</b> (Andhra Pradesh) lies between the <b>Godavari and Krishna deltas</b>, fed by Budameru and Tammileru; the Musi joins the Krishna far upstream in Telangana.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Kolleru: one of India's largest freshwater lakes; Ramsar site (2002); threatened by aquaculture encroachment (Operation Kolleru, 2006).</li>
<li>Keoladeo: Ramsar (1981), WHS (1985), Montreux Record (2014 Q2).</li>
<li>Other confluence wetlands: Ropar (Punjab, Sutlej), Pong (Beas reservoir).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-47", year: 2014, no: 47, subject: "culture", topic: "Six systems of Indian philosophy",
  q: `Which one of the following pairs does not form part of the six systems of Indian Philosophy?`,
  opts: ["Mimamsa and Vedanta", "Nyaya and Vaisheshika", "Lokayata and Kapalika", "Sankhya and Yoga"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Lokayata and Kapalika</h3>
<p>The six orthodox (<b>astika</b>, accepting Vedic authority) systems (<b>shad-darshana</b>) are in three pairs: <b>Sankhya–Yoga</b>, <b>Nyaya–Vaisheshika</b>, <b>Mimamsa–Vedanta</b>. <b>Lokayata</b> (Charvaka) is a heterodox materialist school; <b>Kapalika</b> is a tantric Shaiva ascetic sect, not a darshana.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Mimamsa (Jaimini; Vedic ritual) and Vedanta (Badarayana's Brahma Sutras; Upanishads) are both orthodox systems.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Nyaya (Gautama; logic) and Vaisheshika (Kanada; atomism) are orthodox systems.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Sankhya (Kapila; purusha and prakriti, dualism) and Yoga (Patanjali; eight limbs) are orthodox systems.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Heterodox (nastika): Charvaka/Lokayata (Brihaspati; only perception counts), Buddhism, Jainism, Ajivika.</li>
<li>Vedanta schools: Advaita (Shankara), Vishishtadvaita (Ramanuja), Dvaita (Madhva), Dvaitadvaita (Nimbarka), Shuddhadvaita (Vallabha).</li>
<li>Shaiva sects: Pashupata (Lakulisha), Kapalika, Kalamukha, Lingayat, Kashmir Shaivism.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-48", year: 2014, no: 48, subject: "geography", topic: "Hills and regions",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<table><tr><th>Hills</th><th>Region</th></tr><tr><td>1. Cardamom Hills</td><td>Coromandel Coast</td></tr><tr><td>2. Kaimur Hills</td><td>Konkan Coast</td></tr><tr><td>3. Mahadeo Hills</td><td>Central India</td></tr><tr><td>4. Mikir Hills</td><td>North-East India</td></tr></table>
Which of the above pairs are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "2 and 3", "3 and 4", "2 and 4"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4</h3>
<h3>Pair by pair</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (wrong):</b> the <b>Cardamom Hills</b> are in the southern Western Ghats (Kerala–Tamil Nadu), near the Malabar coast, not the Coromandel coast.</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (wrong):</b> the <b>Kaimur Hills</b> are the eastern part of the Vindhyas (MP, UP, Bihar), far from the Konkan coast.</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (correct):</b> the <b>Mahadeo Hills</b> are part of the Satpura range in <b>Madhya Pradesh</b> (Pachmarhi, Dhupgarh).</li>
<li><b>Pair 4 (correct):</b> the <b>Mikir (Karbi Anglong) Hills</b> are in <b>Assam</b>, south of Kaziranga.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: hill ranges by region</h3>
<ul>
<li>Southern hills: Nilgiris, Anaimalai (Anamudi, highest in south India), Palani, Cardamom.</li>
<li>Vindhya: Bharner, Kaimur (Rohtas plateau). Satpura: Rajpipla, Mahadeo, Maikal (Amarkantak).</li>
<li>North-east: Garo, Khasi, Jaintia (Meghalaya); Mikir, North Cachar (Assam); Patkai, Naga, Lushai/Mizo hills.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-49", year: 2014, no: 49, subject: "polity", topic: "Anti-defection",
  q: `Which one of the following Schedules of the Constitution of India contains provisions regarding anti-defection?`,
  opts: ["Second Schedule", "Fifth Schedule", "Eighth Schedule", "Tenth Schedule"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Tenth Schedule</h3>
<p>The <b>Tenth Schedule</b> (anti-defection law) was added by the <b>52nd Amendment (1985)</b>. A member is disqualified for voluntarily giving up party membership or voting against the party whip (unless condoned within 15 days). The presiding officer decides; the decision is subject to judicial review (<b>Kihoto Hollohan, 1992</b>).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Second Schedule: salaries and allowances of the President, Governors, judges, CAG, Speaker, etc.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Fifth Schedule: Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Eighth Schedule: 22 official languages.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Exception: <b>merger</b> when at least two-thirds of the legislature party agree. The 91st Amendment (2003) deleted the one-third "split" exception and capped ministry size at 15%.</li>
<li>Independent members joining a party, and nominated members joining after six months, are disqualified.</li>
<li>Courts asked Speakers to decide petitions within a reasonable time (three months, Keisham Meghachandra, 2020).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2014-50", year: 2014, no: 50, subject: "environment", topic: "Biosphere reserves",
  q: `The most important strategy for the conservation of biodiversity together with traditional human life is the establishment of`,
  opts: ["biosphere reserves", "botanical gardens", "national parks", "wildlife sanctuaries"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) biosphere reserves</h3>
<p><b>Biosphere reserves</b> (UNESCO's <b>Man and the Biosphere Programme</b>, 1971) combine conservation with sustainable use by local people. Zones: <b>core</b> (strictly protected), <b>buffer</b> (research, education, limited use) and <b>transition</b> (settlements, farming, sustainable development).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Botanical gardens are <b>ex-situ</b> conservation of plants; no traditional human life.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> National parks are strictly protected; no human activity like grazing or habitation is allowed.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wildlife sanctuaries allow some activities, but are focused on wildlife, not integrating human life.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: India's biosphere reserves</h3>
<ul>
<li>18 biosphere reserves; first: <b>Nilgiri</b> (1986); largest: Gulf of Kachchh (Kutch); latest: Panna (2011).</li>
<li>13 are in UNESCO's World Network: Nilgiri, Gulf of Mannar, Sundarbans, Nanda Devi, Nokrek, Pachmarhi, Similipal, Achanakmar-Amarkantak, Great Nicobar, Agasthyamala, Khangchendzonga, Panna, Cold Desert (2025).</li>
</ul>` }

);
