// UPSC CSE Prelims 2013, GS Paper I, Series A, Q26–50.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2013-26", year: 2013, no: 26, subject: "polity", topic: "Forest Rights Act",
  q: `Under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006, who shall be the authority to initiate the process for determining the nature and extent of individual or community forest rights or both?`,
  opts: ["State Forest Department", "District Collector/Deputy Commissioner", "Tahsildar/Block Development Officer/Mandal Revenue Officer", "Gram Sabha"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Gram Sabha</h3>
<p>Under <b>Section 6(1)</b> of the Forest Rights Act, the <b>Gram Sabha</b> initiates the process: it receives claims, verifies them through its Forest Rights Committee and passes a resolution. Claims then go to the Sub-Divisional Level Committee and the District Level Committee (chaired by the Collector), whose decision is final.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Forest Department is represented on the committees but cannot initiate the process; giving it control was what the Act set out to change.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The District Collector chairs the District Level Committee that gives final approval, not the initiation.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The SDO/Tahsildar level is the Sub-Divisional Level Committee, which examines Gram Sabha resolutions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: FRA 2006</h3>
<ul>
<li>Rights: individual forest rights (up to 4 ha of land under cultivation before 13 December 2005), community rights (minor forest produce, grazing, fishing), <b>community forest resource</b> rights (protect and manage forests).</li>
<li>Other Traditional Forest Dwellers must prove 75 years (three generations) of residence.</li>
<li>Nodal ministry: <b>Tribal Affairs</b>. Niyamgiri case (Orissa Mining Corp v. MoEF, 2013): Gram Sabhas of the Dongria Kondh rejected bauxite mining.</li>
<li>Recent: the Forest (Conservation) Amendment Act 2023 (Van Sanrakshan Evam Samvardhan Adhiniyam) raised concerns about FRA consent; Dharti Aaba Janjatiya Gram Utkarsh Abhiyan (2024) includes FRA support cells.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-27", year: 2013, no: 27, subject: "science", topic: "Aflatoxins",
  q: `Improper handling and storage of cereal grains and oilseeds result in the production of toxins known as aflatoxins which are not generally destroyed by normal cooking process. Aflatoxins are produced by`,
  opts: ["bacteria", "protozoa", "moulds", "viruses"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) moulds</h3>
<p><b>Aflatoxins</b> are produced by moulds (fungi) <b><i>Aspergillus flavus</i></b> and <i>A. parasiticus</i>, which grow on groundnuts, maize, cottonseed, chillies and other crops stored warm and damp. They are heat-stable, so cooking does not destroy them. Aflatoxin B1 is a potent liver carcinogen (IARC Group 1).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Bacteria cause food poisoning (e.g. <i>Salmonella</i>, <i>Clostridium botulinum</i> making botulinum toxin), but not aflatoxins.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Protozoa cause amoebiasis, giardiasis; they do not make aflatoxins.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Viruses (e.g. norovirus, hepatitis A) spread through food but produce no toxins of this kind.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>FSSAI limits aflatoxin in foods (e.g. 15 µg/kg in groundnuts for further processing, 10 µg/kg ready-to-eat); aflatoxin M1 in milk from contaminated feed.</li>
<li>Other mycotoxins: ergot (rye), ochratoxin, fumonisin.</li>
<li>Prevention: dry grains below about 10% moisture, good storage, sorting.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-28", year: 2013, no: 28, subject: "polity", topic: "Economic justice",
  q: `'Economic Justice' as one of the objectives of the Indian Constitution has been provided in`,
  opts: ["the Preamble and the Fundamental Rights", "the Preamble and the Directive Principles of State Policy", "the Fundamental Rights and the Directive Principles of State Policy", "None of the above"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) the Preamble and the Directive Principles of State Policy</h3>
<p>The <b>Preamble</b> secures "Justice, social, economic and political". <b>Article 38</b> (DPSP) directs the State to secure a social order in which social, economic and political justice informs all institutions; Article 39 elaborates economic justice (livelihood, equitable distribution of resources, equal pay).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Fundamental Rights mainly secure political and civil liberties (political justice), not economic justice as an objective.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Same problem: FRs are not where economic justice is provided as an objective.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong, since (b) applies.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The ideal of social, economic and political justice was taken from the <b>Russian Revolution (1917)</b>; liberty, equality, fraternity from the French Revolution.</li>
<li>Preamble: "Sovereign Socialist Secular Democratic Republic" ("socialist", "secular", "integrity" added by the 42nd Amendment, 1976; upheld in Balram Singh, November 2024).</li>
<li>DPSPs aim at socio-economic democracy and a welfare state (2015 Q7, Q89).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-29", year: 2013, no: 29, subject: "environment", topic: "E-waste pollutants",
  q: `Due to improper/indiscriminate disposal of old and used computers or their parts, which of the following are released into the environment as e-waste?
<ol><li>Beryllium</li><li>Cadmium</li><li>Chromium</li><li>Heptachlor</li><li>Mercury</li><li>Lead</li><li>Plutonium</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
  opts: ["1, 3, 4, 6 and 7 only", "1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 only", "2, 4, 5 and 7 only", "1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> <b>beryllium</b> in motherboards and connectors (beryllium-copper alloys); causes lung disease.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>cadmium</b> in batteries, chip resistors, old CRTs; kidney damage.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> hexavalent <b>chromium</b> in corrosion protection of steel parts; carcinogenic.</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> <b>heptachlor</b> is an organochlorine <b>pesticide</b> (a POP), not in computers.</li>
<li><b>5 (correct):</b> <b>mercury</b> in switches, older LCD backlights (fluorescent lamps).</li>
<li><b>6 (correct):</b> <b>lead</b> in CRT glass and solder; neurotoxic.</li>
<li><b>7 (wrong):</b> <b>plutonium</b> is a nuclear material, not in computers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: e-waste in India</h3>
<ul>
<li>India is the third-largest e-waste generator (after China and the USA); about 14 lakh tonnes in 2024–25 (CPCB, EPR series; earlier figures differ), with formal collection rising to about 70%, though the informal sector (Seelampur, Moradabad) still handles much of it.</li>
<li><b>E-Waste (Management) Rules, 2022</b> (in force April 2023): EPR with tradable certificates, targets rising to 80%; minimum EPR price (2024).</li>
<li>Global E-waste Monitor 2024: 62 million tonnes worldwide in 2022, only about 22% recycled.</li>
<li>Also: brominated flame retardants (2014 Q16), PVC, rare earths.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-30", year: 2013, no: 30, subject: "environment", topic: "Acid rain",
  q: `Acid rain is caused by the pollution of environment by`,
  opts: ["carbon dioxide and nitrogen", "carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide", "ozone and carbon dioxide", "nitrous oxide and sulphur dioxide"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) nitrous oxide and sulphur dioxide</h3>
<p>Acid rain (pH below about 5.6) forms when <b>sulphur dioxide</b> and <b>nitrogen oxides</b> (NO<sub>x</sub>) from burning coal, oil and vehicles react with water vapour to form <b>sulphuric and nitric acids</b>. (Strictly, the culprit is nitrogen oxides, NO and NO<sub>2</sub>; nitrous oxide, N<sub>2</sub>O, is mainly a greenhouse gas.)</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> CO<sub>2</sub> makes rain only slightly acidic (normal pH 5.6); atmospheric nitrogen (N<sub>2</sub>) is inert.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> CO does not form acids in rain.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Ozone is an oxidant in smog, not an acid-forming gas.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Effects: damage to forests (Black Forest, Germany), lakes (Scandinavia), buildings and monuments (<b>Taj Mahal yellowing</b>, "stone cancer"; Mathura refinery, Taj Trapezium Zone, M.C. Mehta 1996).</li>
<li>Control: flue gas desulphurisation (FGD), low-sulphur fuels, BS-VI (April 2020), catalytic converters.</li>
<li>SO<sub>2</sub>: India is among the largest emitters; FGD norms (2015) were diluted in 2025 for many plants.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-31", year: 2013, no: 31, subject: "environment", topic: "Food chains",
  q: `With reference to food chains in ecosystems, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>A food chain illustrates the order in which a chain of organisms feed upon each other.</li><li>Food chains are found within the populations of a species.</li><li>A food chain illustrates the numbers of each organism which are eaten by others.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3", "None"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> a food chain shows who eats whom: the sequence of energy transfer from producers to consumers (grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → eagle).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> food chains link <b>different species</b> across trophic levels, not individuals within one species' population.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> a food chain does not show numbers; that is shown by the <b>pyramid of numbers</b> (and biomass/energy pyramids).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Food web: interlinked food chains; more stable than a single chain.</li>
<li>Energy pyramid is always upright (10% law); pyramid of numbers can be inverted (a tree supporting many insects); biomass pyramid inverted in oceans.</li>
<li>Biomagnification: DDT, mercury increase up the chain (Minamata disease).</li>
<li>Asked in 2014 Q80 (diatoms → crustaceans → herrings).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-32", year: 2013, no: 32, subject: "geography", topic: "Rivers through national parks",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<table><tr><th>National Park</th><th>River flowing through the Park</th></tr><tr><td>1. Corbett National Park</td><td>Ganga</td></tr><tr><td>2. Kaziranga National Park</td><td>Manas</td></tr><tr><td>3. Silent Valley National Park</td><td>Kaveri</td></tr></table>
Which of the above pairs is/are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "3 only", "1 and 3", "None"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) None</h3>
<h3>Pair by pair</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (wrong):</b> <b>Corbett</b> (Uttarakhand, India's first national park, 1936 as Hailey NP) is drained by the <b>Ramganga</b> (and Kosi along its edge), not the Ganga.</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (wrong):</b> <b>Kaziranga</b> (Assam) lies on the south bank of the <b>Brahmaputra</b>, with the Diphlu and Mora Diphlu rivers; the Manas river flows through <b>Manas National Park</b>.</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (wrong):</b> <b>Silent Valley</b> (Palakkad, Kerala) is drained by the <b>Kunthipuzha</b> (a tributary of the Bharathapuzha); the Kaveri does not flow through it. Its Silent Valley hydro project was dropped after the "Save Silent Valley" movement (1970s–80s).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: parks and rivers</h3>
<ul>
<li>Manas (Assam): Manas river; WHS; tiger and biosphere reserve. Ranthambore: Chambal and Banas. Kanha: Banjar and Halon. Panna: Ken. Bandhavgarh: Son nearby. Periyar: Periyar river. Nagarhole: Kabini.</li>
<li>Kaziranga: WHS (1985), one-horned rhinos (about 2,600+, 2022 census).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-33", year: 2013, no: 33, subject: "science", topic: "Biofertilizers",
  q: `Consider the following organisms:
<ol><li>Agaricus</li><li>Nostoc</li><li>Spirogyra</li></ol>
Which of the above is/are used as biofertilizer/biofertilizers?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "2 only", "2 and 3", "3 only"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> <b>Agaricus</b> is the common edible mushroom (button mushroom), a fungus grown as food, not used as biofertiliser.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>Nostoc</b> is a cyanobacterium (blue-green alga) with <b>heterocysts</b> that fix atmospheric nitrogen; used in paddy fields.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> <b>Spirogyra</b> is a green alga (pond silk) that does not fix nitrogen.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: biofertilisers</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nitrogen fixers: <i>Rhizobium</i> (legume root nodules, symbiotic), <i>Azotobacter</i>, <i>Azospirillum</i> (free-living), cyanobacteria (<i>Anabaena</i>, <i>Nostoc</i>), <b>Azolla</b> fern with <i>Anabaena azollae</i> (rice).</li>
<li>Phosphate solubilisers: <i>Bacillus</i>, <i>Pseudomonas</i>; mycorrhiza (Glomus) improves P uptake (2013 Q97).</li>
<li>Schemes: Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana, National Mission on Natural Farming (2024), PM-PRANAM.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-34", year: 2013, no: 34, subject: "environment", topic: "Nitrogen cycle",
  q: `Which of the following adds/add nitrogen to the soil?
<ol><li>Excretion of urea by animals</li><li>Burning of coal by man</li><li>Death of vegetation</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<div class="note">Debated key: burning coal releases nitrogen oxides that can return to the soil as nitric acid/nitrates in rain, so some argued for (d). UPSC's key is (c).</div>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> animal excretions (urea, uric acid) are broken down by bacteria into ammonia (ammonification), then nitrites and nitrates (nitrification).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong, per key):</b> burning coal mainly releases nitrogen oxides into the <b>air</b> as pollutants; it is not considered a way of adding nitrogen to the soil.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> dead plants decompose, returning their organic nitrogen to the soil as ammonium.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: nitrogen cycle</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fixation: biological (Rhizobium, cyanobacteria), lightning, industrial (Haber–Bosch for fertilisers).</li>
<li>Nitrification: <i>Nitrosomonas</i> (NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> → NO<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>), <i>Nitrobacter</i> (NO<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup> → NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>).</li>
<li>Denitrification: <i>Pseudomonas</i>, <i>Thiobacillus</i> return N<sub>2</sub> to air (waterlogged soils).</li>
<li>India: Indian Nitrogen Assessment (2017); nitrous oxide from fertilisers is a strong GHG.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-35", year: 2013, no: 35, subject: "environment", topic: "Lion-tailed macaque",
  q: `In which of the following States is lion-tailed macaque found in its natural habitat?
<ol><li>Tamil Nadu</li><li>Kerala</li><li>Karnataka</li><li>Andhra Pradesh</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3>
<p>The <b>lion-tailed macaque</b> (<i>Macaca silenus</i>; silver-white mane, tufted tail) is <b>endemic to the Western Ghats</b>, living in tropical evergreen rainforests of Kerala, Karnataka and Tamil Nadu (e.g. Silent Valley, Anamalai, Sharavathi valley, Kalakad-Mundanthurai). IUCN: <b>Endangered</b>; Schedule I.</p>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Tamil Nadu: Anamalai hills, Kalakad-Mundanthurai.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Kerala: Silent Valley (its flagship species), Nelliyampathy, Periyar.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Karnataka: Sharavathi valley, Kudremukh (the northernmost population).</li>
<li><b>4 (wrong):</b> Andhra Pradesh lies in the Eastern Ghats; not part of its range.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>One of the rarest primates; about 4,000 in the wild in fragmented populations; threatened by plantations (tea, coffee) and roads.</li>
<li>Western Ghats endemics: Nilgiri tahr, Nilgiri marten, Malabar civet, purple frog, Malabar grey hornbill.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-36", year: 2013, no: 36, subject: "culture", topic: "Chaitya and Vihara",
  q: `Some Buddhist rock-cut caves are called Chaityas, while the others are called Viharas. What is the difference between the two?`,
  opts: ["Vihara is a place of worship, while Chaitya is the dwelling place of the monks", "Chaitya is a place of worship, while Vihara is the dwelling place of the monks", "Chaitya is the stupa at the far end of the cave, while Vihara is the hall axial to it", "There is no material difference between the two"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Chaitya is a place of worship, while Vihara is the dwelling place of the monks</h3>
<p>A <b>chaitya</b> is a prayer hall with a stupa at the apsidal (rounded) end, often with a horseshoe-shaped window (e.g. <b>Karle</b>, Bhaja, Ajanta Caves 9, 10, 19, 26). A <b>vihara</b> is a monastery with cells around a central hall where monks lived (e.g. most Ajanta caves).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Reverses the two.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The stupa is <b>inside</b> the chaitya hall; the vihara is a separate residential structure, not the hall in front of it.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> They differ in purpose and plan.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Earliest chaityas: Barabar (Ajivika; Lomas Rishi façade), Bhaja (2nd century BCE). Largest chaitya: Karle (1st century CE).</li>
<li>Sangharama: monastery complex; Mahavihara: large monastic university (Nalanda, Vikramashila, Odantapuri).</li>
<li>Repeated theme: Buddhist architecture (2023 Q42 on stupas).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-37", year: 2013, no: 37, subject: "ancient", topic: "Nirvana",
  q: `Which one of the following describes best the concept of Nirvana in Buddhism?`,
  opts: ["The extinction of the flame of desire", "The complete annihilation of self", "A state of bliss and rest", "A mental stage beyond all comprehension"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) The extinction of the flame of desire</h3>
<p><b>Nirvana</b> (Pali: Nibbana) literally means "blowing out", the extinguishing of the fires of <b>craving (tanha), hatred and delusion</b>, which ends rebirth and suffering. It follows from the Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) arises from desire; ending desire ends suffering through the Eightfold Path.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Buddhism rejects annihilationism (ucchedavada); nirvana is not destruction of a self (there is no permanent self, anatta, to annihilate).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Nirvana brings peace, but "bliss and rest" describes an effect, not the core concept.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> It is said to be beyond ordinary description, but the defining idea is the extinction of desire.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Three marks of existence: anicca (impermanence), dukkha (suffering), anatta (no-self).</li>
<li>Eightfold Path: right view, intention, speech, action, livelihood, effort, mindfulness, concentration (middle path).</li>
<li>Parinirvana: final nirvana at death (Kushinagar). Jainism's equivalent: kaivalya and moksha.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-38", year: 2013, no: 38, subject: "polity", topic: "Fundamental in governance",
  q: `According to the Constitution of India, which of the following are fundamental for the governance of the country?`,
  opts: ["Fundamental Rights", "Fundamental Duties", "Directive Principles of State Policy", "Fundamental Rights and Fundamental Duties"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Directive Principles of State Policy</h3>
<p><b>Article 37</b>: the DPSPs "shall not be enforceable by any court, but the principles therein laid down are nevertheless <b>fundamental in the governance of the country</b> and it shall be the duty of the State to apply these principles in making laws".</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Fundamental Rights are justiciable limits on the State (Art 13, 32), but the Constitution does not describe them with this phrase.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Fundamental Duties (Art 51A) are duties of citizens, not described as fundamental in governance.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Same as above; the phrase is used only for DPSPs.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ambedkar: DPSPs are "instruments of instructions" (like those under the GoI Act 1935).</li>
<li>Minerva Mills (1980): harmony and balance between FRs and DPSPs is part of the basic structure.</li>
<li>Asked repeatedly: 2015 Q7, Q89; 2014 Q55.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-39", year: 2013, no: 39, subject: "modern", topic: "Simon Commission",
  q: `The people of India agitated against the arrival of Simon Commission because`,
  opts: ["Indians never wanted the review of the working of the Act of 1919", "Simon Commission recommended the abolition of Dyarchy (Diarchy) in the Provinces", "there was no Indian member in the Simon Commission", "the Simon Commission suggested the partition of the country"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) there was no Indian member in the Simon Commission</h3>
<p>The <b>Indian Statutory Commission</b> (November 1927), headed by <b>Sir John Simon</b>, had seven British MPs (including Clement Attlee) and <b>no Indian</b>, though it was to decide India's constitutional future. It was boycotted with "Simon Go Back" when it arrived in February 1928. <b>Lala Lajpat Rai</b> died after a police lathi charge in Lahore (1928).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Indians had demanded an early review of the 1919 Act; they objected to the all-white composition, not the review.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Commission's report (1930) did recommend abolishing provincial dyarchy, but that came after the protests and was not the reason for them.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> It did not suggest partition.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Response: <b>Nehru Report</b> (1928, Motilal Nehru): dominion status, joint electorates; Jinnah's 14 Points (1929).</li>
<li>Simon report led to the Round Table Conferences and the GoI Act 1935 (provincial autonomy).</li>
<li>Appointed under Lord Irwin; Secretary of State Lord Birkenhead.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-40", year: 2013, no: 40, subject: "modern", topic: "Quit India Movement",
  q: `Quit India Movement was launched in response to`,
  opts: ["Cabinet Mission Plan", "Cripps Proposals", "Simon Commission Report", "Wavell Plan"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Cripps Proposals</h3>
<p>The failure of the <b>Cripps Mission (March–April 1942)</b>, which offered only post-war Dominion status and a provincial opt-out, convinced Congress that Britain would not transfer real power. The <b>Quit India Resolution</b> was passed at Bombay (Gowalia Tank) on <b>8 August 1942</b>; Gandhi gave the call <b>"Do or Die"</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Cabinet Mission came in 1946, after Quit India.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Simon Commission report (1930) led to the Round Table Conferences.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Wavell Plan and Simla Conference were in 1945, after Quit India.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Quit India</h3>
<ul>
<li>Leaders arrested on 9 August (Operation Zero Hour); movement became leaderless and spontaneous; underground leaders JP, Ram Manohar Lohia, Aruna Asaf Ali, Usha Mehta (Congress Radio).</li>
<li>Parallel governments: Ballia (Chittu Pande), Tamluk (Jatiya Sarkar), Satara (Prati Sarkar).</li>
<li>Muslim League, Communists, Hindu Mahasabha and princes stayed away.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-41", year: 2013, no: 41, subject: "economy", topic: "Balance of payments",
  q: `The balance of payments of a country is a systematic record of`,
  opts: ["all import and export transactions of a country during a given period of time, normally a year", "goods exported from a country during a year", "economic transaction between the government of one country to another", "capital movements from one country to another"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) all import and export transactions of a country during a given period of time, normally a year</h3>
<p>The <b>BoP</b> records all economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world over a period: goods, services, income, transfers (current account), and capital and financial flows (capital account). Of the options, (a) is the closest description.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Goods exports alone are only part of the trade balance.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> BoP covers all residents (firms, households), not just governments.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Capital movements are only the capital account.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>BoP always balances in accounting terms: current account + capital account + errors and omissions = change in reserves.</li>
<li>India's 1991 BoP crisis led to liberalisation; reserves covered barely three weeks of imports.</li>
<li>2014 Q30: current account = trade + invisibles. India's exports hit a record $863 billion in 2025–26.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-42", year: 2013, no: 42, subject: "economy", topic: "RBI regulation of banks",
  q: `The Reserve Bank of India regulates the commercial banks in matters of
<ol><li>liquidity of assets</li><li>branch expansion</li><li>merger of banks</li><li>winding-up of banks</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
  opts: ["1 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> liquidity: CRR, SLR, Liquidity Coverage Ratio under the RBI Act and Banking Regulation Act.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> branch licensing/expansion (Section 23, BR Act); now general permission with norms such as opening 25% of new branches in unbanked rural centres.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> amalgamation needs RBI approval (Section 44A); RBI can frame compulsory amalgamation schemes (Section 45, e.g. Lakshmi Vilas Bank with DBS, 2020).</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> RBI can apply for winding up (Section 38) and impose moratoriums; it also cancels licences.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other powers: licensing, appointment of directors/CEOs, inspection, supersession of boards (e.g. YES Bank 2020), Prompt Corrective Action framework.</li>
<li>Banking Laws (Amendment) Act 2025: up to four nominees per account (from 1 November 2025), "substantial interest" threshold raised from ₹5 lakh to ₹2 crore, longer tenure for cooperative bank directors.</li>
<li>Deposit insurance (DICGC): ₹5 lakh per depositor per bank.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-43", year: 2013, no: 43, subject: "economy", topic: "Bank Rate",
  q: `An increase in the Bank Rate generally indicates that the`,
  opts: ["market rate of interest is likely to fall", "Central Bank is no longer making loans to commercial banks", "Central Bank is following an easy money policy", "Central Bank is following a tight money policy"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Central Bank is following a tight money policy</h3>
<p>The <b>Bank Rate</b> is the rate at which the RBI lends long-term funds to banks (Section 49, RBI Act). Raising it makes borrowing costlier, signalling a <b>tight (dear) money policy</b> to curb credit and inflation.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> A higher bank rate pushes market rates <b>up</b>, not down.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The RBI still lends; it just charges more.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> An easy money policy means <b>cutting</b> rates.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Since 2012 the Bank Rate is aligned with the <b>MSF rate</b> (repo + 0.25%): <b>5.75%</b> after the 7 October 2026 repo hike to 5.50%.</li>
<li>Penal interest on CRR/SLR shortfalls is linked to the bank rate.</li>
<li>The repo rate is now the main policy signal (MPC).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-44", year: 2013, no: 44, subject: "economy", topic: "Deficit financing",
  q: `In India, deficit financing is used for raising resources for`,
  opts: ["economic development", "redemption of public debt", "adjusting the balance of payments", "reducing the foreign debt"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) economic development</h3>
<p><b>Deficit financing</b> means financing a budget deficit by borrowing or (historically) by printing money/borrowing from the RBI. In India's plan era it was used to raise resources for <b>economic development</b> (investment in infrastructure and industry) beyond tax revenues.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Redeeming public debt is repayment, the opposite of raising resources through deficits.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> BoP adjustment uses reserves, exchange rate and IMF support, not budget deficit financing.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Deficit financing adds to debt; it does not reduce foreign debt.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Automatic monetisation (ad hoc Treasury Bills) ended in 1997 (RBI–Government agreement, 1994); the FRBM Act 2003 bars RBI from subscribing to primary G-sec issues except in emergencies.</li>
<li>Risks: inflation, crowding out private investment, debt trap.</li>
<li>Fiscal deficit target 4.3% of GDP for 2026–27.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-45", year: 2013, no: 45, subject: "ancient", topic: "Indus Civilisation people",
  q: `Which of the following characterizes/characterize the people of Indus Civilization?
<ol><li>They possessed great palaces and temples.</li><li>They worshipped both male and female deities.</li><li>They employed horse-drawn chariots in warfare.</li></ol>
Select the correct statement/statements using the codes given below.`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3", "None of the statements given above is correct"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> no grand <b>palaces or temples</b> have been found in Harappan cities; public buildings include the Great Bath, granaries and assembly halls.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> they worshipped a <b>Mother Goddess</b> (terracotta figurines) and a male deity ("<b>Proto-Shiva</b>"/Pashupati seal with animals), plus trees (pipal) and animals.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> there is no evidence of horse-drawn chariots; the horse was not significant (disputed Surkotada remains). Spoked-wheel chariots appear with the Vedic people.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Harappan civilisation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Town planning: grid streets, covered drains, burnt bricks (1:2:4 ratio), citadel and lower town.</li>
<li>Sites: Harappa (Ravi), Mohenjo-daro (Indus), Dholavira (water management, signboard; WHS 2021), Lothal (dockyard), Kalibangan (ploughed field, fire altars), Rakhigarhi (largest in India; DNA study 2019).</li>
<li>Script undeciphered; seals with unicorn motif.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-46", year: 2013, no: 46, subject: "science", topic: "Disease spread through tattooing",
  q: `Which of the following diseases can be transmitted from one person to another through tattooing?
<ol><li>Chikungunya</li><li>Hepatitis B</li><li>HIV-AIDS</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> <b>chikungunya</b> (an alphavirus) spreads through bites of <i>Aedes aegypti</i> and <i>Aedes albopictus</i> mosquitoes, not through needles.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>Hepatitis B</b> spreads through blood and body fluids, so unsterilised tattoo needles and ink can transmit it (also hepatitis C).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> <b>HIV</b> also spreads through infected blood on shared needles.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Hepatitis B: vaccine in the UIP (birth dose); India aims to eliminate viral hepatitis by 2030 (National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme, 2018).</li>
<li>HIV: about 25 lakh people living with HIV in India; NACO; ART is free; HIV/AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017.</li>
<li>Chikungunya: first approved vaccine Ixchiq (US FDA, 2023; licence suspended on 22 August 2025 over serious safety concerns).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-47", year: 2013, no: 47, subject: "ancient", topic: "Jain doctrine",
  q: `Which of the following statements is/are applicable to Jain doctrine?
<ol><li>The surest way of annihilating Karma is to practice penance.</li><li>Every object, even the smallest particle has a soul.</li><li>Karma is the bane of the soul and must be ended.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Jainism stresses <b>severe penance (tapas)</b> and asceticism, including fasting unto death (sallekhana), to burn off accumulated karma (nirjara).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Jainism holds that <b>everything has a soul (jiva)</b>: not only humans and animals but plants, earth, water, fire and air bodies; hence extreme ahimsa.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> karma is subtle matter that sticks to the soul and binds it to rebirth; it must be ended to attain <b>kaivalya</b> and moksha.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes: Jainism</h3>
<ul>
<li>Three jewels (triratna): right faith, right knowledge, right conduct.</li>
<li>Five vows: ahimsa, satya, asteya, aparigraha, brahmacharya (added by Mahavira).</li>
<li>Anekantavada and syadvada (many-sidedness of truth). Sects: Digambara (Bhadrabahu) and Svetambara (Sthulabhadra); split after the famine in Chandragupta Maurya's time.</li>
<li>Councils: Pataliputra (3rd century BCE), Vallabhi (5th century CE, canon written).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-48", year: 2013, no: 48, subject: "environment", topic: "Ecological niche",
  q: `Which one of the following terms describes not only the physical space occupied by an organism, but also its functional role in the community of organisms?`,
  opts: ["Ecotone", "Ecological niche", "Habitat", "Home range"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Ecological niche</h3>
<p>An <b>ecological niche</b> (Grinnell, Elton, Hutchinson) is the full role of a species: where it lives plus what it eats, when it is active, how it interacts with others and its effect on the environment. Two species cannot occupy the same niche indefinitely (<b>Gause's competitive exclusion principle</b>).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> An <b>ecotone</b> is a transition zone between two ecosystems (e.g. mangroves, grassland–forest edge).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> A <b>habitat</b> is only the physical place where an organism lives (its "address"), not its role (its "profession").</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> A <b>home range</b> is the area an animal normally moves through for food and mates.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Edge effect: ecotones often have more species.</li>
<li>Keystone species (e.g. tiger, fig trees) have outsized roles; flagship and umbrella species are conservation terms.</li>
<li>Asked again: 2014 Q84 (ecosystem definition).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-49", year: 2013, no: 49, subject: "environment", topic: "Photochemical smog",
  q: `Photochemical smog is a resultant of the reaction among`,
  opts: ["NO2, O3 and peroxyacetyl nitrate in the presence of sunlight", "CO, O2 and peroxyacetyl nitrate in the presence of sunlight", "CO, CO2 and NO2 at low temperature", "high concentration of NO2, O3 and CO in the evening"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) NO2, O3 and peroxyacetyl nitrate in the presence of sunlight</h3>
<p><b>Photochemical (Los Angeles) smog</b> forms on warm, sunny days when nitrogen oxides and volatile organic compounds from vehicles react in <b>sunlight</b> to form <b>ozone</b>, <b>PAN</b> (peroxyacetyl nitrate), aldehydes and NO<sub>2</sub>. It is oxidising, brownish, and irritates eyes and lungs.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> CO and O<sub>2</sub> are not the key reactants of photochemical smog.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Low temperature and these gases describe neither type correctly; photochemical smog needs warmth and sunlight.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> It peaks around midday and afternoon with strong sunlight, not in the evening.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Classical (London) smog</b>: smoke + SO<sub>2</sub> + fog in cold, humid conditions; reducing in nature (Great Smog of London, 1952).</li>
<li>Delhi's winter smog mixes both, worsened by stubble burning, inversion and low winds; GRAP stages by AQI.</li>
<li>Ground-level ozone is one of the eight AQI pollutants.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2013-50", year: 2013, no: 50, subject: "science", topic: "Minerals in muscle contraction",
  q: `Consider the following minerals:
<ol><li>Calcium</li><li>Iron</li><li>Sodium</li></ol>
Which of the minerals given above is/are required by human body for the contraction of muscles?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<div class="note">Debated key: iron is not directly part of the contraction mechanism, so many expected (c). UPSC's key counts iron because myoglobin and haemoglobin supply the oxygen that working muscles need.</div>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> <b>calcium</b> released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin and lets actin and myosin interact; it triggers contraction.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct, per key):</b> <b>iron</b> in myoglobin (stores oxygen in muscle) and haemoglobin supports aerobic energy production for sustained contraction; iron deficiency causes muscle fatigue.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> <b>sodium</b> (with potassium) creates the action potential that carries the nerve signal to the muscle fibre.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Potassium: repolarisation; deficiency causes cramps and arrhythmia. Magnesium: muscle relaxation.</li>
<li>ATP powers the myosin heads (sliding filament theory).</li>
<li>Anaemia: about 57% of women aged 15–49 (NFHS-5); Anaemia Mukt Bharat (2023 Q52).</li>
</ul>` }

);
;
