// UPSC CSE Prelims 2011, GS Paper I, Series A, Q26-50. Answers: official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2011-26", year: 2011, no: 26, subject: "modern", topic: "Marx: dialectical materialism",
  q: `Karl Marx explained the process of class struggle with the help of which one of the following theories?`,
  opts: ["Empirical liberalism", "Existentialism", "Darwin's theory of evolution", "Dialectical materialism"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Dialectical materialism</h3>
<p>Marx adapted Hegel's dialectic (thesis–antithesis–synthesis) to material conditions. Change comes from contradictions in the mode of production, expressed as <b>class struggle</b> (slave owners vs slaves, lords vs serfs, bourgeoisie vs proletariat). Applied to history, this is <b>historical materialism</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Empirical liberalism (Locke, Hume, Mill) stresses individual rights and experience; Marx critiqued liberalism.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Existentialism (Kierkegaard, Sartre) is about individual freedom and meaning, not class.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Marx admired Darwin, but class struggle is not explained by biological evolution.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Key works: Communist Manifesto (1848, with Engels), Das Kapital (1867). Concepts: surplus value, alienation, base and superstructure, dictatorship of the proletariat.</li>
<li>Indian links: M.N. Roy founded the Communist Party of India at Tashkent (1920); the CPI dates its founding in India to Kanpur (1925).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-27", year: 2011, no: 27, subject: "geography", topic: "Ionosphere and radio",
  q: `A layer in the Earth's atmosphere called Ionosphere facilitates radio communication. Why?
<ol><li>The presence of ozone causes the reflection of radio waves to Earth.</li><li>Radio waves have a very long wavelength.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the ionosphere (about 80–600 km) reflects radio waves because of <b>free electrons and ions</b> made by solar UV and X-rays. Ozone is in the stratosphere and absorbs UV; it does not reflect radio waves.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> wavelength alone is not the reason; the reflection depends on the ionised layer. Radio wavelengths range from millimetres to kilometres, and higher frequencies (VHF and above) pass through the ionosphere.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Layers: D (absorbs, disappears at night), E (Kennelly–Heaviside), F1/F2 (Appleton). Skywave (shortwave, HF) propagation bounces off the F layer; satellite links use frequencies that pass through.</li>
<li>Solar storms disturb the ionosphere (GPS and HF blackouts); the May 2024 geomagnetic storm caused auroras visible from Ladakh. Aditya-L1 studies the Sun from L1.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-28", year: 2011, no: 28, subject: "economy", topic: "FDI vs FII",
  q: `Both Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) and Foreign Institutional Investor (FII) are related to investment in a country. Which one of the following statements best represents an important difference between the two?`,
  opts: ["FII helps bring better management skills and technology, while FDI only brings in capital", "FII helps in increasing capital availability in general, while FDI only targets specific sectors", "FDI flows only into the secondary market, while FII targets primary market", "FII is considered to be more stable than FDI"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) FII increases capital availability in general, while FDI targets specific sectors</h3>
<p>FII money flows into the stock and bond markets broadly; FDI goes into particular enterprises and sectors with a lasting interest and control.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Reversed: <b>FDI</b> brings management, technology and market access; FII brings only financial capital.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Wrong: FDI goes into companies directly (greenfield/brownfield), not the secondary market; FIIs trade mainly in the secondary market.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Reversed: FII ("hot money") is volatile; FDI is more stable.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Threshold: 10% or more of a listed company's equity is FDI; below 10% is portfolio investment (FPI). FIIs are now called <b>Foreign Portfolio Investors</b> (SEBI FPI Regulations 2014/2019).</li>
<li>Net FDI into India fell sharply in 2024–25 because of high repatriation, though gross inflows stayed above $80 bn. FPIs sold heavily in 2025 and again during the 2026 oil-price shock.</li>
<li>Budget 2025–26 raised the FDI cap in insurance to 100%.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-29", year: 2011, no: 29, subject: "science", topic: "Bt brinjal objective",
  q: `A genetically engineered form of brinjal, known as the Bt-brinjal, has been developed. The objective of this is`,
  opts: ["To make it pest-resistant", "To improve its taste and nutritive qualities", "To make it drought-resistant", "To make its shelf-life longer"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) To make it pest-resistant</h3>
<p>The <b>cry1Ac</b> gene from the soil bacterium <i>Bacillus thuringiensis</i> makes the plant produce a protein toxic to the <b>fruit and shoot borer</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Taste or nutrition is not altered; nutritional traits are the aim of crops like Golden Rice (beta-carotene).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Drought tolerance is a different trait (e.g. HB4 wheat in Argentina).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Shelf life was the aim of the Flavr Savr tomato.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>GEAC cleared Bt brinjal in 2009, but a <b>moratorium</b> was imposed in February 2010 and still continues. Bangladesh has grown it since 2014. Bt cotton (2002) is India's only commercial GM crop; GM mustard DMH-11 was conditionally approved in 2022 (litigation in the Supreme Court).</li>
<li>Genome-edited (non-GM) rice varieties were released by ICAR in May 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-30", year: 2011, no: 30, subject: "economy", topic: "Aam Admi Bima Yojana",
  q: `With reference to "Aam Admi Bima Yojana", consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The member insured under the scheme must be the head of the family or an earning member of the family in a rural landless household.</li><li>The member insured must be in the age group of 30 to 65 years.</li><li>There is a provision for free scholarship for up to two children of the insured who are studying between classes 9 and 12.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> launched in 2007 (LIC), it covered the head or one earning member of a rural landless household (later other occupational groups).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> the age band was <b>18 to 59</b> years.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> the add-on <b>Shiksha Sahyog Yojana</b> gave ₹100 a month (per child) for up to two children in classes 9–12.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>AABY was converged with <b>PMJJBY</b> (life, ₹2 lakh for ₹436 a year, age 18–50) and <b>PMSBY</b> (accident, ₹2 lakh for ₹20 a year, age 18–70) from 2017–18.</li>
<li>Social security now sits under the <b>Code on Social Security 2020</b> (in force 21 November 2025), which also covers gig and platform workers (registered on e-Shram).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-31", year: 2011, no: 31, subject: "economy", topic: "Brent crude",
  q: `In the context of global oil prices, "Brent crude oil" is frequently referred to in the news. What does this term imply?
<ol><li>It is a major classification of crude oil.</li><li>It is sourced from North Sea.</li><li>It does not contain sulphur.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["2 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Brent is one of the main benchmark grades (with WTI and Dubai/Oman) used to price about two-thirds of the world's crude.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> it comes from <b>North Sea</b> fields (Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk, Troll; WTI Midland was added to the basket in 2023).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> it is "sweet" (low sulphur, about 0.4%), not sulphur-free.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's crude basket is mostly Dubai/Oman-linked (sour) grades plus Brent-linked sweet grades. India imports about 88% of its crude; Russia became the top supplier after 2022.</li>
<li><b>Latest:</b> the Iran war (from 28 February 2026) and the Strait of Hormuz disruption pushed Brent above $100 a barrel; it was around $100–103 in September–October 2026.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-32", year: 2011, no: 32, subject: "science", topic: "Heavy water in reactors",
  q: `The function of heavy water in a nuclear reactor is to`,
  opts: ["Slow down the speed of neutrons", "Increase the speed of neutrons", "Cool down the reactor", "Stop the nuclear reaction"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Slow down the speed of neutrons</h3>
<p>Heavy water (D<sub>2</sub>O) is a <b>moderator</b>: it slows fast neutrons to thermal speeds so they can split U-235 efficiently. Because deuterium absorbs few neutrons, natural (unenriched) uranium can be used.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Moderators slow neutrons; fast breeder reactors (like PFBR) use no moderator.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> In Indian PHWRs heavy water is also the coolant, but the question asks for its key function, which is moderation.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Control rods (boron, cadmium) absorb neutrons to stop or control the reaction.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's PHWRs (220 MW and indigenous 700 MW units at Kakrapar, Rajasthan) form stage 1 of the three-stage programme. Heavy Water Board plants make D<sub>2</sub>O; India also exports it.</li>
<li>PFBR (stage 2) reached first criticality on 6 April 2026. The <b>SHANTI Act 2025</b> opened nuclear power to private players; the target is 100 GW by 2047.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-33", year: 2011, no: 33, subject: "polity", topic: "National minority status",
  q: `In India, if a religious sect/community is given the status of a national minority, what special advantages it is entitled to?
<ol><li>It can establish and administer exclusive educational institutions.</li><li>The President of India automatically nominates a representative of the community to Lok Sabha.</li><li>It can derive benefits from the Prime Minister's 15-Point Programme.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> <b>Art 30</b> gives religious and linguistic minorities the right to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> the President had power to nominate only two <b>Anglo-Indians</b> to the Lok Sabha (Art 331), and that provision ended in 2020 (104th Amendment).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> the PM's New 15-Point Programme (2006) targets notified minorities (education, jobs, credit, housing).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Six notified minorities under the NCM Act 1992: Muslims, Christians, Sikhs, Buddhists, Parsis and <b>Jains</b> (added 2014).</li>
<li>Minority status is decided State-wise (T.M.A. Pai, 2002). In November 2024 the Supreme Court overruled the 1967 Azeez Basha judgment, laying down tests for whether an institution (like AMU) is a minority institution.</li>
<li>Waqf (Amendment) Act 2025 (renamed UMEED Act): the Supreme Court stayed some provisions in September 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-34", year: 2011, no: 34, subject: "polity", topic: "Rights of persons with disabilities",
  q: `India is home to lakhs of persons with disabilities. What are the benefits available to them under the law?
<ol><li>Free schooling till the age of 18 years in government-run schools.</li><li>Preferential allotment of land for setting up business.</li><li>Ramps in public buildings.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the PwD Act 1995 (and now the RPwD Act 2016, Section 31) gives free education to children with benchmark disabilities from <b>6 to 18</b> years.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the law provides preferential allotment of land at concessional rates for housing, business, recreation and special schools.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> barrier-free access, including ramps in public buildings, is mandated.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>RPwD Act 2016</b>: 21 disabilities (up from 7), <b>4%</b> reservation in government jobs, <b>5%</b> in higher education, guardianship, special courts.</li>
<li>Accessible India Campaign (2015). In <b>Rajive Raturi v UoI</b> (November 2024) the Supreme Court held that accessibility rules must be mandatory, not just guidelines; revised rules followed in 2025.</li>
<li>India ratified the UN CRPD in 2007.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-35", year: 2011, no: 35, subject: "economy", topic: "Mega Food Parks",
  q: `With what purpose is the Government of India promoting the concept of "Mega Food Parks"?
<ol><li>To provide good infrastructure facilities for the food processing industry.</li><li>To increase the processing of perishable items and reduce wastage.</li><li>To provide emerging and eco-friendly food processing technologies to entrepreneurs.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the codes given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> parks provide common infrastructure (cold chain, warehousing, testing labs, roads, power) for processing units.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> the scheme linked farmers to processors to raise processing of perishables and cut wastage (cluster, hub-and-spoke model with collection centres).</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong, per key):</b> providing processing technologies to entrepreneurs was not a stated objective; units bring their own technology.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Launched 2008 by MoFPI; later a component of PMKSY. The scheme <b>was discontinued after 31 March 2021</b> (only committed liabilities): of 41 approved parks, 24 are operational. The smaller <b>Agro Processing Cluster</b> scheme (about 10 acres) replaced it.</li>
<li>Other food-processing schemes: PLI for food processing, PM-FME (micro enterprises, One District One Product), Operation Greens. World Food India 2025 was held in New Delhi.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-36", year: 2011, no: 36, subject: "polity", topic: "Withdrawal from Consolidated Fund",
  q: `The authorization for the withdrawal of funds from the Consolidated Fund of India must come from`,
  opts: ["The President of India", "The Parliament of India", "The Prime Minister of India", "The Union Finance Minister"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) The Parliament of India</h3>
<p><b>Art 266(3)</b>: no money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund except under law (appropriation). Under <b>Art 114</b>, Parliament passes the Appropriation Act; even charged expenditure needs an appropriation law, though it is not voted.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The President recommends money bills and controls the <b>Contingency Fund</b>, but cannot authorise withdrawal from the Consolidated Fund.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The PM has no such power.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Finance Minister presents the Budget but needs Parliament's law.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The CAG (Art 148) audits spending after the fact; the PAC examines the CAG's reports.</li>
<li>Vote on account (Art 116) allows withdrawal for part of the year before the full Budget passes.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-37", year: 2011, no: 37, subject: "polity", topic: "Consolidated Fund of India",
  q: `All revenues received by the Union Government by way of taxes and other receipts for the conduct of Government business are credited to the`,
  opts: ["Contingency Fund of India", "Public Account", "Consolidated Fund of India", "Deposits and Advances Fund"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Consolidated Fund of India</h3>
<p><b>Art 266(1)</b>: all revenues, loans raised and repayments of loans received go into the Consolidated Fund.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Contingency Fund (Art 267, ₹30,000 crore since 2021) is an imprest at the President's disposal for unforeseen expenses.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Public Account (Art 266(2)) holds money the government holds as a <b>banker</b> or trustee (provident funds, small savings, deposits); withdrawals need no appropriation.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Deposits and advances are part of the Public Account, not where revenues go.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Charged expenditure on the Consolidated Fund (Art 112(3)): President's emoluments, salaries of judges of the SC, CAG, UPSC chair, debt charges.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-38", year: 2011, no: 38, subject: "economy", topic: "Microfinance services",
  q: `Microfinance is the provision of financial services to people of low-income groups. This includes both the consumers and the self-employed. The service/services rendered under microfinance is/are:
<ol><li>Credit facilities</li><li>Savings facilities</li><li>Insurance facilities</li><li>Fund Transfer facilities</li></ol>
Select the correct answer using the codes given below the lists:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> microcredit (small loans without collateral, often to SHGs or joint-liability groups).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> savings accounts and thrift through SHGs.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> micro-insurance (life, health, crop, livestock).</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> remittances and money transfer services.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Models: SHG–Bank Linkage (NABARD, 1992), NBFC-MFIs, Grameen-type joint-liability groups. RBI's 2022 framework set a household income cap of ₹3 lakh and repayment limits (50% of income).</li>
<li>Microfinance stress rose in 2024–25 because of over-lending; the industry tightened guardrails (no more than three lenders per borrower).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-39", year: 2011, no: 39, subject: "ir", topic: "Southeast Asia's geostrategic importance",
  q: `Southeast Asia has captivated the attention of global community over space and time as a geostrategically significant region. Which among the following is the most convincing explanation for this global perspective?`,
  opts: ["It was the hot theatre during the Second World War", "Its location between the Asian powers of China and India", "It was the arena of superpower confrontation during the Cold War period", "Its location between the Pacific and Indian oceans and its pre-eminent maritime character"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) location between the Pacific and Indian oceans and its maritime character</h3>
<p>The straits of <b>Malacca</b>, Sunda and Lombok carry a large share of world trade and energy; this lasting maritime position explains interest "over space and time".</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> WWII was a passing episode.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> True, but secondary to its maritime position.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Cold War (Vietnam) was also temporary.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>"Malacca dilemma" for China; South China Sea disputes (2016 arbitral award for the Philippines). India's Act East Policy, BrahMos exports to the Philippines (2024) and Indonesia.</li>
<li>Timor-Leste became ASEAN's 11th member in October 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-40", year: 2011, no: 40, subject: "science", topic: "Trans fats",
  q: `A company marketing food products advertises that its items do not contain trans-fats. What does this campaign signify to the customers?
<ol><li>The food products are not made out of hydrogenated oils.</li><li>The food products are not made out of animal fats/oils.</li><li>The oils used are not likely to damage the cardiovascular health of the consumers.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> industrial trans fats come from <b>partial hydrogenation</b> of vegetable oils (vanaspati).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> "no trans fat" says nothing about animal fats; some ruminant fats (milk, butter, meat) even contain small natural trans fats.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> trans fats raise LDL and lower HDL, increasing heart disease risk, so avoiding them protects the heart.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>FSSAI capped industrial trans fats at <b>2%</b> of total fat in oils and foods from January 2022, in line with WHO's REPLACE goal of eliminating industrial trans fats.</li>
<li>Other FSSAI moves: front-of-pack labelling proposals, Eat Right India, limits on reusing cooking oil (total polar compounds above 25% banned).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-41", year: 2011, no: 41, subject: "economy", topic: "MGNREGA eligibility",
  q: `Among the following who are eligible to benefit from the "Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act"?`,
  opts: ["Adult members of only the scheduled caste and scheduled tribe households", "Adult members of below poverty line (BPL) households", "Adult members of households of all backward communities", "Adult members of any household"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Adult members of any household</h3>
<p>MGNREGA (2005) guaranteed 100 days of unskilled manual wage work a year to <b>any rural household</b> whose adult members volunteered; it was universal and demand-driven.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> SC/ST households get priority for individual works, but eligibility is not limited to them.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> No BPL condition.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> No caste-based condition.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Latest:</b> MGNREGA was replaced by the <b>Viksit Bharat – Guarantee for Rozgar and Ajeevika Mission (Gramin) Act, 2025 (VB-G RAM G)</b>, notified to take effect from <b>1 July 2026</b>. It raises the guarantee to <b>125 days</b>, keeps universal eligibility for rural households, splits funding 60:40 between Centre and States (90:10 for north-eastern and Himalayan States), and lets States pause work for up to 60 days in peak farm seasons. Critics questioned the shift of cost to States and the removal of Gandhi's name.</li>
<li>Earlier features: one-third of beneficiaries women, unemployment allowance if work not given within 15 days, social audits (Section 17).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-42", year: 2011, no: 42, subject: "ir", topic: "Look East Policy",
  q: `With reference to "Look East Policy" of India, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>India wants to establish itself as an important regional player in the East Asian affairs.</li><li>India wants to plug the vacuum created by the termination of Cold War.</li><li>India wants to restore the historical and cultural ties with its neighbours in Southeast and East Asia.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the policy (1991, P.V. Narasimha Rao) aimed to make India a regional player in East Asia.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> India sought new economic partners after the Soviet collapse, but "plugging a vacuum" was not an aim; India had neither the capacity nor the intent.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> restoring historical and cultural ties (Buddhism, Hindu kingdoms, trade) was part of the policy.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Upgraded to <b>Act East Policy</b> (2014), with a stronger security and connectivity focus: India–Myanmar–Thailand Trilateral Highway, Kaladan project, Quad, ASEAN–India Comprehensive Strategic Partnership (2022).</li>
<li>The review of the ASEAN–India Trade in Goods Agreement (AITIGA) is still under negotiation.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-43", year: 2011, no: 43, subject: "polity", topic: "Budget defeat",
  q: `When the annual Union Budget is not passed by the Lok Sabha,`,
  opts: ["the Budget is modified and presented again", "the Budget is referred to the Rajya Sabha for suggestions", "the Union Finance Minister is asked to resign", "the Prime Minister submits the resignation of Council of Ministers"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) the PM submits the resignation of the Council of Ministers</h3>
<p>Defeat of the Budget (a money bill/financial business) in the Lok Sabha means loss of confidence. Under <b>collective responsibility</b> (Art 75(3)), the whole Council of Ministers must resign.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> A defeated Budget is not simply re-presented by the same government.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Rajya Sabha has only recommendatory power on money bills (14 days) and no role after a Lok Sabha defeat.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Responsibility is collective, not just of the Finance Minister.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other ways the government can fall: defeat of a no-confidence motion (Rule 198, 50 members), a cut motion passed, or defeat on a confidence vote.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-44", year: 2011, no: 44, subject: "polity", topic: "Fundamental Duties",
  q: `Under the Constitution of India, which one of the following is not a fundamental duty?`,
  opts: ["To vote in public elections", "To develop the scientific temper", "To safeguard public property", "To abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) To vote in public elections</h3>
<p>Voting is not among the 11 Fundamental Duties in <b>Art 51A</b>. The right to vote is a statutory right (Representation of the People Act 1951).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Art 51A(h): develop scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Art 51A(i): safeguard public property and abjure violence.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Art 51A(a): abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals, institutions, the National Flag and Anthem.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Added by the 42nd Amendment (1976, Swaran Singh Committee); the 11th duty (education for children aged 6–14) was added by the 86th Amendment (2002). Not enforceable by courts directly.</li>
<li>Verma Committee (1999) found existing laws that enforce some duties. Compulsory voting was proposed in Gujarat for local bodies (2009 bill), but is not national law.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-45", year: 2011, no: 45, subject: "economy", topic: "Finance Commission role",
  q: `With reference to the Finance Commission of India, which of the following statements is correct?`,
  opts: ["It encourages the inflow of foreign capital for infrastructure development", "It facilitates the proper distribution of finances among the Public Sector Undertakings", "It ensures transparency in financial administration", "None of the statements (a), (b) and (c) given above is correct in this context"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) None of the above</h3>
<p>Under <b>Art 280</b>, the Finance Commission recommends the division of central tax proceeds between the Centre and States (vertical) and among States (horizontal), grants-in-aid (Art 275), and measures to augment State funds for local bodies.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Foreign capital is handled by the Finance Ministry (DEA), RBI and DPIIT, not the FC.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> It deals with Centre–State finances, not PSUs.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Transparency in financial administration is broader (CAG, Parliament, FRBM disclosures), not the FC's mandate.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>16th Finance Commission</b> (chair Arvind Panagariya; award 2026–31): report submitted November 2025 and tabled with the Budget on 1 February 2026. It kept the States' share at <b>41%</b> and added <b>contribution to GDP</b> as a horizontal criterion, which helps southern States. It declined to bring cesses and surcharges into the divisible pool.</li>
<li>Quasi-judicial body; chair plus four members, appointed by the President every five years.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-46", year: 2011, no: 46, subject: "polity", topic: "UDHR rights",
  q: `Consider the following:
<ol><li>Right to education.</li><li>Right to equal access to public service.</li><li>Right to food.</li></ol>
Which of the above is/are Human Right/Human Rights under "Universal Declaration of Human Rights"?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Article 26: everyone has the right to education; elementary education shall be free and compulsory.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Article 21(2): everyone has the right of equal access to public service in his country.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Article 25: right to a standard of living adequate for health and well-being, including <b>food</b>, clothing, housing and medical care.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>UDHR adopted by the UN General Assembly on <b>10 December 1948</b> (Human Rights Day); drafted by a committee chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt; Hansa Mehta changed "all men" to "all human beings". It has 30 articles and is not legally binding; the ICCPR and ICESCR (1966) made it binding for parties.</li>
<li>India: Protection of Human Rights Act 1993 (NHRC); Right to Education (Art 21A, 2002); National Food Security Act 2013, with PMGKAY free grains extended to 2028.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-47", year: 2011, no: 47, subject: "environment", topic: "Harmful algal blooms",
  q: `There is a concern over the increase in harmful algal blooms in the seawaters of India. What could be the causative factors for this phenomenon?
<ol><li>Discharge of nutrients from the estuaries.</li><li>Run-off from the land during the monsoon.</li><li>Upwelling in the seas.</li></ol>
Select the correct answer from the codes given below:`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> estuaries carry sewage and fertiliser nutrients (N, P) to the sea, feeding blooms (eutrophication).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> monsoon run-off brings a pulse of nutrients from farmland and cities.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> upwelling brings nutrient-rich deep water to the surface, triggering natural blooms.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Harmful blooms (red tides) produce toxins and create <b>dead zones</b> (low oxygen) that kill fish. In the Arabian Sea, <i>Noctiluca scintillans</i> blooms have spread in winter, linked to warming and weaker mixing.</li>
<li>Bioluminescent "blue tides" (Noctiluca) are seen on Goa, Karnataka and Kerala beaches.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-48", year: 2011, no: 48, subject: "environment", topic: "Carbon cycle sources",
  q: `Consider the following:
<ol><li>Photosynthesis</li><li>Respiration</li><li>Decay of organic matter</li><li>Volcanic action</li></ol>
Which of the above add carbon dioxide to the carbon cycle on Earth?`,
  opts: ["1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2, 3 and 4 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> photosynthesis <b>removes</b> CO<sub>2</sub> from the air to make sugars.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> respiration by plants, animals and microbes releases CO<sub>2</sub>.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> decomposers release CO<sub>2</sub> (and methane in low-oxygen conditions).</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> volcanoes release CO<sub>2</sub> from the Earth's interior (about 0.3–0.4 Gt a year, about 1% of human emissions).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other sources: fossil-fuel burning, deforestation, cement making. Sinks: oceans, forests, soils, rock weathering.</li>
<li>Global fossil CO<sub>2</sub> emissions hit a record of about 38 Gt in 2025 (Global Carbon Project).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-49", year: 2011, no: 49, subject: "ir", topic: "Australia Group and Wassenaar Arrangement",
  q: `Recently, the USA decided to support India's membership in multi-lateral export control regimes called the "Australia Group" and the "Wassenaar Arrangement". What is the difference between them?
<ol><li>The Australia Group is an informal arrangement which aims to allow exporting countries to minimize the risk of assisting chemical and biological weapons proliferation, whereas the Wassenaar Arrangement is a formal group under the OECD holding identical objectives.</li><li>The Australia Group comprises predominantly of Asian, African and North American countries, whereas the member countries of Wassenaar Arrangement are predominantly from the European Union and American continents.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the Australia Group (1985) is indeed informal and covers chemical and biological weapons, but the <b>Wassenaar Arrangement</b> (1996) is not under the OECD and does not have identical aims: it controls <b>conventional arms and dual-use goods and technologies</b>. It is also a voluntary, non-treaty arrangement.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> the Australia Group is mainly European and Western countries plus the EU Commission, not mainly Asian and African. Wassenaar members are also mostly European, but include countries like Japan, South Korea, India, Australia and South Africa.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India joined: <b>MTCR</b> (June 2016), <b>Wassenaar</b> (December 2017), <b>Australia Group</b> (January 2018). The <b>NSG</b> membership bid is blocked by China (citing NPT non-signature).</li>
<li>India's SCOMET list aligns with these regimes. The US–India 10-year defence framework (October 2025) and TRUST initiative cover technology controls.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2011-50", year: 2011, no: 50, subject: "science", topic: "Anomalous expansion of water",
  q: `The surface of a lake is frozen in severe winter, but the water at its bottom is still liquid. What is the reason?`,
  opts: ["Ice is a bad conductor of heat", "Since the surface of the lake is at the same temperature as the air, no heat is lost", "The density of water is maximum at 4°C", "None of the statements (a), (b) and (c) given above is correct"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) The density of water is maximum at 4°C</h3>
<p>As the surface cools, water sinks until the whole lake reaches 4°C (densest). Further cooling makes surface water lighter, so it stays on top and freezes; the 4°C water remains at the bottom (<b>anomalous expansion</b>).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Ice is a poor conductor and insulates the water below, which helps, but the root cause is the density maximum at 4°C (UPSC key).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Heat is still lost from the surface to colder air.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong, because (c) is correct.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>This lets aquatic life survive winter. Ice floats because it is about 9% less dense than water (hydrogen-bonded open lattice).</li>
<li>Similar effect: frozen pipes burst because water expands on freezing.</li>
</ul>` }

);
;
