// UPSC CSE Prelims 2010, GS Paper I, Series A (GS questions only), part 2.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2010-30", year: 2010, no: 30, subject: "economy", topic: "FDI via Mauritius",
  q: `A great deal of Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) to India comes from Mauritius than from many major and mature economies like UK and France. Why?`,
  opts: ["India has preference for certain countries as regards receiving FDI", "India has double taxation avoidance agreement with Mauritius", "Most citizens of Mauritius have ethnic identity with India and so they feel secure to invest in India", "Impending dangers of global climatic change prompt Mauritius to make huge investments in India"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) India has a double taxation avoidance agreement with Mauritius</h3>
<p>Under the 1983 DTAA, capital gains on Indian shares were taxable only in Mauritius, which levied no capital gains tax. Global investors routed money through Mauritius shell companies ("round-tripping" and treaty shopping).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> India's FDI policy does not prefer specific countries (apart from the 2020 curbs on land-border countries, Press Note 3).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Ethnic ties exist, but Mauritius' small economy could not supply such sums itself.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Climate change is irrelevant.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The 2016 protocol moved to <b>source-based</b> capital gains tax (on shares bought after April 2017); GAAR came in 2017; a 2024 protocol added a principal purpose test. <b>Singapore</b> became the top FDI source after 2018–19, with Mauritius second.</li>
<li>In <b>AAR v Tiger Global</b> (15 January 2026) the Supreme Court held Tiger Global's Flipkart exit gains taxable in India, denying India–Mauritius treaty relief to a structure lacking substance (GAAR applied).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-32", year: 2010, no: 32, subject: "geography", topic: "Pepper cultivation conditions",
  q: `Following are the characteristics of an area in India:
<ol><li>Hot and humid climate</li><li>Annual rainfall 200 cm</li><li>Hill slopes up to an altitude of 1100 metres</li><li>Annual range of temperature 15°C to 30°C</li></ol>
Which one among the following crops are you most likely to find in the area described above?`,
  opts: ["Mustard", "Cotton", "Pepper", "Virginia tobacco"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Pepper</h3>
<p>Black pepper (a climbing vine) needs hot, humid conditions, heavy rainfall (150–250 cm) and shaded hill slopes up to about 1,500 m: the Western Ghats of Kerala and Karnataka.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Mustard is a rabi crop of cool, dry plains (Rajasthan, Haryana).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Cotton needs moderate rain (50–100 cm), black soil and a dry picking season.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Virginia tobacco grows in light soils of Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka with moderate rain.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pepper is the "King of Spices". Karnataka has overtaken Kerala as the largest producer. Malabar pepper has a GI tag; Vietnam is the world's top producer.</li>
<li>Similar climate crops: cardamom (Queen of Spices), coffee, rubber, areca nut.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-34", year: 2010, no: 34, subject: "science", topic: "Neutrinos and INO",
  q: `India-based Neutrino Observatory is included by the Planning Commission as a mega science project under the 11th Five-Year Plan. In this context, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Neutrinos are chargeless elementary particles that travel close to the speed of light.</li><li>Neutrinos are created in nuclear reactions of beta decay.</li><li>Neutrinos have a negligible, but nonzero mass.</li><li>Trillions of Neutrinos pass through human body every second.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "2, 3 and 4", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> neutrinos are neutral leptons moving at nearly the speed of light, interacting only by the weak force and gravity.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> they are produced in beta decay, fusion in the Sun, reactors, supernovae and cosmic-ray showers.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> neutrino oscillation proves they have a tiny non-zero mass (Nobel 2015: Kajita and McDonald).</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> about 100 trillion solar neutrinos pass through each human body every second.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>INO: a 50,000-tonne magnetised iron calorimeter planned under about 1,200 m of rock at Pottipuram, Theni (Tamil Nadu). It has been stalled for years by environmental objections and NGT litigation (nearness to Mathikettan Shola National Park).</li>
<li>Major detectors: IceCube (Antarctica), Super-Kamiokande (Japan), DUNE (US), JUNO (China, data from 2025). KATRIN capped neutrino mass below 0.45 eV (2025).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-35", year: 2010, no: 35, subject: "polity", topic: "Instrument of Instructions",
  q: `The "Instrument of Instructions" contained in the Government of India Act 1935 have been incorporated in the Constitution of India in the year 1950 as`,
  opts: ["Fundamental Rights", "Directive Principles of State Policy", "Extent of executive power of State", "Conduct of business of the Government of India"], ans: 1, provKey: "D",
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Directive Principles of State Policy</h3>
<p>Ambedkar described the Directive Principles as a "novel feature" resembling the <b>Instrument of Instructions</b> issued to the Governor-General and Governors under the 1935 Act. The difference is that DPSPs are instructions to the legislature and executive, and they are non-justiciable.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Fundamental Rights were inspired by the US Bill of Rights.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Extent of executive power (Arts 73, 162) derives from the federal scheme of the 1935 Act, not the Instrument of Instructions.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Conduct of business (Art 77) is covered by Rules of Business made by the President.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The scanned key shows (d), but standard texts (Ambedkar's speech in the Constituent Assembly; Laxmikanth) link the Instrument of Instructions to the DPSPs, so (b) is given.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sources of DPSPs: Irish Constitution (1937). 1935 Act gave: federal scheme, Governor's office, judiciary, Public Service Commissions, emergency provisions.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-36", year: 2010, no: 36, subject: "science", topic: "Hydrogen fuel cell exhaust",
  q: `Hydrogen fuel cell vehicles produce one of the following as "exhaust"`,
  opts: ["NH₃", "CH₄", "H₂O", "H₂O₂"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) H₂O</h3>
<p>A fuel cell combines hydrogen with oxygen from air: 2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O + electricity + heat. The only exhaust is water vapour.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Ammonia is not produced; there is no nitrogen in the reaction (ammonia is being studied as a hydrogen carrier).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Methane needs carbon, which is absent.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Hydrogen peroxide is not a normal product.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>PEM fuel cells use platinum catalysts. "Green" only if hydrogen comes from electrolysis using renewable power.</li>
<li><b>National Green Hydrogen Mission</b> (2023, ₹19,744 crore): 5 MMT a year by 2030. Hydrogen fuel cell buses run in Leh (NTPC) and Delhi; India's first indigenous hydrogen train began trial runs on the Jind–Sonipat route in early 2026.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-37", year: 2010, no: 37, subject: "science", topic: "LASIK",
  q: `Recently, LASIK (Laser Assisted In Situ Keratomileusis) procedure is being made popular for vision correction. Which one of the following statements in this context is not correct?`,
  opts: ["LASIK procedure is used to correct refractive errors of the eye", "It is a procedure that permanently changes the shapes of the cornea", "It reduces a person's dependence on glasses or contact lenses", "It is a procedure that can be done on the person of any age"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) (not correct)</h3>
<p>LASIK is done only on adults (usually 18 or older) whose prescription has been stable for about a year; it is unsuitable for children, pregnant women, and people with thin corneas or some eye diseases.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Correct: it treats myopia, hypermetropia and astigmatism.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Correct: an excimer laser reshapes the corneal stroma permanently.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Correct: most patients no longer need glasses for distance vision.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Newer methods: SMILE (small-incision lenticule extraction), PRK, ICL. Presbyopia (age-related) is not corrected by standard LASIK.</li>
<li>Myopia is rising fast in Indian children (screen time); the National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment runs school screening.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-38", year: 2010, no: 38, subject: "environment", topic: "Acid rain causes",
  q: `Consider the following:
<ol><li>Oxides of Hydrogen</li><li>Oxides of Nitrogen</li><li>Oxides of Sulphur</li></ol>
Which of the above causes/cause acid rain?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the "oxide of hydrogen" is water; it does not acidify rain.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> NOx forms nitric acid (HNO<sub>3</sub>).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> SO<sub>2</sub> forms sulphuric acid (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>), the major cause.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Normal rain has pH about 5.6 (dissolved CO<sub>2</sub>); acid rain is below 5.6. Effects: lake acidification, forest damage, corrosion of monuments ("stone cancer" at the Taj Mahal, from Mathura refinery emissions).</li>
<li>India: SO<sub>2</sub> controls (FGD) were relaxed in July 2025 for most coal plants away from cities; NCAP targets particulate reduction in 130 cities.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-41", year: 2010, no: 41, subject: "economy", topic: "UNCTAD emerging economies",
  q: `Consider the following countries:
<ol><li>Brazil</li><li>Mexico</li><li>South Africa</li></ol>
According to UNCTAD, which of the above is/are categorized as "Emerging Economies"?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Brazil, a BRICS member, is a major emerging economy.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Mexico, a large middle-income economy (and an OECD member), is classed as emerging.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> South Africa, also BRICS, is classed as emerging.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>UNCTAD (1964, Geneva) publishes the World Investment Report and Trade and Development Report; it is now called <b>UN Trade and Development</b> (renamed 2024).</li>
<li>BRICS expanded in 2024 (Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, UAE) and Indonesia joined in 2025; India chaired BRICS in 2026 (New Delhi summit, September).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-42", year: 2010, no: 42, subject: "ir", topic: "Bank for International Settlements",
  q: `Which one of the following is not related to United Nations?`,
  opts: ["Multilateral Investment Guarantee Agency", "International Finance Corporation", "International Centre for Settlement of Investment Disputes", "Bank for International Settlements"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Bank for International Settlements</h3>
<p>The BIS (1930, Basel) is owned by central banks, including RBI (member since 1996), and is not part of the UN system. It hosts the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> MIGA is part of the World Bank Group, a UN specialised agency.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> IFC (private-sector lending) is part of the World Bank Group.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> ICSID (investment arbitration) is part of the World Bank Group; India is not a member.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>World Bank Group: IBRD, IDA, IFC, MIGA, ICSID.</li>
<li>BIS Innovation Hub runs Project Nexus (linking fast-payment systems, including UPI) and Project mBridge. Basel III endgame norms are being phased in.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-43", year: 2010, no: 43, subject: "economy", topic: "Meltdown, recession, slowdown",
  q: `In the context of Indian economy, consider the following pairs:
<table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Most appropriate description</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Melt down</td><td>Fall in stock prices</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Recession</td><td>Fall in growth rate</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Slow down</td><td>Fall in GDP</td></tr></table>
Which of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (correct):</b> a meltdown is a steep, sudden fall in stock (asset) prices, as in 2008.</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (wrong):</b> recession is a fall in <b>GDP itself</b> (commonly two consecutive quarters of negative growth), not just slower growth.</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (wrong):</b> slowdown is a fall in the <b>growth rate</b> while GDP still grows; the descriptions of 2 and 3 are swapped.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India had a technical recession in 2020–21 (Covid; GDP fell about 6% under the old series). Depression is a prolonged, deep recession.</li>
<li>Stagflation = slow growth plus high inflation; the 2026 oil shock raised such fears globally.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-44", year: 2010, no: 44, subject: "economy", topic: "BRIC comparisons",
  q: `With reference to BRIC countries, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>At present, China's GDP is more than the combined GDP of all the three other countries.</li><li>China's population is more than the combined population of any two other countries.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> in 2009–10 China's GDP (about $5–6 trillion) exceeded Brazil, Russia and India together (about $4.5–5 trillion).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> India (about 1.18 billion) plus Brazil (about 0.19 billion) exceeded China (about 1.34 billion).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Today India's population exceeds China's (since 2023). China's GDP (about $19 trillion) is still larger than all the other original BRIC members combined.</li>
<li>"BRIC" was coined by Jim O'Neill (Goldman Sachs, 2001); South Africa joined in 2010, making BRICS. New Development Bank (Shanghai, 2015).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-45", year: 2010, no: 45, subject: "economy", topic: "SDR as artificial currency",
  q: `Which of the following is/are treated as artificial currency?`,
  opts: ["ADR", "GDR", "SDR", "Both ADR and SDR"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) SDR</h3>
<p><b>Special Drawing Rights</b> (IMF, 1969) are an international reserve asset ("paper gold") valued on a basket of five currencies: US dollar, euro, Chinese renminbi (added 2016), yen and pound.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> ADRs are certificates issued by a US bank representing shares of a foreign company traded in the US.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> GDRs are similar certificates traded in markets other than the US (London, Luxembourg).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong because ADR is not a currency.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The largest SDR allocation was $650 bn in August 2021 (India got about $17.9 bn). SDRs form part of India's forex reserves (record about $741 bn in August 2026).</li>
<li>SDR interest rate tracks short-term rates of basket currencies.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-46", year: 2010, no: 46, subject: "ir", topic: "Stiglitz Commission",
  q: `Stiglitz Commission established by the President of the United Nations General Assembly was in the international news. The commission was supposed to deal with`,
  opts: ["The challenges posed by the impending global climate change and prepare a road map", "The workings of the global financial systems and to explore ways and means to secure a more sustainable global order", "Global terrorism and prepare a global action plan for the mitigation of terrorism", "Expansion of the United Nations Security Council in the present global scenario"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) workings of the global financial system</h3>
<p>The <b>Commission of Experts on Reforms of the International Monetary and Financial System</b> (2009), chaired by Joseph Stiglitz, studied the global financial crisis and proposed reforms such as a new global reserve system and a Global Economic Coordination Council.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Climate roadmaps come through the UNFCCC and IPCC (the Stern Review was on climate economics).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Counter-terrorism is handled by UN CTC and the Global Counter-Terrorism Strategy (2006).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> UNSC expansion is negotiated through Intergovernmental Negotiations (IGN).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Another Stiglitz-led body, the Stiglitz–Sen–Fitoussi Commission (2009, France), worked on measuring progress beyond GDP.</li>
<li>Reform of the global financial architecture: the UN's Fourth Conference on Financing for Development (Seville, July 2025) adopted the "Compromiso de Sevilla".</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-47", year: 2010, no: 47, subject: "geography", topic: "Mineral sources",
  q: `With reference to the mineral resources of India, consider the following pairs:
<table><tr><th>Mineral</th><th>90% Natural sources in</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Copper</td><td>Jharkhand</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Nickel</td><td>Orissa</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Tungsten</td><td>Kerala</td></tr></table>
Which of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (wrong):</b> copper reserves are spread over Rajasthan (Khetri, largest), Madhya Pradesh (Malanjkhand, largest producer) and Jharkhand (Singhbhum); Jharkhand does not have 90%.</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (correct):</b> about 90%+ of nickel resources are in Odisha (Sukinda chromite belt).</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (wrong):</b> tungsten is mainly in Rajasthan (Degana), Karnataka and Haryana, not Kerala.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Copper, nickel and tungsten are <b>critical minerals</b>. The National Critical Mineral Mission (2025, ₹16,300 crore) and auction tranches under the MMDR Amendment 2023 aim to raise output.</li>
<li>India imports most of its copper concentrate; Hindustan Copper is the only vertically integrated producer. KABIL acquires overseas lithium and cobalt assets (Argentina, Australia).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-48", year: 2010, no: 48, subject: "ir", topic: "Codex Alimentarius",
  q: `As regards the use of international food safety standards as reference point for the dispute settlements, which one of the following does WTO collaborate with?`,
  opts: ["Codex Alimentarius Commission", "International Federation of Standards Users", "International Organization for Standardization", "World Standards Cooperation"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Codex Alimentarius Commission</h3>
<p>The WTO's <b>SPS Agreement</b> names Codex standards as the reference for food safety; measures based on them are presumed consistent with WTO rules.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> IFAN is a body of standards users, not a food-safety reference for the WTO.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> ISO sets technical and management standards (relevant to the TBT Agreement), not food safety under SPS.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> World Standards Cooperation is a coordination forum of ISO, IEC and ITU.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Codex (1963) is a joint FAO–WHO body. The SPS "three sisters": Codex (food), WOAH/OIE (animal health), IPPC (plant health).</li>
<li>FSSAI represents India; India has led Codex standards for spices (black pepper, cumin, cardamom) through the Committee on Spices and Culinary Herbs, which it chairs.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-49", year: 2010, no: 49, subject: "economy", topic: "National Food Security Mission",
  q: `An objective of the National Food Security Mission is to increase the production of certain crops through area expansion and productivity enhancement in a sustainable manner in the identified districts of the country. What are those crops?`,
  opts: ["Rice and wheat only", "Rice, wheat and pulses only", "Rice, wheat, pulses and oil seeds only", "Rice, wheat, pulses, oil seeds and vegetables"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Rice, wheat and pulses only</h3>
<p>NFSM was launched in 2007–08 (11th Plan) to raise production by 10 MT rice, 8 MT wheat and 2 MT pulses by 2011–12.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> It also covered pulses.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Oilseeds had separate programmes (ISOPOM, later NMOOP, now the <b>National Mission on Edible Oils</b>).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Vegetables come under the Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Later NFSM added coarse cereals, nutri-cereals and commercial crops (cotton, jute, sugarcane).</li>
<li><b>Latest:</b> the <b>Mission for Aatmanirbharta in Pulses</b> (2025–26 to 2030–31, ₹11,440 crore), launched 11 October 2025, aims for 350 lakh tonnes of pulses on 310 lakh ha by 2030–31. National Mission on Edible Oils covers oilseeds and oil palm.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-50", year: 2010, no: 50, subject: "economy", topic: "Savings account interest rate",
  q: `In India, the interest rate on savings accounts in all the nationalized commercial banks is fixed by`,
  opts: ["Union Ministry of Finance", "Union Finance Commission", "Indian Banks' Association", "None of the above"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) None of the above</h3>
<p>In 2010 the savings bank rate (3.5%) was fixed by the <b>Reserve Bank of India</b>, which is not among the options. RBI deregulated it in <b>October 2011</b>; since then each bank sets its own rate.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Finance Ministry does not fix bank deposit rates (it sets small-savings rates quarterly).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Finance Commission deals with Centre–State tax sharing.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The IBA is an industry body; it negotiates wages but does not fix rates.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Interest rate deregulation: lending rates (1994 onwards), term deposits (1997), savings (2011). Lending now uses external benchmarks (repo-linked, since 2019).</li>
<li>Small savings rates (PPF 7.1%, SSY 8.2%) are set by the government based on the Shyamala Gopinath formula.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-51", year: 2010, no: 51, subject: "modern", topic: "Simon Commission recommendations",
  q: `With reference to Simon Commission's recommendations, which one of the following statements is correct?`,
  opts: ["It recommended the replacement of diarchy with responsible government in the provinces", "It proposed the setting up of inter-provincial council under the Home Department", "It suggested the abolition of bicameral legislature at the Centre", "It recommended the creation of Indian Police Service with a provision for increased pay and allowances for British recruits as compared to Indian recruits"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) replacement of diarchy with responsible government in the provinces</h3>
<p>The Simon Report (1930) recommended abolishing provincial dyarchy and giving <b>provincial autonomy</b> with ministers responsible to legislatures, which the 1935 Act implemented.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> It did not propose an inter-provincial council under the Home Department.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> It did not suggest abolishing the bicameral central legislature; it proposed a federal arrangement in the long run.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> It did not propose an IPS with discriminatory pay.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Commission appointed 1927 with no Indian member ("Simon, go back", 1928). Lala Lajpat Rai died after a lathi charge in Lahore.</li>
<li>Responses: Nehru Report (1928), Jinnah's Fourteen Points (1929), Round Table Conferences (1930–32).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-52", year: 2010, no: 52, subject: "modern", topic: "Calcutta Congress 1906 resolutions",
  q: `Four resolutions were passed at the famous Calcutta session of Indian National Congress in 1906. The question of either retention OR of rejection of these four resolutions became the cause of a split in Congress at the next Congress session held in Surat in 1907. Which one of the following was not one of those resolutions?`,
  opts: ["Annulment of partition of Bengal", "Boycott", "National education", "Swadeshi"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Annulment of partition of Bengal</h3>
<p>Under Dadabhai Naoroji's presidency, Calcutta 1906 passed four resolutions: <b>Swaraj</b> (self-government), <b>Swadeshi</b>, <b>Boycott</b> and <b>National Education</b>. Moderates wanted to drop them at Surat; Extremists wanted them kept, causing the split.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Boycott of foreign goods was one of the four.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> National education was one of the four.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Swadeshi was one of the four.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Naoroji first used "Swaraj" as the goal at Calcutta. Surat (1907) president: Rash Behari Ghosh. Partition of Bengal was annulled in 1911 (Delhi Durbar).</li>
<li>Reunion at Lucknow (1916), along with the Lucknow Pact.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-54", year: 2010, no: 54, subject: "modern", topic: "Rajagopalachari's 'The Way Out'",
  q: `After Quit India Movement, C. Rajagopalachari issued a pamphlet entitled "The Way Out". Which one of the following was a proposal in this pamphlet?`,
  opts: ["The establishment of a \"War Advisory Council\" composed of representatives of British India and the Indian States", "Reconstitution of the Central Executive Council in such a way that all its members, except the Governor General and the Commander-in-Chief should be Indian leaders", "Fresh elections to the Central and Provincial Legislatures to be held at the end of 1945 and the Constitution making body to be convened as soon as possible", "A solution for the constitutional deadlock"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) A solution for the constitutional deadlock</h3>
<p>Rajaji's <i>The Way Out</i> (1944) offered a plan to break the Congress–League deadlock, leading to the <b>C.R. Formula</b>: the League would back independence, and after the war a plebiscite in Muslim-majority areas would decide on separation.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> A War Advisory Council was part of the August Offer (1940).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> An all-Indian Executive Council except the Viceroy and Commander-in-Chief was the <b>Wavell Plan</b> (Simla Conference, 1945).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Elections at the end of 1945 and a constitution-making body were announced by the government after the war (Wavell, September 1945).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Gandhi–Jinnah talks (September 1944) on the C.R. Formula failed.</li>
<li>Rajaji left Congress in 1942 over the Quit India resolution; he later became the first Indian Governor-General (1948) and founded the Swatantra Party (1959).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-55", year: 2010, no: 55, subject: "culture", topic: "Gupta paintings: Bagh",
  q: `There are only two known examples of cave paintings of the Gupta period in ancient India. One of these is paintings of Ajanta caves. Where is the other surviving example of Gupta paintings?`,
  opts: ["Bagh caves", "Ellora caves", "Lomas Rishi cave", "Nasik caves"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Bagh caves</h3>
<p>The Buddhist caves at <b>Bagh</b> (Dhar district, Madhya Pradesh, about 5th–6th century CE) have mural paintings similar in style to Ajanta.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Ellora (Rashtrakuta, 6th–10th century) is famous for rock-cut temples (Kailasa); its paintings are later.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Lomas Rishi (Barabar Hills, Bihar) is a Mauryan cave known for its carved entrance, with no Gupta paintings.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Nasik caves (Satavahana) are Buddhist viharas with inscriptions, not Gupta paintings.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ajanta (2nd century BCE–6th century CE; World Heritage 1983) uses the fresco-secco technique. Other painting sites: Sittanavasal (Jain, Pandya), Badami.</li>
<li>Bagh painting is also the name of a living hand-block print tradition from the same district (GI tag).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-56", year: 2010, no: 56, subject: "environment", topic: "UNFCCC origin",
  q: `The United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) is an international treaty drawn at`,
  opts: ["United Nations Conference on the Human Environment, Stockholm, 1972", "UN Conference on Environment and Development, Rio de Janeiro, 1992", "World Summit on Sustainable Development, Johannesburg, 2002", "UN Climate Change Conference, Copenhagen, 2009"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Rio de Janeiro, 1992</h3>
<p>The UNFCCC was opened for signature at the <b>Earth Summit</b> (UNCED, Rio, 1992) and entered into force in 1994; 198 parties.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Stockholm (1972) created UNEP; Indira Gandhi spoke there.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Johannesburg (2002, Rio+10) reviewed Agenda 21.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Copenhagen (COP15, 2009) produced the non-binding Copenhagen Accord.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Rio also produced the CBD, UNCCD (opened 1994), Agenda 21 and the Rio Declaration (common but differentiated responsibilities).</li>
<li>COP30 was held at Belém, Brazil (November 2025). COP31 (November 2026) is in Antalya, Türkiye, with Australia leading the negotiations.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-57", year: 2010, no: 57, subject: "science", topic: "Oil-eating bacteria",
  q: `Which bacterial strain, developed from natural isolates by genetic manipulations, can be used for treating oil spills?`,
  opts: ["Agrobacterium", "Clostridium", "Nitrosomonas", "Pseudomonas"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Pseudomonas</h3>
<p>Ananda Mohan Chakrabarty engineered a <i>Pseudomonas putida</i> "superbug" carrying several plasmids to degrade hydrocarbons. It was the subject of <b>Diamond v. Chakrabarty</b> (1980), the first US patent on a living organism.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Agrobacterium is used to transfer genes into plants (Ti plasmid).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Clostridium includes anaerobes causing tetanus and botulism, and some used in fermentation.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Nitrosomonas oxidises ammonia to nitrite (nitrification).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>TERI's <b>Oilzapper</b> (2011 Q) uses a consortium of hydrocarbon-eating bacteria. Bioremediation is used after spills such as the 2025 MSC Elsa 3 sinking off Kerala.</li>
<li>Patentability of life forms in India: micro-organisms can be patented (Section 3(j) excludes plants and animals).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-58", year: 2010, no: 58, subject: "science", topic: "Blue-green algae as biofertiliser",
  q: `Which feature of some species of blue-green algae helps promote them as bio-fertilizers?`,
  opts: ["They convert atmospheric methane into ammonia which the crop plants can absorb readily", "They induce the crop plants to produce the enzymes which help convert atmospheric nitrogen to nitrates", "They have the mechanism to convert atmospheric nitrogen into a form that the crop plants can absorb readily", "They induce the roots of the crop plants to absorb the soil nitrates in larger quantities"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) they fix atmospheric nitrogen</h3>
<p>Cyanobacteria such as <i>Anabaena</i> and <i>Nostoc</i> have <b>heterocysts</b> containing the enzyme nitrogenase, which converts N<sub>2</sub> to ammonia usable by plants. They are used in paddy fields; <i>Anabaena azollae</i> lives in the fern Azolla.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Methane is not converted to ammonia; nitrogen fixation uses N<sub>2</sub>.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Crop plants cannot fix nitrogen themselves; the microbes carry the enzyme.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> They add nitrogen rather than increasing nitrate uptake.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other biofertilisers: Rhizobium (legumes), Azotobacter and Azospirillum (free-living), phosphate-solubilising bacteria, mycorrhiza.</li>
<li>Government pushes: PM-PRANAM (cut chemical fertiliser use), National Mission on Natural Farming (2024), nano urea and nano DAP.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-59", year: 2010, no: 59, subject: "environment", topic: "Ramsar wise use",
  q: `India is a party to the Ramsar Convention and has declared many areas as Ramsar Sites. Which of the following statements best describes as to how we should maintain these sites in the context of this Convention?`,
  opts: ["Keep all the sites completely inaccessible to man so that they will not be exploited", "Conserve all the sites through ecosystem approach and permit tourism and recreation only", "Conserve all the sites through ecosystem approach for a period without any exploitation, with specific criteria and specific period for each site, and then allow sustainable use of them by future generations", "Conserve all the sites through ecosystem approach and allow their simultaneous sustainable use"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) conservation with simultaneous sustainable use</h3>
<p>The Ramsar Convention's core principle is <b>"wise use"</b>: maintaining ecological character through an ecosystem approach while allowing sustainable use by people.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Ramsar does not require keeping sites off-limits to people.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Use is not limited to tourism and recreation; fishing and other livelihoods are allowed sustainably.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> There is no requirement for a no-use period followed by use.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ramsar Convention (Iran, 1971); India joined in 1982 (Chilika and Keoladeo first). India now has <b>100 Ramsar sites</b> (Surha Tal, June 2026), the most in Asia; Tamil Nadu has the most.</li>
<li>Montreux Record lists threatened sites (Keoladeo and Loktak in India). COP15 was at Victoria Falls, Zimbabwe (July 2025). India's Wetlands (Conservation and Management) Rules 2017; Amrit Dharohar scheme.</li>
</ul>` }

);
;
