// UPSC CSE Prelims 2010, GS Paper I, Series A (GS questions only), part 3.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2010-60", year: 2010, no: 60, subject: "environment", topic: "Pongamia for biodiesel",
  q: `Other than Jatropha curcas, why is Pongamia pinnata also considered a good option for the production of bio-diesel in India?
<ol><li>Pongamia pinnata grows naturally in most of the arid regions of India.</li><li>The seeds of Pongamia pinnata are rich in lipid content of which nearly half is oleic acid.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2 (as per key)</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct, per key):</b> Pongamia (karanj) is a hardy, drought-tolerant, nitrogen-fixing legume tree that grows across most of India, including dry and degraded land.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> its seeds hold about 30–40% oil, about half of it oleic acid, which makes good biodiesel.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Debated: Pongamia grows best in humid tropical areas and along rivers; "naturally in most arid regions" was questioned, and (b) was argued. The key gives (c).</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The 2003 National Mission on Biodiesel focused on Jatropha, but plantations largely failed due to low yields. The <b>National Policy on Biofuels 2018</b> (amended 2022) targets 5% biodiesel blending by 2030.</li>
<li>India achieved 20% ethanol blending in petrol (E20) in 2025; the Global Biofuels Alliance was launched at the G20 summit (2023).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-61", year: 2010, no: 61, subject: "environment", topic: "Rice and greenhouse gases",
  q: `Due to their extensive rice cultivation, some regions may be contributing to global warming. To what possible reason/reasons is this attributable?
<ol><li>The anaerobic conditions associated with rice cultivation cause the emission of methane.</li><li>When nitrogen based fertilizers are used, nitrous oxide is emitted from the cultivated soil.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> flooded paddies create oxygen-free soil where methanogenic archaea produce methane. Rice gives about 10% of global methane from human activity.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> nitrification and denitrification of nitrogen fertilisers release nitrous oxide, a GHG about 270 times stronger than CO<sub>2</sub> over 100 years.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mitigation: alternate wetting and drying, direct-seeded rice, System of Rice Intensification, neem-coated urea.</li>
<li>India did not join the Global Methane Pledge (COP26), citing farm livelihoods. Agriculture accounts for about 13–14% of India's GHG emissions.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-62", year: 2010, no: 62, subject: "environment", topic: "Sustainable development and carrying capacity",
  q: `Sustainable development is described as the development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. In this perspective, inherently the concept of sustainable development is intertwined with which of the following concepts?`,
  opts: ["Social justice and empowerment", "Inclusive Growth", "Globalization", "Carrying capacity"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Carrying capacity</h3>
<p>Meeting future needs means not exceeding the <b>carrying capacity</b> of ecosystems: the maximum population or resource use the environment can sustain indefinitely.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Social justice is part of the social pillar, but not inherent to the intergenerational definition.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Inclusive growth is about sharing growth within the present generation.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Globalization is unrelated to the definition, and can even strain resources.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The definition comes from the <b>Brundtland Report</b>, "Our Common Future" (1987). The SDGs (17 goals, 2015–2030) build on it.</li>
<li>Related ideas: ecological footprint, planetary boundaries (7 of 9 crossed by 2025), Earth Overshoot Day, India's <b>Mission LiFE</b>.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-63", year: 2010, no: 63, subject: "environment", topic: "Energy crops for ethanol",
  q: `Given below are the names of four energy crops. Which one of them can be cultivated for ethanol?`,
  opts: ["Jatropha", "Maize", "Pongamia", "Sunflower"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Maize</h3>
<p>Maize starch is converted to sugar and fermented to <b>ethanol</b>; the US makes most of its ethanol from corn.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Jatropha seeds give oil for <b>biodiesel</b>, not ethanol.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Pongamia is also an oilseed for biodiesel.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Sunflower is an edible oilseed; it can make biodiesel but is not an ethanol crop.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's ethanol feedstocks: sugarcane juice, B-heavy and C-heavy molasses, damaged grains, FCI rice and maize. Maize became the largest feedstock in 2024–25, turning India into a maize importer and raising feed prices.</li>
<li>E20 was reached in 2025; the roadmap now looks at E27/E30 and flex-fuel vehicles. 2G ethanol plant at Panipat (IOCL) uses rice straw.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-64", year: 2010, no: 64, subject: "environment", topic: "Protected areas and species",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<table><tr><th>Protected area</th><th>Well-known for</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Bhitarkanika, Orissa</td><td>Salt Water Crocodile</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Desert National Park, Rajasthan</td><td>Great Indian Bustard</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Eravikulam, Kerala</td><td>Hoolak Gibbon</td></tr></table>
Which of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (correct):</b> Bhitarkanika has India's largest saltwater crocodile population (1,858 in the January 2026 census).</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (correct):</b> Desert National Park (Jaisalmer–Barmer) is the last stronghold of the Great Indian Bustard.</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (wrong):</b> Eravikulam is famous for the <b>Nilgiri tahr</b> (841 in 2025, the largest population). The Hoolock gibbon is found in the North-East (Hollongapar Gibbon Sanctuary, Assam).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>GIB: about 173 birds (2025), of which about 45 are in captive breeding at Sam and Ramdevra (Jaisalmer). Chicks have been hatched by artificial insemination since October 2024; the first releases to the wild are planned for 2026.</li>
<li>Eravikulam marked 50 years in 2025; Neelakurinji blooms there every 12 years (next due 2030).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-65", year: 2010, no: 65, subject: "science", topic: "Insectivorous plants",
  q: `Some species of plants are insectivorous. Why?`,
  opts: ["Their growth in shady and dark places does not allow them to undertake sufficient photosynthesis and thus they depend on insects for nutrition", "They are adapted to grow in nitrogen deficient soils and thus depend on insects for sufficient nitrogenous nutrition", "They cannot synthesize certain vitamins themselves and depend on the insects digested by them", "They have remained in that particular stage of evolution as living fossils, a link between autotrophs and heterotrophs"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) adapted to nitrogen-deficient soils</h3>
<p>Pitcher plants (<i>Nepenthes</i>), sundew (<i>Drosera</i>), bladderwort (<i>Utricularia</i>) and Venus flytrap grow in bogs and acidic soils poor in nitrogen. They photosynthesise normally but digest insects for nitrogen and phosphorus.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> They are green and photosynthesise; most grow in open, sunny bogs.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Vitamin deficiency is not the reason.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> They are not living fossils; carnivory evolved several times independently.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's pitcher plant <i>Nepenthes khasiana</i> (Meghalaya) is endemic and endangered (CITES Appendix I).</li>
<li>Other nutrition modes: parasitic (Cuscuta), saprophytic (Monotropa), symbiotic (lichens, mycorrhiza).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-67", year: 2010, no: 67, subject: "economy", topic: "Measures to control fiscal deficit",
  q: `In the context of governance, consider the following:
<ol><li>Encouraging Foreign Direct Investment inflows</li><li>Privatization of higher educational Institutions</li><li>Down-sizing of bureaucracy</li><li>Selling/offloading the shares of Public Sector Undertakings</li></ol>
Which of the above can be used as measures to control the fiscal deficit in India?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 4", "3 and 4 only"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3 and 4 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> FDI flows to the private sector and the capital account; it does not directly reduce government borrowing.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> privatising higher education does not directly cut the deficit and was not seen as a fiscal measure.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> a smaller bureaucracy cuts salary and pension spending.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> disinvestment proceeds are non-debt capital receipts that reduce the fiscal deficit.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fiscal deficit = total expenditure − (revenue receipts + non-debt capital receipts). Budget 2026–27 target: 4.3% of GDP (4.4% in 2025–26).</li>
<li>Disinvestment receipts have often missed targets; the IDBI Bank stake sale to Fairfax (approved October 2026, about ₹52,878 crore including LIC's share) is the biggest pending deal.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-68", year: 2010, no: 68, subject: "geography", topic: "Urbanisation growth by region",
  q: `As per the UN-Habitat's Global Report on Human Settlements 2009, which one among the following regions has shown the fastest growth rate of urbanization in the last three decades?`,
  opts: ["Asia", "Europe", "Latin America and Caribbean", "North America"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Asia</h3>
<p>Asia's urban population grew fastest from the 1980s (China and India); Latin America was already largely urbanised, and Europe and North America urbanised much earlier.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Europe is about 75% urban, with slow growth.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Latin America urbanised earlier (now about 80% urban), so its recent growth rate is lower.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> North America is highly urbanised with slow growth.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>UN World Urbanization Prospects 2025 uses a new harmonised "degree of urbanisation" method: about 45% of people live in cities, Jakarta is the largest city, and Dhaka is projected to become the largest by 2050.</li>
<li>India's urban share was 31% (Census 2011) and is estimated at about 36–37% now; Census 2027 will update it.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-69", year: 2010, no: 69, subject: "geography", topic: "Forest types by area",
  q: `In India, which type of forest among the following occupies the largest area?`,
  opts: ["Montane Wet Temperate Forest", "Sub-tropical Dry Evergreen Forest", "Tropical Moist Deciduous Forest", "Tropical Wet Evergreen Forest"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Tropical Moist Deciduous Forest</h3>
<p>Among the options, tropical moist deciduous forests (monsoon forests: sal and teak) cover the largest area. Taken together, tropical dry and moist deciduous forests cover over half of India's forest area.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Montane wet temperate forests are limited to the Nilgiris, Himalaya and North-East hills.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Sub-tropical dry evergreen forests are limited to the Shiwaliks and parts of Punjab and J&amp;K.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Tropical wet evergreen forests are confined to the Western Ghats, North-East and Andaman and Nicobar Islands.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Champion and Seth (1968) classified 16 major forest type groups. ISFR 2021 found tropical dry deciduous forests the most extensive overall, followed by tropical moist deciduous.</li>
<li>ISFR 2023: forest and tree cover 25.17%; Madhya Pradesh has the largest forest cover.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-70", year: 2010, no: 70, subject: "economy", topic: "Inclusive growth in 11th Plan",
  q: `Inclusive growth as enunciated in the Eleventh Five Year Plan does not include one of the following:`,
  opts: ["Reduction of poverty", "Extension of employment opportunities", "Strengthening of capital market", "Reduction of gender inequality"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Strengthening of capital market</h3>
<p>The 11th Plan (2007–12), "Towards Faster and More Inclusive Growth", aimed at poverty reduction, employment, education, health, and reducing regional and gender disparities. Capital-market strengthening is a financial-sector goal, not part of inclusion.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Poverty reduction was a core inclusive-growth goal.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Employment generation (58 million jobs targeted) was central.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Gender equity was explicitly targeted.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Planning Commission was replaced by <b>NITI Aayog</b> (2015); the 12th Plan (2012–17) was the last. The long-term vision is <b>Viksit Bharat @2047</b>.</li>
<li>Multidimensional poverty fell from about 29% (2013–14) to about 11% (2022–23) per NITI Aayog's estimates.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-72", year: 2010, no: 72, subject: "geography", topic: "Tamil Nadu cotton yarn",
  q: `Tamil Nadu is a leading producer of mill-made cotton yarn in the country. What could be the reason?
<ol><li>Black cotton soil is the predominant type of soil in the State.</li><li>Rich pool of skilled labour is available.</li></ol>
Which of the above is/are the correct reasons?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Tamil Nadu's soils are mostly <b>red</b> soils; black soil is predominant in Maharashtra, Gujarat and Madhya Pradesh. Tamil Nadu actually buys much of its raw cotton from other States.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> skilled labour, cheap hydro and wind power, entrepreneurship (Coimbatore, Tiruppur) and market access explain its spinning lead.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Coimbatore is the "Manchester of South India"; Tiruppur is the knitwear hub (about half of India's knitwear exports).</li>
<li>PM MITRA textile parks (seven, including Virudhunagar in Tamil Nadu). US tariffs of 50% (August 2025) hit Tiruppur's exports; the India–UK CETA (in force July 2026) and India–EU FTA give new duty-free access.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-73", year: 2010, no: 73, subject: "geography", topic: "Fresh water distribution",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>On the planet Earth, the fresh water available for use amounts to about less than 1% of the total water found.</li><li>Of the total fresh water found on the planet Earth 95% is bound up in polar ice caps and glaciers.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0, provKey: "D",
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> about 97.5% of Earth's water is salty and 2.5% is fresh; fresh water that is accessible (lakes, rivers, shallow groundwater) is well under 1% of the total.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> about <b>69%</b> of fresh water is in ice caps and glaciers, about 30% is groundwater, and about 1% is surface and atmospheric water. 95% is too high.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The scanned key shows (d). By standard USGS/UNESCO figures statement 1 holds, so (a) is given.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India has about 18% of world population but only about 4% of its fresh water; per capita availability is about 1,400 m³ (water-stressed is below 1,700 m³).</li>
<li>2025 was the UN International Year of Glaciers' Preservation; the UN 2026 Water Conference, co-hosted by the UAE and Senegal, is scheduled for December 2026.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-74", year: 2010, no: 74, subject: "geography", topic: "Albedo",
  q: `Which one of the following reflects back more sunlight as compared to other three?`,
  opts: ["Sand desert", "Paddy crop land", "Land covered with fresh snow", "Prairie land"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Land covered with fresh snow</h3>
<p>Fresh snow has the highest <b>albedo</b> (reflectivity), about 80–90%.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Sand deserts reflect about 30–40%.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Paddy fields (water and green crops) reflect only about 10–20%.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Grassland (prairie) reflects about 20–25%.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Earth's average albedo is about 0.30. Melting ice lowers albedo and speeds warming (<b>ice–albedo feedback</b>); Arctic sea ice hit record lows in 2025.</li>
<li>Black carbon (soot) on Himalayan glaciers darkens snow and speeds melting.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-75", year: 2010, no: 75, subject: "geography", topic: "Rivers of Himachal Pradesh",
  q: `Rivers that pass through Himachal Pradesh are`,
  opts: ["Beas and Chenab only", "Beas and Ravi only", "Chenab, Ravi and Satluj only", "Beas, Chenab, Ravi, Satluj and Yamuna"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Beas, Chenab, Ravi, Satluj and Yamuna</h3>
<p>All five flow through Himachal: the <b>Chenab</b> (Chandra + Bhaga at Tandi, Lahaul), <b>Ravi</b> (Chamba), <b>Beas</b> (Rohtang to Kangra), <b>Satluj</b> (enters at Shipki La from Tibet; Bhakra dam), and the <b>Yamuna</b> (along the Uttarakhand border, with its tributaries Giri and Tons).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> leaves out Ravi, Satluj and Yamuna.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> leaves out Chenab, Satluj and Yamuna.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> leaves out Beas and Yamuna.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dams: Bhakra (Govind Sagar, Satluj), Pong (Maharana Pratap Sagar, Beas, a Ramsar site), Nathpa Jhakri (Satluj), Chamera (Ravi).</li>
<li>Himachal suffered severe monsoon cloudbursts and floods in 2023 and 2025 (Mandi, Kullu).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-76", year: 2010, no: 76, subject: "polity", topic: "Finance Commission report",
  q: `Who of the following shall cause every recommendation made by the Finance Commission to be laid before each House of Parliament?`,
  opts: ["The President of India", "The Speaker of Lok Sabha", "The Prime Minister of India", "The Union Finance Minister"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) The President of India</h3>
<p><b>Art 281</b>: the President causes every FC recommendation, with an explanatory memorandum on the action taken, to be laid before each House.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> The Speaker presides over the House but does not lay FC reports.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The PM has no constitutional role here.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> In practice the Finance Minister tables it, but on the President's behalf; the constitutional duty is the President's.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other reports the President causes to be laid: CAG (Art 151), UPSC (Art 323), NCSC (Art 338), NCST (Art 338A), Special Officer for Linguistic Minorities (Art 350B).</li>
<li>16th FC report was laid on 1 February 2026: 41% share for States; GDP contribution added as a criterion.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-77", year: 2010, no: 77, subject: "polity", topic: "Budget preparation",
  q: `Which one of the following is responsible for the preparation and presentation of Union Budget to the Parliament?`,
  opts: ["Department of Revenue", "Department of Economic Affairs", "Department of Financial Services", "Department of Expenditure"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Department of Economic Affairs</h3>
<p>The <b>Budget Division</b> of DEA prepares the Union Budget (the Annual Financial Statement, Art 112) and presents it through the Finance Minister.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Department of Revenue handles taxes (CBDT, CBIC); it provides inputs.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Department of Financial Services oversees banks, insurance and pension.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Department of Expenditure handles expenditure management and Finance Commission matters.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Since 2017: Budget on 1 February, Railway Budget merged, plan/non-plan distinction ended. Budget printing begins with the "Halwa ceremony"; paperless Budget since 2021.</li>
<li>Six departments under the Finance Ministry: Economic Affairs, Expenditure, Revenue, DIPAM, Financial Services, Public Enterprises.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-79", year: 2010, no: 79, subject: "economy", topic: "Fiscal stimulus measures",
  q: `Consider the following actions by the Government:
<ol><li>Cutting the tax rates</li><li>Increasing the government spending</li><li>Abolishing the subsidies</li></ol>
In the context of economic recession, which of the above actions can be considered a part of the "fiscal stimulus" package?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> tax cuts leave more income with households and firms, raising demand.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> more government spending directly adds to aggregate demand.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> abolishing subsidies reduces disposable income and demand; it is fiscal consolidation, not stimulus.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's 2008–09 stimulus: excise and service tax cuts plus higher spending. 2020: Atmanirbhar packages.</li>
<li>Keynesian multiplier: spending multiplier is usually larger than the tax-cut multiplier. GST 2.0 rate cuts (September 2025) were described as a consumption stimulus.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-81", year: 2010, no: 81, subject: "economy", topic: "CRR increase",
  q: `When the Reserve Bank of India announces an increase of the Cash Reserve Ratio, what does it mean?`,
  opts: ["The commercial banks will have less money to lend", "The Reserve Bank of India will have less money to lend", "The Union Government will have less money to lend", "The commercial banks will have more money to lend"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) The commercial banks will have less money to lend</h3>
<p>CRR is the share of deposits banks must keep as cash with the RBI, earning no interest. A higher CRR locks up more funds, reducing lendable resources and liquidity.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> RBI does not lend from CRR balances; it creates money.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The government is not a lender in this context.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> This happens when CRR is <b>cut</b>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>CRR is <b>3%</b> of NDTL (cut in stages from 4% in September–November 2025, releasing about ₹2.5 lakh crore). Legal basis: Section 42 of the RBI Act.</li>
<li>Repo 5.50% (raised 7 October 2026 amid oil-driven inflation).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-82", year: 2010, no: 82, subject: "modern", topic: "Covenanted Civil Service",
  q: `Who among the following Governor Generals created the Covenanted Civil Service of India which later came to be known as the Indian Civil Service?`,
  opts: ["Warren Hastings", "Wellesley", "Cornwallis", "William Bentinck"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Cornwallis</h3>
<p>Cornwallis (1786–93), the "Father of Civil Services in India", reorganised the Company's servants into a well-paid, rule-bound Covenanted Civil Service, banning private trade and gifts. Higher posts were reserved for Europeans.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Warren Hastings laid revenue and judicial foundations but did not create the covenanted service.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Wellesley set up <b>Fort William College</b> (1800) to train civil servants.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Bentinck's Charter Act of 1833 opened posts to Indians in principle.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Open competition from 1853 (Charter Act) and 1855 (Macaulay Committee). Satyendranath Tagore was the first Indian ICS (1863). Aitchison Commission (1886) created Imperial, Provincial and Subordinate services. Lee Commission (1924) led to the Public Service Commission (1926).</li>
<li>National Civil Services Day: 21 April (Patel's 1947 address).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-83", year: 2010, no: 83, subject: "modern", topic: "Swadeshi movement cause",
  q: `What was the immediate cause for the launch of the Swadeshi movement?`,
  opts: ["The partition of Bengal done by Lord Curzon", "A sentence of 18 months rigorous imprisonment imposed on Lokmanya Tilak", "The arrest and deportation of Lala Lajpat Rai and Ajit Singh; and passing of the Punjab Colonization Bill", "Death sentence pronounced on the Chapekar brothers"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) The partition of Bengal by Lord Curzon</h3>
<p>Partition was announced in July 1905; the boycott resolution was passed at Calcutta Town Hall on <b>7 August 1905</b> (now National Handloom Day), and partition took effect on 16 October 1905.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Tilak's 18-month sentence came in 1897; in 1908 he got six years (Mandalay). Both are unrelated to the movement's launch.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Lajpat Rai and Ajit Singh were deported in 1907 over the Punjab colonisation agitation, after Swadeshi began.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Chapekar brothers were hanged (1898–99) for the Rand assassination in Poona.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Methods: boycott, swadeshi enterprises, national education, samitis. Leaders: Aurobindo, Bipin Chandra Pal, Surendranath Banerjee; Tagore wrote <i>Amar Sonar Bangla</i>.</li>
<li>2025 marked 120 years of the movement.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-84", year: 2010, no: 84, subject: "modern", topic: "Champaran Satyagraha",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Dr. Rajendra Prasad persuaded Mahatma Gandhi to come to Champaran to investigate the problem of peasants.</li><li>Acharya J. B. Kriplani was one of Mahatma Gandhi's colleagues in his Champaran investigation.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> it was <b>Raj Kumar Shukla</b>, a local indigo cultivator, who persuaded Gandhi at the Lucknow Congress (1916) to visit Champaran.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> J.B. Kripalani joined Gandhi, along with Rajendra Prasad, Brajkishore Prasad, Mazharul Haq, Mahadev Desai and Narhari Parikh.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Issue: the <b>tinkathia</b> system (planting indigo on 3/20 of land). The Champaran Agrarian Act (1918) abolished it.</li>
<li>Gandhi's first civil disobedience in India (1917); its centenary was marked in 2017.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-85", year: 2010, no: 85, subject: "modern", topic: "Collector stripped of judicial powers (1793)",
  q: `By a regulation in 1793, the District Collector was deprived of his judicial powers and made the collecting agent only. What was the reason for such regulation?`,
  opts: ["Lord Cornwallis felt that the District Collector's efficiency of revenue collection would enormously increase without the burden of other work", "Lord Cornwallis felt that Judicial power should compulsorily be in the hands of Europeans while Indians can be given the job of revenue collection in the districts", "Lord Cornwallis was alarmed at the extent of power concentrated in the District Collector and felt that such absolute power was undesirable in one person", "The judicial work demanded a deep knowledge of India and a good training in law and Lord Cornwallis felt that District Collector should be only a revenue collector"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) concentration of absolute power in one person was undesirable</h3>
<p>Influenced by the separation of powers, Cornwallis believed a Collector who both assessed revenue and judged disputes over it could abuse power against landholders. The <b>Cornwallis Code</b> (1793) created a separate District Judge.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Efficiency was not the stated motive; the aim was a check on power to protect property rights.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Collectors were also Europeans; the change was not about race.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Legal expertise was not the reasoning given.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under Munro (Madras, 1820s) magisterial powers returned to Collectors, because separation proved slow and costly. In 1831 Bentinck re-combined collector and magistrate in Bengal.</li>
<li>Today Art 50 (DPSP) asks for separation of the judiciary from the executive; CrPC 1973 (now BNSS 2023) separated judicial magistrates from executive magistrates.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-86", year: 2010, no: 86, subject: "economy", topic: "WPI vs CPI-IW",
  q: `With reference to India, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Wholesale Price Index (WPI) in India is available on a monthly basis only.</li><li>As compared to Consumer Price Index for Industrial Workers (CPI(IW)), the WPI gives less weight to food articles.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2 (as per key)</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct, per key):</b> from October 2009 the headline (all-commodities) WPI moved from weekly to <b>monthly</b> release.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> food articles had about 14–15% weight in WPI (2004–05 base) against about 46% in CPI-IW.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Debated: in 2010 weekly WPI was still released for primary articles and fuel (until 2012), so statement 1 ("monthly basis only") was argued to be wrong, giving (b). The key gives (c).</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Latest:</b> a new <b>WPI series with base 2022–23</b> (957 items, up from 697) was launched on 15 June 2026 along with Producer Price Indices; WPI inflation was 9.68% in May 2026 due to oil. CPI moved to base 2024 in 2026. CPI-IW (Labour Bureau) has base 2016.</li>
<li>The RBI targets headline CPI (4% ± 2%), not WPI.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-88", year: 2010, no: 88, subject: "science", topic: "Car radiator principle",
  q: `What is the principle by which a cooling system (Radiator) in a motor car works?`,
  opts: ["Conduction only", "Convection", "Radiation only", "Both conduction and radiation"], ans: 1, provKey: "D",
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Convection</h3>
<p>Coolant circulates through the engine block and radiator (forced convection by the pump), and air blown through the radiator fins by the fan and vehicle motion carries heat away (convection). Heat passes through the metal walls by conduction; radiation plays a minor role despite the name.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Conduction alone cannot move heat from engine to air; fluid movement is essential.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Radiation is a small fraction of heat loss at radiator temperatures (about 90°C).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Leaves out convection, the dominant process.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The scanned key shows (d). Heat-transfer texts describe radiators as convective heat exchangers (with conduction through the walls), so (b) is given; this was a widely debated item.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thermosiphon cooling in old engines relied on natural convection. Electric vehicles use liquid cooling loops for batteries (thermal management).</li>
<li>Sea breeze, land breeze and monsoon winds are natural convection currents.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-89", year: 2010, no: 89, subject: "science", topic: "GSM family technologies",
  q: `Which among the following do/does not belong/belongs to the GSM family of wireless technologies?`,
  opts: ["EDGE", "LTE", "DSL", "Both EDGE and LTE"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) DSL</h3>
<p><b>DSL</b> (Digital Subscriber Line) is a wired broadband technology over copper telephone lines, not a wireless mobile standard.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> EDGE (2.75G) is an enhancement of GSM/GPRS.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> LTE (4G) evolved from the GSM/UMTS (3GPP) family.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong, since both EDGE and LTE belong to the family.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>GSM path: GSM (2G) → GPRS → EDGE → UMTS/HSPA (3G) → LTE (4G) → 5G NR. CDMA was the rival family.</li>
<li>India launched 5G in October 2022 and has one of the fastest rollouts; BSNL's indigenous 4G stack (TCS–C-DOT–Tejas) went live in September 2025. Bharat 6G Alliance targets 2030.</li>
</ul>` }

);
;
