window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2010-90", year: 2010, no: 90, subject: "science", topic: "CyberKnife",
  q: `With reference to the treatment of cancerous tumours, a tool called cyberknife has been making the news. In this context, which one of the following statements is not correct?`,
  opts: ["It is a robotic image guided system", "It delivers an extremely precise dose of radiation", "It has the capability of achieving sub-millimetre accuracy", "It can map the spread of tumour in the body"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) (not correct)</h3>
<p>CyberKnife is a <b>treatment</b> device, a robotic stereotactic radiosurgery system. Mapping the spread of a tumour is done by imaging (PET-CT, MRI), not by CyberKnife.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Correct: a linear accelerator mounted on a robotic arm, guided by real-time X-ray imaging that tracks tumour movement (even breathing).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Correct: it delivers high-dose, precisely targeted radiation, sparing healthy tissue.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Correct: sub-millimetre targeting accuracy.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Related: Gamma Knife (brain tumours), proton beam therapy (Tata Memorial Centre, Navi Mumbai, since 2023), CAR-T cell therapy (India's NexCAR19, 2023).</li>
<li>Budget 2025–26 set up day-care cancer centres in all district hospitals within three years and exempted customs duty on 36 life-saving drugs.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-91", year: 2010, no: 91, subject: "geography", topic: "Red soil colour",
  q: `When you travel in certain parts of India, you will notice red soil. What is the main reason for this colour?`,
  opts: ["Abundance of magnesium", "Accumulated humus", "Presence of ferric oxides", "Abundance of phosphates"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Presence of ferric oxides</h3>
<p>Red soils develop from crystalline igneous and metamorphic rocks (granite, gneiss). Iron diffused through them oxidises to ferric oxide, giving a red colour (yellow when hydrated).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Magnesium does not give red colour; black soils are rich in magnesium and lime.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Humus makes soil dark brown or black; red soils are poor in humus.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Red soils are actually <b>deficient</b> in phosphate, nitrogen and humus.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Found in Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Odisha, Chhattisgarh and Jharkhand; second most widespread soil after alluvium. Suited to millets, pulses and groundnut with fertiliser.</li>
<li>Laterite soils (leaching in high rainfall) are rich in iron and aluminium oxides, used for bricks and cashew.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-92", year: 2010, no: 92, subject: "geography", topic: "Gondwana rocks and coal",
  q: `Which one of the following is the appropriate reason for considering the Gondwana rocks as most important of rock systems of India?`,
  opts: ["More than 90% of limestone reserves of India are found in them", "More than 90% of India's coal reserves are found in them", "More than 90% of fertile black cotton soils are spread over them", "None of the reasons given above is appropriate in this context"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) More than 90% of India's coal reserves</h3>
<p>Gondwana sediments (Carboniferous to Jurassic, about 300–150 million years old) in the Damodar, Mahanadi, Son, Godavari and Wardha valleys hold about 98% of India's coal.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Limestone is mainly in Vindhyan and Cuddapah rocks.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Black soils come from the <b>Deccan Traps</b> (basalt), not Gondwana rocks.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong because (b) is right.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tertiary coal (lignite) is found in the North-East, Neyveli (Tamil Nadu) and Rajasthan.</li>
<li>India produced over 1 billion tonnes of coal in 2024–25 (Coal India about 780 Mt). Odisha has the largest reserves; Jharkhand has the most coking coal.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-93", year: 2010, no: 93, subject: "geography", topic: "Strait of Malacca",
  q: `Which one of the following can one come across if one travels through the Strait of Malacca?`,
  opts: ["Bali", "Brunei", "Java", "Singapore"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Singapore</h3>
<p>The Strait of Malacca runs between the Malay Peninsula and Sumatra; Singapore lies at its southern end, where it meets the Singapore Strait.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Bali is east of Java, near the Lombok Strait.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Brunei is on Borneo, facing the South China Sea.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Java is south of Sumatra, across the Sunda Strait.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>About a quarter of global trade and much of China's oil imports pass through Malacca (the "Malacca dilemma"). Its narrowest point is the Phillips Channel (about 2.8 km).</li>
<li>India's Andaman and Nicobar Command sits near the western entrance (Six Degree Channel). The Great Nicobar transhipment port is planned at Galathea Bay.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-94", year: 2010, no: 94, subject: "geography", topic: "Luni river",
  q: `With reference to the river Luni, which one of the following statements is correct?`,
  opts: ["It flows into Gulf of Khambhat", "It flows into Gulf of Kuchchh", "It flows into Pakistan and merges with a tributary of Indus", "It is lost in the marshy land of the Rann of Kuchchh"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) It is lost in the marshes of the Rann of Kachchh</h3>
<p>The Luni rises in the Aravallis near Ajmer (Pushkar valley), flows south-west through western Rajasthan, and ends in the Rann of Kachchh without reaching the sea.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The Sabarmati and Mahi flow into the Gulf of Khambhat.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> It does not reach the Gulf of Kachchh.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> It does not enter Pakistan or join the Indus system.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Its water is sweet in the upper course but salty below Balotra ("Lavanavari"). Tributaries: Sukri, Jawai, Bandi (left); Jojari (right).</li>
<li>Rivers of inland drainage: Luni, Ghaggar (lost in the Thar). Textile effluents from Balotra and Pali pollute the Luni (NGT orders).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-95", year: 2010, no: 95, subject: "geography", topic: "Dams/lakes and rivers",
  q: `Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?
<table><tr><th>Dam/Lake</th><th>River</th></tr>
<tr><td>(a) Govind Sagar</td><td>Satluj</td></tr>
<tr><td>(b) Kolleru Lake</td><td>Krishna</td></tr>
<tr><td>(c) Ukai Reservoir</td><td>Tapi</td></tr>
<tr><td>(d) Wular Lake</td><td>Jhelum</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["Govind Sagar – Satluj", "Kolleru Lake – Krishna", "Ukai Reservoir – Tapi", "Wular Lake – Jhelum"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Kolleru Lake – Krishna (incorrect)</h3>
<p>Kolleru is a freshwater lake between the <b>Godavari and Krishna deltas</b>, fed by seasonal streams (Budameru, Tammileru) and Godavari canals; it is not formed by the Krishna.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Correct: Govind Sagar is the reservoir of the Bhakra dam on the Satluj.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Correct: the Ukai dam is on the Tapi in Gujarat.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Correct: the Jhelum flows through Wular, one of Asia's largest freshwater lakes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Kolleru (Ramsar 2002) was damaged by aquaculture; Operation Kolleru (2006) demolished fish tanks, and encroachment returned. Wular (Ramsar 1990) hosts the stalled Tulbul navigation project; India moved to revive it after holding the Indus Waters Treaty in abeyance (April 2025).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-96", year: 2010, no: 96, subject: "geography", topic: "Mediterranean climate",
  q: `A geographic region has the following distinct characteristics:
<ol><li>Warm and dry climate</li><li>Mild and wet winter</li><li>Evergreen oak trees</li></ol>
The above features are the distinct characteristics of which one of the following regions?`,
  opts: ["Mediterranean", "Eastern China", "Central Asia", "Atlantic coast of North America"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Mediterranean</h3>
<p>Mediterranean climate (30–45° on western sides of continents): dry, warm summers under subtropical highs and mild, rainy winters from westerlies. Vegetation is sclerophyllous evergreen (cork oak, olive).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Eastern China has a monsoonal climate with summer rain.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Central Asia is continental: cold winters, dry, with grassland and desert.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Atlantic coast of North America has rain all year (humid subtropical or continental).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other Mediterranean regions: California, central Chile, Cape Town, south-west Australia. Known for citrus, grapes and wine ("orchard of the world").</li>
<li>Mediterranean regions face more wildfires; 2025 saw record burnt area in the EU (Spain, Portugal, Greece).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-97", year: 2010, no: 97, subject: "polity", topic: "National R&R Policy 2007",
  q: `With reference to the National Rehabilitation and Resettlement Policy, 2007, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>This policy is applicable only to the persons affected by the acquisition of land for projects and not to the involuntary displacement due to any other reason.</li><li>This policy has been formulated by the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the policy also covered involuntary displacement for other reasons (e.g. permanent displacement due to natural calamities), not only land acquisition.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> it was framed by the <b>Ministry of Rural Development</b> (Department of Land Resources).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>It was replaced by the <b>Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act, 2013</b>: social impact assessment, consent of 80% (private) or 70% (PPP) of affected families, compensation up to four times market value in rural areas.</li>
<li>States have diluted SIA through their own amendments (Gujarat, Maharashtra, Telangana).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-98", year: 2010, no: 98, subject: "economy", topic: "Shift in Five Year Plan focus",
  q: `In the context of India's Five Year Plans, a shift in the pattern of industrialization, with lower emphasis on heavy industries and more on infrastructure begins in`,
  opts: ["Fourth Plan", "Sixth Plan", "Eighth Plan", "Tenth Plan"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Eighth Plan (1992–97)</h3>
<p>After the 1991 reforms, the Eighth Plan (indicative planning) left heavy industry to the private sector and focused public investment on <b>infrastructure</b> (energy, transport, communication) and human development.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Fourth Plan (1969–74): growth with stability and self-reliance; still public-sector led.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Sixth Plan (1980–85): poverty removal, some liberalisation, but heavy industry focus remained.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Tenth Plan (2002–07): 8% growth target and governance; the shift had already happened.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Second Plan (1956–61, Mahalanobis model) began the heavy-industry focus. Plan holidays: 1966–69 and 1990–92.</li>
<li>Today: National Infrastructure Pipeline, PM Gati Shakti (2021); capex of about ₹11–12 lakh crore a year in recent Budgets.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-99", year: 2010, no: 99, subject: "economy", topic: "Swadhar and Swayam Siddha",
  q: `Two of the schemes launched by the Government of India for Women's development are Swadhar and Swayam Siddha. As regards the difference between them, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Swayam Siddha is meant for those in difficult circumstances such as women survivors of natural disasters or terrorism, women prisoners released from jails, mentally challenged women etc. whereas Swadhar is meant for holistic empowerment of women through Self Help Groups.</li><li>Swayam Siddha is implemented through Local Self Government bodies or reputed Voluntary Organizations whereas Swadhar is implemented through the ICDS units set up in the States.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the descriptions are swapped. <b>Swadhar</b> (2001–02) is for women in difficult circumstances (shelter, food, counselling); <b>Swayam Siddha</b> (2001) was for holistic empowerment through SHGs.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> again swapped: Swayam Siddha worked through ICDS blocks, while Swadhar is run through NGOs and voluntary organisations.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Swadhar Greh is now part of <b>Mission Shakti</b> (2022): "Sambal" (One Stop Centres, Beti Bachao Beti Padhao, Nari Adalat) and "Samarthya" (Shakti Sadan, PMMVY, creches).</li>
<li>Women's SHGs today: DAY-NRLM (about 10 crore women), Lakhpati Didi target 6 crore.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-100", year: 2010, no: 100, subject: "polity", topic: "UN Convention on the Rights of the Child",
  q: `With reference to the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child, consider the following:
<ol><li>The Right to Development</li><li>The Right to Expression</li><li>The Right to Recreation</li></ol>
Which of the above is/are the Rights of the child?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Article 6 guarantees survival and development; development is one of the four core rights (survival, development, protection, participation).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Article 13 gives freedom of expression; Article 12 the right to be heard.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> Article 31 gives the right to rest, leisure, play and recreation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>UNCRC adopted 20 November 1989 (World Children's Day); India ratified it in 1992. It is the most widely ratified human rights treaty; the US has not ratified.</li>
<li>Indian laws: RTE Act 2009, POCSO 2012, Juvenile Justice Act 2015, NCPCR. The DPDP Rules 2025 require verifiable parental consent for processing data of children under 18.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-102", year: 2010, no: 102, subject: "polity", topic: "District Consumer Forum",
  q: `With reference to the Consumer Disputes Redressal at district level in India, which one of the following statements is not correct?`,
  opts: ["A State Government can establish more than one District Forum in a district if it deems fit", "One of the members of the District Forum shall be a woman", "The District Forum entertains the complaints where the value of goods or services does not exceed rupees fifty lakhs", "A complaint in relation to any goods sold or any service provided may be filed with a District Forum by the State Government as a representative of the interests of the consumers in general"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (not correct, in 2010)</h3>
<p>Under the Consumer Protection Act 1986 (as amended in 2002), the District Forum's limit was <b>₹20 lakh</b>, not ₹50 lakh.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Correct: a State may set up more than one District Forum in a district.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Correct: one member must be a woman.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Correct: the Central or State Government can file complaints on behalf of consumers in general.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Consumer Protection Act 2019</b> renamed them District Commissions. Pecuniary limits since December 2021: District up to ₹50 lakh, State ₹50 lakh–₹2 crore, National above ₹2 crore (so (c) would be true today). Complaints can be filed online (e-Daakhil). The CCPA was set up in 2020.</li>
<li>CCPA guidelines target dark patterns (2023) and misleading advertisements.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-103", year: 2010, no: 103, subject: "environment", topic: "King cobra nest",
  q: `King Cobra is the only snake that makes its own nest. Why does it make its nest?`,
  opts: ["It is a snake-eater and the nest helps attract other snakes", "It is a viviparous snake and needs a nest to give birth to its offspring", "It is an oviparous snake and lays its eggs in the nest and guards the nest until they are hatched", "It is a large, cold blooded animal and needs a nest to hibernate in the cold season"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) oviparous; lays eggs in the nest and guards it</h3>
<p>The female king cobra gathers leaf litter into a mound, lays 20–40 eggs, and guards the nest until shortly before hatching. Rotting leaves provide warmth for incubation.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> It does eat snakes (ophiophagus), but the nest is not a lure.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> It lays eggs; it is not viviparous.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The nest is for eggs, not hibernation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>A 2024 study split the king cobra into four species; the Western Ghats species is <i>Ophiophagus kaalinga</i>. IUCN: Vulnerable; WPA Schedule II (2022 Act).</li>
<li>Agumbe Rainforest Research Station (Karnataka) studies king cobras. India's snakebite deaths (about 58,000 a year) led to a National Action Plan (2024) and making snakebite notifiable in several States.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-104", year: 2010, no: 104, subject: "environment", topic: "Greendex",
  q: `As a result of their annual survey, the National Geographic Society and an international polling firm GlobeScan gave India top rank in Greendex 2009 score. What is this score?`,
  opts: ["It is a measure of efforts made by different countries in adopting technologies for reducing carbon footprint", "It is a measure of environmentally sustainable consumer behaviour in different countries", "It is an assessment of programmes/schemes undertaken by different countries for improving the conservation of natural resources", "It is an index showing the volume of carbon credits sold by different countries"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) environmentally sustainable consumer behaviour</h3>
<p>Greendex surveyed consumers on housing, transport, food and goods. India and China ranked high because of lower consumption, smaller homes and more public transport.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> It measured household behaviour, not national technology adoption.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> It did not assess government programmes.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> It had nothing to do with carbon credit volumes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Greendex ran from 2008 to 2014; India topped most editions.</li>
<li>Other indices: Climate Change Performance Index (India in the top 10 in recent years), Environmental Performance Index (India near the bottom). India's <b>Mission LiFE</b> (2022) promotes sustainable lifestyles.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-105", year: 2010, no: 105, subject: "environment", topic: "Taxus tree and taxol",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Taxus tree naturally found in the Himalayas.</li><li>The Taxus tree is listed in the Red Data Book.</li><li>A drug called "taxol" is obtained from Taxus tree is effective against Parkinson's disease.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1, provKey: "D",
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the Himalayan yew (<i>Taxus wallichiana</i>, thuner) grows in temperate Himalayan forests at about 1,800–3,300 m.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> it is listed as Endangered on the IUCN Red List because of over-harvesting of bark and leaves.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> taxol (paclitaxel) is an <b>anti-cancer</b> drug (breast, ovarian, lung cancer) that stops cell division; it is not used for Parkinson's disease.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The scanned key shows (d), but taxol is an anticancer drug, not a Parkinson's treatment, so (b) is given.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other plant drugs: vinblastine and vincristine (Madagascar periwinkle, cancer), artemisinin (malaria), quinine (cinchona), reserpine (Rauwolfia, blood pressure), L-dopa (Mucuna, used for Parkinson's).</li>
<li><i>Taxus</i> is on CITES Appendix II; India bans its export.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-107", year: 2010, no: 107, subject: "geography", topic: "Coriolis effect",
  q: `What causes wind to deflect toward left in the Southern hemisphere?`,
  opts: ["Temperature", "Magnetic field", "Rotation of the earth", "Pressure"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Rotation of the earth</h3>
<p>The <b>Coriolis force</b>, from Earth's rotation, deflects moving air to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern hemisphere (Ferrel's law). It is zero at the equator and greatest at the poles.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Temperature differences create pressure gradients but not deflection.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> The magnetic field does not affect neutral air.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The pressure gradient starts the wind; deflection comes from rotation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Coriolis makes cyclones rotate anticlockwise in the north and clockwise in the south; cyclones do not form within about 5° of the equator.</li>
<li>Geostrophic wind: balance of pressure gradient and Coriolis force, flowing parallel to isobars.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-108", year: 2010, no: 108, subject: "science", topic: "Mercury in fluorescent lamps",
  q: `Indiscriminate disposal of used fluorescent electric lamps causes mercury pollution in the environment. Why is mercury used in the manufacture of these lamps?`,
  opts: ["A mercury coating on the inside of the lamp makes the light bright white", "When the lamp is switched on, the mercury in the lamp causes the emission of ultra-violet radiations", "When the lamp is switched on, it is the mercury which converts the ultra-violet energy into visible light", "None of the statement given above is correct about the use of mercury in the manufacture of fluorescent lamps"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) mercury vapour emits UV radiation when the lamp is on</h3>
<p>Electric discharge excites mercury vapour, which emits ultraviolet light (mainly 254 nm). The <b>phosphor coating</b> on the glass then converts UV into visible light.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The inner coating is phosphor, not mercury.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> UV-to-visible conversion is done by the phosphor, not mercury.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong because (b) is correct.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>A CFL holds about 3–5 mg of mercury; broken lamps release toxic vapour. The <b>Minamata Convention</b> phased out most mercury-containing CFLs by 2025 (COP5 decision).</li>
<li>LEDs need no mercury; India's E-Waste Management Rules 2022 cover lamps through extended producer responsibility.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-109", year: 2010, no: 109, subject: "geography", topic: "Role of the Himalayas",
  q: `If there were no Himalayan ranges, what would have been the most likely geographical impact on India?
<ol><li>Much of the country would experience the cold waves from Siberia.</li><li>Indo-gangetic plain would be devoid of such extensive alluvial soils.</li><li>The pattern of monsoon would be different from what it is at present.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the Himalayas block cold, dry Siberian air masses in winter, keeping north India warmer than places at the same latitude.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> Himalayan rivers brought the sediment that built the Indo-Gangetic alluvial plain.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> they trap monsoon winds, force orographic rain, and influence the jet stream and the thermal low that drive the monsoon.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other roles: source of perennial rivers (glaciers), forest and mineral wealth, a defence barrier. The Tibetan Plateau's heating helps set up the tropical easterly jet.</li>
<li>Himalayan glaciers are losing mass faster (ICIMOD, 2023: up to 75% loss by 2100 under high warming). Glacial lake outburst floods: South Lhonak (Sikkim, 2023).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-110", year: 2010, no: 110, subject: "science", topic: "Bhuvan geoportal",
  q: `In the context of space technology, what is "Bhuvan", recently in the news?`,
  opts: ["A mini satellite launched by ISRO for promoting the distance education in India", "The name given to the next Moon Impact Probe, for Chandrayan-II", "A geoportal of ISRO with 3D imaging capabilities of India", "A space telescope developed by India"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) a geoportal of ISRO with 3D imaging of India</h3>
<p><b>Bhuvan</b> (2009, NRSC Hyderabad) is ISRO's web-based geoportal for viewing Indian satellite imagery and thematic maps, India's answer to Google Earth.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The distance-education satellite was <b>EDUSAT</b> (2004).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Chandrayaan-1 carried the Moon Impact Probe (2008); Chandrayaan-2 had no such probe.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> India's space telescope is <b>AstroSat</b> (2015).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bhuvan applications: Bhuvan Panchayat, crop and flood mapping, Yuktdhara (MGNREGA asset planning). Geospatial Guidelines 2021 and National Geospatial Policy 2022 liberalised mapping.</li>
<li>Indian navigation: NavIC. ISRO's Bharatiya Antariksh Station module (BAS-1) is planned for 2028.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-111", year: 2010, no: 111, subject: "geography", topic: "Latitude of Sikkim",
  q: `The latitudes that pass through Sikkim also pass through`,
  opts: ["Rajasthan", "Punjab", "Himachal Pradesh", "Jammu & Kashmir"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Rajasthan</h3>
<p>Sikkim lies between about 27°05′N and 28°08′N. Rajasthan extends from about 23°03′N to 30°12′N, so it covers Sikkim's latitudes.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Punjab lies between about 29°30′N and 32°30′N, north of Sikkim's range.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Himachal lies between about 30°22′N and 33°12′N.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Jammu &amp; Kashmir lies north of about 32°N.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The same latitudes also pass through Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Assam and Arunachal. The Tropic of Cancer (23.5°N) crosses eight States: Gujarat, Rajasthan, MP, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura and Mizoram.</li>
<li>Sikkim: Kanchenjunga National Park (World Heritage, 2016); first fully organic State (2016).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-119", year: 2010, no: 119, subject: "modern", topic: "Bombay Manifesto 1936",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The "Bombay Manifesto" signed in 1936 openly opposed the preaching of socialist ideals.</li><li>It evoked support from a large section of business community from all across India.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> in May 1936, 21 Bombay businessmen signed a manifesto attacking Jawaharlal Nehru's socialist speeches (Lucknow Congress, 1936) as a threat to private property.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> it drew criticism, and businessmen elsewhere (and leaders like G.D. Birla, who preferred quiet influence) did not openly back it; it did not get broad all-India business support.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Unverified: the Series A cell in the scanned key is illegible; this answer is from historical analysis (Bipan Chandra). Another unofficial key gave (c).</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nehru's socialist turn: Lucknow (1936) and Faizpur (1936, first rural session) presidencies. Congress Socialist Party (1934, Acharya Narendra Dev, JP).</li>
<li>Later, the <b>Bombay Plan</b> (1944) by industrialists (Tata, Birla and others) backed state planning and investment in heavy industry.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-120", year: 2010, no: 120, subject: "medieval", topic: "Bhakti proponents",
  q: `Among the following, who was not a proponent of bhakti cult?`,
  opts: ["Nagarjuna", "Tukaram", "Tyagaraja", "Vallabhacharya"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Nagarjuna</h3>
<p>Nagarjuna (about 2nd century CE) was a Buddhist philosopher who founded the <b>Madhyamaka</b> (Shunyavada) school of Mahayana. He was not part of the bhakti movement.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Tukaram (17th century), a Varkari saint of Maharashtra, wrote abhangas to Vithoba.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Tyagaraja (18th–19th century), the Carnatic composer, wrote devotional kritis to Rama.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Vallabhacharya (1479–1531) founded the Pushti Marg (Krishna bhakti, Shuddhadvaita).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nagarjunakonda (Andhra) is named after him; the site was submerged by the Nagarjuna Sagar dam, with monuments shifted to an island.</li>
<li>Bhakti began with the Alvars and Nayanars (Tamil, 6th–9th century).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-121", year: 2010, no: 121, subject: "modern", topic: "Karachi Resolution 1931",
  q: `For the Karachi session of Indian National Congress in 1931 presided over by Sardar Patel, who drafted the Resolution on Fundamental Rights and Economic Programme?`,
  opts: ["Mahatma Gandhi", "Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru", "Dr. Rajendra Prasad", "Dr. B. R. Ambedkar"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru</h3>
<p>Nehru drafted the Karachi resolution on Fundamental Rights and Economic Policy: civil liberties, equality, universal adult franchise, free primary education, a living wage, state ownership of key industries, and relief from rent and debt.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Gandhi supported it but did not draft it.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Rajendra Prasad did not draft it.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Ambedkar was not in the Congress; he attended the Round Table Conferences separately.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Karachi (March 1931) also endorsed the Gandhi–Irwin Pact, days after the execution of Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev.</li>
<li>The resolution prefigured the Fundamental Rights and DPSPs of the Constitution.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-122", year: 2010, no: 122, subject: "modern", topic: "Cripps Mission negotiators",
  q: `Who among the following were official Congress negotiators with Cripps Mission?`,
  opts: ["Mahatma Gandhi and Sardar Patel", "Acharya J. B. Kripalani and C. Rajagopalachari", "Pandit Nehru and Maulana Azad", "Dr. Rajendra Prasad and Rafi Ahmed Kidwai"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Pandit Nehru and Maulana Azad</h3>
<p>Maulana Azad (Congress president) and Jawaharlal Nehru were the Congress's official negotiators with Sir Stafford Cripps (March–April 1942).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Gandhi met Cripps but was not the official negotiator; he called the offer a "post-dated cheque".</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Rajaji favoured accepting the offer but was not the official negotiator.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Prasad and Kidwai had no such role.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cripps offer: dominion status after the war, an elected constitution-making body, provinces could opt out (a seed of partition). It failed over the defence portfolio and the Viceroy's veto.</li>
<li>Its failure led to the Quit India Movement (August 1942).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-123", year: 2010, no: 123, subject: "science", topic: "Digestion",
  q: `Which one of the following processes in the bodies of living organisms is a digestive process?`,
  opts: ["Breakdown of proteins into amino acids", "Breakdown of glucose into CO₂ and H₂O", "Conversion of glucose into glycogen", "Conversion of amino acids into proteins"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Breakdown of proteins into amino acids</h3>
<p>Digestion is the enzymatic hydrolysis of large food molecules into absorbable units: proteins → amino acids (pepsin, trypsin), carbohydrates → simple sugars, fats → fatty acids and glycerol.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Breakdown of glucose to CO<sub>2</sub> and water is <b>cellular respiration</b>.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Glucose to glycogen is <b>glycogenesis</b> (storage in liver and muscle).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Amino acids to proteins is <b>protein synthesis</b> (anabolism).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Enzymes: salivary amylase (mouth), pepsin (stomach, acidic), trypsin, lipase and amylase (pancreas), bile emulsifies fats.</li>
<li>Most absorption occurs in the small intestine (villi).</li>
</ul>` }

);
;
