window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2010-124", year: 2010, no: 124, subject: "science", topic: "Sequence of evolution",
  q: `From the point of view of evolution of living organisms, which one of the following is the correct sequence of evolution?`,
  opts: ["Otter – Tortoise – Shark", "Shark – Tortoise – Otter", "Tortoise – Shark – Otter", "Shark – Otter – Tortoise"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Shark – Tortoise – Otter</h3>
<p>Fish (shark, cartilaginous) appeared first, then reptiles (tortoise), then mammals (otter). The order follows vertebrate evolution: fish → amphibians → reptiles → birds and mammals.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Reverse order: a mammal cannot come before a fish.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Reptiles did not come before fish.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> A mammal (otter) did not evolve before reptiles (tortoise).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sharks date back about 450 million years, turtles about 220 million years, and otters (mustelids) about 25 million years.</li>
<li>Otters in India: smooth-coated, small-clawed and Eurasian. Smooth-coated and small-clawed otters were moved to CITES Appendix I (2019).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-125", year: 2010, no: 125, subject: "science", topic: "Hepatitis B",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Hepatitis B is several times more infectious than HIV/AIDS</li><li>Hepatitis B can cause liver cancer</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> HBV is about 50–100 times more infectious than HIV; it survives outside the body for a week and spreads through blood, sex and mother-to-child.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> chronic infection causes cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma (liver cancer).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>A safe vaccine exists (birth dose plus three doses under India's Universal Immunisation Programme). Hepatitis C has no vaccine but is curable with antivirals.</li>
<li>India's <b>National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme</b> (2018) aims to eliminate hepatitis C and cut hepatitis B as public-health threats by 2030 (SDG 3.3). World Hepatitis Day is 28 July (Baruch Blumberg's birthday).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-126", year: 2010, no: 126, subject: "science", topic: "Carbon monoxide poisoning",
  q: `Excessive release of the pollutant carbon monoxide (CO) into the air may produce a condition in which oxygen supply in the human body decreases. What causes this condition?`,
  opts: ["When inhaled into the human body, CO is converted into CO₂", "The inhaled CO has much higher affinity for haemoglobin as compared to oxygen", "The inhaled CO destroys the chemical structure of haemoglobin", "The inhaled CO adversely affects the respiratory centre in the brain"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) CO binds haemoglobin far more strongly than oxygen</h3>
<p>CO's affinity for haemoglobin is about 200–250 times that of O<sub>2</sub>, forming <b>carboxyhaemoglobin</b>, which cannot carry oxygen and also stops the remaining haemoglobin from releasing O<sub>2</sub> to tissues.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> CO is not converted to CO<sub>2</sub> in the body in any meaningful amount.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> It does not destroy haemoglobin; binding is reversible with oxygen therapy.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Brain effects result from hypoxia, not direct action on the respiratory centre.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sources: incomplete combustion (vehicles, angithis or braziers in closed rooms, generators). Winter deaths from coal heaters in closed rooms recur in north India.</li>
<li>Treatment: 100% oxygen or hyperbaric oxygen. CO is one of the pollutants in India's National Ambient Air Quality Standards.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-127", year: 2010, no: 127, subject: "science", topic: "Swine flu",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Every individual in the population is equally susceptible host for Swine Flu.</li><li>Antibiotics have no role in the primary treatment of Swine Flu.</li><li>To prevent the future spread of Swine Flu in the epidemic area, the swine (pigs) must all be culled.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> susceptibility varies; pregnant women, young children, the elderly and people with chronic illness are at higher risk, and prior exposure gives some immunity.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> it is a viral infection (H1N1 influenza A), treated with antivirals (oseltamivir); antibiotics are used only for secondary bacterial infections.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> the 2009 pandemic spread from person to person, not from pigs, so culling pigs was unnecessary.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The 2009 H1N1 pandemic was declared by WHO in June 2009; H1N1 is now a seasonal flu strain.</li>
<li>Other zoonotic flu: H5N1 bird flu, which spread to US dairy cattle (2024–25) and causes recurring outbreaks in Indian poultry. The <b>WHO Pandemic Agreement</b> was adopted in May 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-128", year: 2010, no: 128, subject: "science", topic: "HIV transmission",
  q: `With regard to the transmission of the Human Immunodeficiency Virus, which one of the following statements is not correct?`,
  opts: ["The chances of transmission from female to male are twice as likely as from male to female", "The chances of transmission are more if a person suffers from other sexually transmitted infections", "An infected mother can transmit the infection to her baby during pregnancy, at childbirth and by breast feeding", "The risk of contracting infection from transfusion of infected blood is much higher than an exposure to contaminated needle"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) (not correct)</h3>
<p>It is the reverse: male-to-female transmission is about twice as likely as female-to-male, because of a larger mucosal surface and higher viral concentration in semen.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Correct: other STIs cause ulcers and inflammation that raise the risk.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Correct: vertical transmission can occur in pregnancy, delivery or breastfeeding (prevented by antiretroviral therapy).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Correct: transfusion of infected blood carries a risk above 90%, against about 0.3% for a needle-stick.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India has about 25 lakh people living with HIV (second largest globally), adult prevalence about 0.2%; new infections have fallen sharply since 2010 (NACO). HIV/AIDS (Prevention and Control) Act 2017 bans discrimination.</li>
<li>Lenacapavir, a twice-yearly injectable PrEP, was approved by the US FDA (June 2025) and recommended by WHO.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-129", year: 2010, no: 129, subject: "environment", topic: "India's limitations in mitigating warming",
  q: `What are the possible limitations of India in mitigating the global warming at present and in the immediate future?
<ol><li>Appropriate alternate technologies are not sufficiently available.</li><li>India cannot invest huge funds in research and development.</li><li>Many developed countries have already set up their polluting industries in India.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> in 2010, clean technologies (cheap solar, storage, carbon capture) were limited and costly.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> India's R&amp;D spending is low (about 0.65% of GDP), limiting home-grown solutions.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> it is not true that many developed countries had shifted polluting industries to India on a scale that limits mitigation; this is a generalisation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's updated NDC: 45% cut in emissions intensity (2005 base) by 2030 and 50% of power capacity from non-fossil sources by 2030 (reached in 2025). Net zero by 2070.</li>
<li>The ₹1 lakh crore Research Development and Innovation (RDI) scheme (2025) and the Anusandhan National Research Foundation aim to boost private R&amp;D.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-130", year: 2010, no: 130, subject: "ir", topic: "The Commonwealth",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Commonwealth has no charter, treaty or constitution.</li><li>All the territories/countries once under the British empire (jurisdiction/rule/mandate) automatically joined the Commonwealth as its members.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only (as of 2010)</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct in 2010):</b> the Commonwealth was a voluntary association based on declarations (Singapore 1971, Harare 1991), with no founding charter or treaty. A <b>Commonwealth Charter</b> was adopted in December 2012 and signed in 2013, so this is no longer true.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> membership is voluntary. Myanmar, Aden (Yemen), Sudan and others never joined; Ireland left in 1949. Mozambique (1995), Rwanda (2009), Gabon and Togo (2022) joined with no British colonial link.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>56 members, about 2.5 billion people; head: King Charles III (symbolic). Secretary-General: Shirley Botchwey of Ghana (since April 2025).</li>
<li>India hosted CWG 2010 and will host the 2030 centenary Games in Ahmedabad.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-132", year: 2010, no: 132, subject: "ir", topic: "European Commission vs European Council",
  q: `In the context of bilateral trade negotiations between India and European Union, what is the difference between European Commission and European Council?
<ol><li>European Commission represents the EU in trade negotiations whereas European Council participates in the legislation of matters pertaining to economic policies of the European Union.</li><li>European Commission comprises the Heads of State or government of member countries whereas the European Council comprises of the persons nominated by European Parliament.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the Commission does negotiate trade deals, but the <b>European Council</b> (heads of state or government) sets political direction and does <b>not legislate</b>. Legislation is done by the Council of the EU (ministers) and the European Parliament.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> reversed. The European Council comprises heads of state or government; the Commission has one commissioner per member state, proposed by governments and approved by Parliament.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Leaders (2026): Commission President Ursula von der Leyen; European Council President António Costa.</li>
<li><b>India–EU FTA</b> negotiations were concluded on 27 January 2026 (launched 2007, relaunched 2022), along with an investment protection agreement and a GI agreement under talks. The EU's CBAM (definitive phase from January 2026) affects Indian steel and aluminium exports.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-133", year: 2010, no: 133, subject: "geography", topic: "Land use in India",
  q: `The approximate representation of land use classification in India is`,
  opts: ["Net area sown 25%; forests 33%; other areas 42%", "Net area sown 58%; forests 17%; other areas 25%", "Net area sown 43%; forests 29%; other areas 28%", "Net area sown 47%; forests 23%; other areas 30%"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Net area sown 47%; forests 23%; other areas 30%</h3>
<p>Of India's reporting area (about 305 million ha), net sown area is about 140 million ha (about 46%) and area under forests about 22–23%.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Net sown area is not 25%, and recorded forest area is not 33% (33% is the National Forest Policy target).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> 58% sown and 17% forest overstates agriculture and understates forests.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Overstates forest area (29%).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Nine-fold land use classification (Land Use Statistics): forests, barren land, non-agricultural uses, pastures, tree crops, culturable waste, current and other fallows, net sown area.</li>
<li>Forest and tree cover (satellite) is 25.17% (ISFR 2023), different from recorded forest area. Net sown area is highest in Punjab and Haryana (over 80%).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-134", year: 2010, no: 134, subject: "economy", topic: "National Investment Fund",
  q: `With reference to the National Investment Fund to which the disinvestment proceeds are routed, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The assets in the National Investment Fund are managed by the Union Ministry of Finance.</li><li>The National Investment Fund is to be maintained within the Consolidated Fund of India.</li><li>Certain Asset Management Companies are appointed as the fund managers.</li><li>A certain proportion of annual income is used for financing select social sectors.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2", "2 only", "3 and 4", "3 only"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> the fund was professionally managed by public-sector AMCs, not directly by the Finance Ministry.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> NIF (2005) was kept <b>outside</b> the Consolidated Fund of India (in the Public Account).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> UTI AMC, SBI Funds and LIC Mutual Fund managed it.</li>
<li><b>4 (correct):</b> 75% of its annual income financed social-sector schemes (education, health, employment); 25% went to profitable CPSEs.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>From 2013 proceeds were used for capital spending; since 2015–16 disinvestment receipts go to the Consolidated Fund, and NIF is a book account.</li>
<li>Do not confuse NIF with <b>NIIF</b> (National Investment and Infrastructure Fund, 2015), a quasi-sovereign infrastructure fund.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-135", year: 2010, no: 135, subject: "economy", topic: "Forward Markets Commission",
  q: `In India, which of the following is regulated by the Forward Markets Commission?`,
  opts: ["Currency Futures Trading", "Commodities Futures Trading", "Equity Futures Trading", "Both Commodities Futures and Financial Futures Trading"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Commodities Futures Trading</h3>
<p>The FMC (1953, under the Forward Contracts (Regulation) Act 1952) regulated commodity futures (MCX, NCDEX).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Currency futures were regulated jointly by SEBI and RBI.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Equity futures are regulated by SEBI.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Financial futures were not under FMC.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>FMC was <b>merged with SEBI</b> in September 2015 after the NSEL scam (2013); SEBI now regulates commodity derivatives too.</li>
<li>Futures trading in seven farm commodities (wheat, paddy (non-basmati), chana, mustard, soybean, crude palm oil, moong) has been suspended since December 2021, with the suspension extended several times.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-136", year: 2010, no: 136, subject: "economy", topic: "Limited Liability Partnership",
  q: `Which one of the following is not a feature of Limited Liability Partnership firm?`,
  opts: ["Partners should be less than 20", "Partnership and management need not be separate", "Internal governance may be decided by mutual agreement among partners", "It is corporate body with perpetual succession"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Partners should be less than 20 (not a feature)</h3>
<p>The <b>LLP Act 2008</b> sets a minimum of two partners and <b>no maximum</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> A feature: partners can also manage the business.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> A feature: an LLP agreement governs internal affairs flexibly.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> A feature: an LLP is a body corporate with a separate legal entity and perpetual succession; partners' liability is limited.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>At least two designated partners, one of them resident in India. Registered with the Ministry of Corporate Affairs.</li>
<li>LLP (Amendment) Act 2021 decriminalised many offences and created "small LLPs". Jan Vishwas (Amendment of Provisions) Act 2023 and the Jan Vishwas 2.0 Bill (2025) continue decriminalisation.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-137", year: 2010, no: 137, subject: "economy", topic: "Banking Ombudsman",
  q: `With reference to the institution of Banking Ombudsman in India, which one of the statements is not correct?`,
  opts: ["The Banking Ombudsman is appointed by the Reserve Bank of India", "The Banking Ombudsman can consider complaints from Non-Resident Indians having accounts in India", "The orders passed by the Banking Ombudsman are final and binding on the parties concerned", "The service provided by the Banking Ombudsman is free of any fee"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (not correct)</h3>
<p>An aggrieved party could appeal against the Ombudsman's award to the <b>Appellate Authority</b> (a Deputy Governor of RBI), so orders were not final.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Correct: RBI appoints Ombudsmen (scheme since 1995, under Section 35A of the Banking Regulation Act).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Correct: NRIs with accounts in India can complain.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Correct: the service is free.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The <b>RBI Integrated Ombudsman Scheme</b> (November 2021) merged the banking, NBFC and digital payments ombudsmen ("One Nation, One Ombudsman"), with appeals to an Executive Director of RBI.</li>
<li>Complaints are filed at cms.rbi.org.in; compensation up to ₹20 lakh plus ₹1 lakh for mental agony.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-138", year: 2010, no: 138, subject: "economy", topic: "Financial inclusion steps",
  q: `With reference to India, consider the following:
<ol><li>Nationalization of Banks</li><li>Formation of Regional Rural Banks</li><li>Adoption of villages by Bank Branches</li></ol>
Which of the above can be considered as steps taken to achieve the "financial inclusion" in India?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> bank nationalisation (1969, 1980) expanded branches into rural areas and directed credit to priority sectors.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> RRBs (1975, Narasimham Working Group) took credit to small farmers and rural artisans.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> under the Lead Bank Scheme and service area approach, branches adopted villages to provide credit.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Later steps: no-frills accounts (2005), business correspondents (2006), <b>PMJDY</b> (2014: about 59 crore accounts and ₹3.17 lakh crore deposits by August 2026, 56% held by women), UPI, payments banks.</li>
<li>RBI's Financial Inclusion Index was 67 in March 2025. RRBs were consolidated to 28 (one per State) from May 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-139", year: 2010, no: 139, subject: "medieval", topic: "Third Battle of Panipat",
  q: `What was the immediate reason for Ahmad Shah Abdali to invade India and fight the Third Battle of Panipat?`,
  opts: ["He wanted to avenge the expulsion by Marathas of his viceroy Timur Shah from Lahore", "The frustrated governor of Jullundhar Adina Beg Khan invited him to invade Punjab", "He wanted to punish Mughal administration for non-payment of the revenues of the Chahar Mahal (Gujarat, Aurangabad, Sialkot and Pasrur)", "He wanted to annex all the fertile plains of Punjab up to the borders of Delhi to his kingdom"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) to avenge the Marathas' expulsion of his viceroy Timur Shah from Lahore</h3>
<p>In 1758 the Marathas under Raghunath Rao captured Lahore and drove out Timur Shah (Abdali's son). Abdali returned in 1759, leading to Panipat on <b>14 January 1761</b>, where the Marathas under Sadashivrao Bhau were defeated.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Adina Beg Khan had invited the <b>Marathas</b> into Punjab against the Afghans.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Chahar Mahal revenues (ceded in 1752) were an older grievance; the districts were Sialkot, Pasrur, Gujrat and Aurangabad (in Punjab), and this was not the immediate cause.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Annexing Punjab was a long-term aim, not the immediate trigger.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Abdali was supported by Najib-ud-Daula (Rohilla) and Shuja-ud-Daula (Awadh). The defeat halted Maratha expansion in the north and indirectly helped the British (Plassey 1757, Buxar 1764).</li>
<li>Earlier Panipat battles: 1526 (Babur v Ibrahim Lodi), 1556 (Akbar v Hemu).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-140", year: 2010, no: 140, subject: "modern", topic: "Pondicherry and Europeans",
  q: `With reference to Pondicherry (now Puducherry), consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The first European power to occupy Pondicherry were the Portuguese.</li><li>The second European power to occupy Pondicherry were the French.</li><li>The English never occupied Pondicherry.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0, provKey: "C",
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> the Portuguese set up a trading post at Pondicherry in the early 16th century.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> the Danes and the Dutch had trading interests there before the French settlement of 1674 (François Martin); the Dutch also captured it in 1693.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> the English captured Pondicherry several times (1761 after the siege, 1778, 1793) before returning it to France in 1816.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The scanned key shows (c), but the British clearly occupied Pondicherry (Eyre Coote, 1761), so statement 3 is false and (a) is given. Statement 2 is also debated.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>De facto transfer to India on 1 November 1954 (Kizhoor referendum); de jure transfer in 1962. Union Territory of Puducherry: Puducherry, Karaikal, Mahe and Yanam.</li>
<li>Other French settlements: Chandernagore (merged with West Bengal, 1954). Portuguese Goa was liberated in 1961.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-141", year: 2010, no: 141, subject: "ancient", topic: "Decline of Buddhism",
  q: `Why did Buddhism start declining in India in the early medieval times?
<ol><li>Buddha was by that time considered as one of the incarnations of Vishnu and thus became a part of Vaishnavism.</li><li>The invading tribes from Central Asia till the time of last Gupta king adopted Hinduism and persecuted Buddhists.</li><li>The Kings of Gupta dynasty were strongly opposed to Buddhism.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Puranas listed the Buddha among Vishnu's avatars, absorbing Buddhism into Vaishnavism and blurring its identity.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> the generalisation is false. Most Central Asian groups (Indo-Greeks, Kushanas) patronised Buddhism; the Huna Mihirakula persecuted Buddhists, but invaders as a whole did not.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> the Guptas were tolerant; Kumaragupta I founded <b>Nalanda</b>, and Buddhist art flourished (Sarnath school).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other causes: loss of royal patronage, Bhakti and Shankaracharya's revival, monastic wealth and corruption, Tantric changes (Vajrayana), and the Turkish destruction of Nalanda and Vikramshila (Bakhtiyar Khalji, about 1200).</li>
<li>Pali was granted classical language status in October 2024. The Piprahwa relics were brought back to India in 2025.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-142", year: 2010, no: 142, subject: "economy", topic: "Functions of commercial banks",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
The functions of commercial banks in India include
<ol><li>Purchase and sale of shares and securities on behalf of customers.</li><li>Acting as executors and trustees of wills.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2, provKey: "D",
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> buying and selling securities for customers is an <b>agency function</b> permitted under Section 6(1)(a) of the Banking Regulation Act; banks run demat and broking services.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> acting as executor, trustee or attorney is also listed in Section 6 and is offered through trustee subsidiaries (e.g. SBICAP Trustee).</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The scanned key shows (d), but Section 6 of the Banking Regulation Act 1949 expressly permits both activities, so (c) is given.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Primary functions: accepting deposits and lending. Agency functions: collecting cheques and dividends, paying bills, portfolio services. Utility functions: lockers, letters of credit, guarantees.</li>
<li>Banks may not trade in goods (Section 8).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-143", year: 2010, no: 143, subject: "economy", topic: "Tax shares in gross tax revenue",
  q: `In India, the tax proceeds of which one of the following as a percentage of gross tax revenue has significantly declined in the last five years?`,
  opts: ["Service tax", "Personal income tax", "Excise duty", "Corporation tax"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Excise duty</h3>
<p>Between 2005 and 2010 Union excise duty's share in gross tax revenue fell sharply because of rate cuts (CENVAT rationalisation, the 2008–09 stimulus) and the rising share of direct taxes.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Service tax's share was rising as more services were taxed.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Personal income tax's share rose with incomes and better compliance.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Corporation tax became the largest single source in that period.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Excise (except on petroleum and tobacco) was subsumed into GST in 2017. Today personal income tax has overtaken corporation tax as the largest direct tax, and GST is the largest indirect tax.</li>
<li>Budget 2025–26 made income up to ₹12 lakh effectively tax-free (new regime); the Income-tax Act 2025 replaced the 1961 Act from April 2026.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-144", year: 2010, no: 144, subject: "polity", topic: "State Finance Commission",
  q: `Which one of the following authorities makes recommendation to the Governor of a State as to the principles for determining the taxes and duties which may be appropriated by the Panchayats in that particular State?`,
  opts: ["District Planning Committees", "State Finance Commission", "Finance Ministry of that State", "Panchayati Raj Ministry of that State"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) State Finance Commission</h3>
<p><b>Art 243I</b>: the SFC reviews panchayat finances and recommends principles for sharing State taxes, assigning taxes to panchayats, and grants-in-aid.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> DPCs (Art 243ZD) consolidate district development plans.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The State Finance Department acts on SFC recommendations but does not make them constitutionally.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The Panchayati Raj Department administers PRIs but has no such constitutional role.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>SFCs are to be set up every five years, but many States delay them. The Union Finance Commission (Art 280(3)(bb)) recommends measures to supplement panchayat resources based on SFC recommendations.</li>
<li>The 16th FC (2026–31) continued grants to rural and urban local bodies.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-145", year: 2010, no: 145, subject: "polity", topic: "Tax on stock exchange transactions",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
In India, taxes on transactions in Stock Exchanges and Futures Markets are
<ol><li>levied by the Union</li><li>collected by the States</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> Union List entry 90 covers "taxes other than stamp duties on transactions in stock exchanges and futures markets"; the Union levies Securities Transaction Tax and Commodities Transaction Tax.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> they are collected by the Union. (Only <b>stamp duties</b> on such transactions are levied by the Union but collected by the States under Art 268.)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>STT was introduced in 2004. Budget 2024–25 raised STT on futures to 0.02% and on options to 0.1%.</li>
<li>Since 2020 the Indian Stamp Act amendment has stamp duty on securities collected centrally by exchanges and depositories and passed to States.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-146", year: 2010, no: 146, subject: "geography", topic: "Stagnant crop area",
  q: `In India, during the last decade the total cultivated land for which one of the following crops has remained more or less stagnant?`,
  opts: ["Rice", "Oilseeds", "Pulses", "Sugarcane"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Pulses</h3>
<p>From 2000 to 2010, pulses area stayed at about 22–23 million ha, mostly rainfed with no assured irrigation or price support.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Rice area fluctuated with the monsoon around 43–45 million ha but is not the textbook example.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Oilseeds area rose in the mid-2000s (soybean expansion).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Sugarcane area swings with price cycles (4–5 million ha).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Pulses area rose to about 28–30 million ha after MSP increases and NFSM (2016–2020), but India still imports pulses (tur, urad, lentils, yellow peas).</li>
<li><b>Mission for Aatmanirbharta in Pulses</b> (October 2025): 310 lakh ha and 350 lakh tonnes by 2030–31, with assured procurement of tur, urad and masoor.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-147", year: 2010, no: 147, subject: "economy", topic: "Sugarcane pricing and ECA",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Union Government fixes the Statutory Minimum Price of sugarcane for each sugar season.</li><li>Sugar and sugarcane are essential commodities under the Essential Commodities Act.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Both 1 and 2 (as per key)</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct, per key):</b> the Centre fixed the cane price under the Sugarcane (Control) Order 1966, made under the Essential Commodities Act.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> sugar and sugarcane are listed essential commodities.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Debated: in October 2009 the Statutory Minimum Price was replaced by the <b>Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP)</b>, so for the 2010 exam statement 1 was argued to be outdated, giving (b). The key gives (c).</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>FRP 2026–27:</b> ₹365 per quintal at 10.25% recovery (up from ₹355), approved by the CCEA on CACP advice. States such as UP, Punjab and Haryana add a higher State Advised Price.</li>
<li>Sugar decontrol (Rangarajan Committee, 2013) ended levy sugar and release regulation. The Centre also fixes a minimum selling price of sugar and allows diversion of cane to ethanol.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-148", year: 2010, no: 148, subject: "economy", topic: "GDP growth and public sector share",
  q: `With reference to Indian economy, consider the following statements:
<ol><li>The Gross Domestic Product (GDP) has increased by four times in the last 10 years.</li><li>The percentage share of Public Sector in GDP has declined in the last 10 years.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> from 2000 to 2010 real GDP roughly doubled (about 7% a year) and nominal GDP roughly tripled, not quadrupled.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> with liberalisation and private-sector growth, the public sector's share of GDP fell (from about 25% to about 20%).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India's nominal GDP was about ₹345 lakh crore in 2025–26 under the new series (base 2022–23); real growth was about 7.6–7.7%.</li>
<li>India is projected to pass Japan as the fourth-largest economy in 2027 (IMF, October 2026).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-149", year: 2010, no: 149, subject: "environment", topic: "Warming agents",
  q: `Consider the following which can be found in the ambient atmosphere:
<ol><li>Soot</li><li>Sulphur hexafluoride</li><li>Water vapour</li></ol>
Which of the above contribute to the warming up of the atmosphere?`,
  opts: ["1 and 2 only", "3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> soot (black carbon) absorbs sunlight and darkens snow; it is a short-lived climate forcer and the second-largest warming agent after CO<sub>2</sub> by some estimates.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> SF<sub>6</sub> (used in electrical switchgear) is the most potent greenhouse gas, about 23,500 times CO<sub>2</sub> over 100 years, and lasts thousands of years; it is a Kyoto gas.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> water vapour is the most abundant natural greenhouse gas and amplifies warming (feedback).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Kyoto basket: CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O, HFCs, PFCs, SF<sub>6</sub> (NF<sub>3</sub> added in 2012).</li>
<li>2024 was the first calendar year above 1.5°C of warming; 2025 was the second or third warmest on record (WMO).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2010-150", year: 2010, no: 150, subject: "economy", topic: "International Development Association",
  q: `The International Development Association, a lending agency, is administered by the`,
  opts: ["International Bank for Reconstruction and Development", "International Fund for Agricultural Development", "United Nations Development Programme", "United Nations Industrial Development Organization"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) International Bank for Reconstruction and Development</h3>
<p>IDA (1960) is the World Bank's concessional window for the poorest countries. It shares staff, headquarters and the President with IBRD; together they form "the World Bank".</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> IFAD (1977, Rome) is a separate UN agency funding rural agriculture.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> UNDP gives grants and technical help, not IDA credits.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> UNIDO (Vienna) promotes industrial development.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India was the largest IDA borrower historically and "graduated" in 2014; it is now an IDA donor.</li>
<li>IDA21 replenishment: about $100 bn (December 2024). World Bank President: Ajay Banga (since 2023).</li>
</ul>` }

);
;
