window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2009-141", year: 2009, no: 141, subject: "environment", topic: "Clean Development Mechanism",
  q: `In the context of CO₂ emission and Global Warming, what is the name of a market driven device under the UNFCC that allows developing countries to get funds/incentives from the developed countries to adopt better technologies that reduce greenhouse gas emissions?`,
  opts: ["Carbon Footprint", "Carbon Credit Rating", "Clean Development Mechanism", "Emission Reduction Norm"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Clean Development Mechanism</h3>
<p>The CDM (Kyoto Protocol, Article 12) let developed countries fund emission-cutting projects in developing countries and earn <b>Certified Emission Reductions</b> (CERs) towards their targets. India hosted the second-largest number of CDM projects.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> A carbon footprint measures emissions; it is not a funding device.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Carbon credit rating assesses credit quality; it is not a UNFCCC mechanism.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> "Emission reduction norm" is not a UNFCCC market device.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The iasexamportal key gave (b); the Education Province key gives (c), which is correct.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Under the Paris Agreement, <b>Article 6.4</b> (Paris Agreement Crediting Mechanism) replaces the CDM; eligible CDM projects can transition. Article 6.2 allows bilateral trades (ITMOs).</li>
<li>India's own Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (2023) has compliance and offset mechanisms.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-142", year: 2009, no: 142, subject: "environment", topic: "Origin of carbon credits",
  q: `The concept of carbon credit originated from which one of the following?`,
  opts: ["Earth Summit, Rio de Janeiro", "Kyoto Protocol", "Montreal Protocol", "G-8 Summit, Heiligendamm"], ans: 1, provKey: "C",
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Kyoto Protocol</h3>
<p>The Kyoto Protocol (1997, in force 2005) created binding emission caps for developed countries and three market mechanisms (emissions trading, CDM and Joint Implementation), which gave rise to tradable carbon credits.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Rio 1992 produced the UNFCCC framework, without binding caps or credits.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The Montreal Protocol (1987) deals with ozone-depleting substances, not carbon credits.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The G-8 Heiligendamm summit (2007) discussed halving emissions by 2050 but did not create carbon credits.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The Education Province key shows (c); the iasexamportal key gives (b), which is the standard answer.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The EU Emissions Trading System (2005) is the largest carbon market. China launched its national ETS in 2021.</li>
<li>Voluntary carbon markets face integrity questions; the Integrity Council's Core Carbon Principles (2023) set standards.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-143", year: 2009, no: 143, subject: "ir", topic: "World Economic Forum founder",
  q: `Who among the following is the founder of World Economic Forum?`,
  opts: ["Klaus Schwab", "John Kenneth Galbraith", "Robert Zoellick", "Paul Krugman"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Klaus Schwab</h3>
<p>Schwab founded the European Management Forum in 1971, renamed the World Economic Forum in 1987; it is based in Cologny (Geneva), and its annual meeting is at Davos.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> J.K. Galbraith was an economist and US ambassador to India (1961–63).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Robert Zoellick was World Bank President (2007–12).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Paul Krugman is an economist (Nobel 2008, new trade theory).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Schwab stepped down in April 2025 after whistleblower allegations; the board's review cleared him of material wrongdoing. Larry Fink (BlackRock) and André Hoffmann (Roche) became interim co-chairs in August 2025; Børge Brende is President.</li>
<li>WEF reports: Global Gender Gap (India 131st of 148 in 2025), Global Risks, Travel and Tourism Development Index.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-144", year: 2009, no: 144, subject: "ir", topic: "UNSC non-permanent members",
  q: `The Security Council of UN consists of 5 permanent members, and the remaining 10 members are elected by the General Assembly for a term of`,
  opts: ["1 year", "2 years", "3 years", "5 years"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 years</h3>
<p>Non-permanent members serve two-year terms; five are elected each year by a two-thirds majority of the General Assembly, by regional groups. They cannot be re-elected immediately.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> One year is too short; terms are staggered over two years.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Three-year terms apply to ECOSOC and the Human Rights Council.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Five years is not a UN organ term (ICJ judges serve nine).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>India has served eight terms, most recently 2021–22, and is a candidate for 2028–29. It seeks a permanent seat with the G4 (Brazil, Germany, Japan).</li>
<li>Non-permanent seats were increased from 6 to 10 in 1965.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-145", year: 2009, no: 145, subject: "ir", topic: "Kosovo independence",
  q: `From which one of the following did Kosovo declare its independence?`,
  opts: ["Bulgaria", "Croatia", "Macedonia", "Serbia"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Serbia</h3>
<p>Kosovo, an ethnic-Albanian-majority region, declared independence from Serbia on 17 February 2008, after UN administration following the 1999 NATO intervention.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Bulgaria never controlled Kosovo.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Croatia broke away from Yugoslavia itself in 1991.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Macedonia (now North Macedonia, since 2019) is a neighbour, not the parent state.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The ICJ advisory opinion (2010) said the declaration did not violate international law. About 100 UN members recognise Kosovo; <b>India does not</b> (along with Russia, China, Spain and Serbia).</li>
<li>Tensions in North Kosovo continue; EU-mediated Serbia–Kosovo normalisation talks are stalled.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-146", year: 2009, no: 146, subject: "ir", topic: "Ogaden",
  q: `Which one of the following pairs of countries fought wars over a region called Ogaden?`,
  opts: ["Eritrea and Sudan", "Ethiopia and Somalia", "Kenya and Somalia", "Ethiopia and Sudan"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Ethiopia and Somalia</h3>
<p>The Ogaden (Somali-inhabited eastern Ethiopia) was the cause of the 1964 border war and the 1977–78 Ogaden War, in which Ethiopia (backed by the USSR and Cuba) defeated Somalia.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Eritrea and Sudan have had border tensions, but not over Ogaden.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Kenya and Somalia disputed Kenya's North-Eastern Province (Shifta War, 1960s) and a maritime boundary (ICJ, 2021).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Ethiopia and Sudan dispute the al-Fashaga triangle.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ethiopia–Somalia tensions rose again in 2024 over Ethiopia's port deal with Somaliland; Turkey brokered the Ankara Declaration (December 2024).</li>
<li>The Horn of Africa sits next to the Bab-el-Mandeb strait (Houthi attacks on shipping, 2023–25).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-147", year: 2009, no: 147, subject: "environment", topic: "First Climate Act",
  q: `In the middle of the year 2008 the Parliament of which one of the following countries became the first in the world to enact a Climate Act by passing "The Climate Change Accountability Bill"?`,
  opts: ["Australia", "Canada", "Germany", "Japan"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Canada</h3>
<p>Canada's House of Commons passed Bill C-377, the Climate Change Accountability Act, in June 2008, setting long-term emission targets; it later died in the Senate.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Australia's climate laws came later (Clean Energy Act 2011, Climate Change Act 2022).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Germany's Federal Climate Change Act came in 2019.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Japan's laws on global warming measures date from 1998 but had no binding targets act like this.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The UK's <b>Climate Change Act 2008</b> (passed November 2008) is usually called the world's first legally binding national climate law. Among the given options, Canada fits the question's "mid-2008" bill.</div>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Canada's Net-Zero Emissions Accountability Act was passed in 2021. India has no framework climate law; it uses NDCs, the Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act 2022 and the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme.</li>
<li>The ICJ's advisory opinion (July 2025) said states have legal obligations to prevent significant climate harm.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-148", year: 2009, no: 148, subject: "science", topic: "Energy of visible light",
  q: `Which one among the following has the highest energy?`,
  opts: ["Blue light", "Green light", "Red light", "Yellow light"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Blue light</h3>
<p>Photon energy E = hc/λ: the shorter the wavelength, the higher the energy. Blue (about 450 nm) has a shorter wavelength than green (about 530 nm), yellow (about 580 nm) and red (about 700 nm).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Green has a longer wavelength than blue, so less energy.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Red has the longest wavelength and the lowest energy among visible colours.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Yellow is between green and red.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>VIBGYOR order: violet has the highest energy and red the lowest. Beyond violet is UV, beyond red is infrared.</li>
<li>Blue LEDs enabled white LED lighting (Nobel Prize 2014).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-149", year: 2009, no: 149, subject: "economy", topic: "Early economic events",
  q: `In the context of independent India's economy, which one of the following was the earliest event to take place?`,
  opts: ["Nationalisation of Insurance companies", "Nationalisation of State Bank of India", "Enactment of Banking Regulation Act", "Introduction of First Five-Year Plan"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Enactment of Banking Regulation Act (1949)</h3>
<p>Order: Banking Regulation Act (March 1949), First Five-Year Plan (1951), State Bank of India created from the Imperial Bank (1955), life insurance nationalised and LIC set up (1956).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Life insurance was nationalised in 1956 (general insurance in 1972–73).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> SBI was formed in 1955.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> The First Plan began in 1951.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>RBI was nationalised on 1 January 1949. Banks were nationalised in 1969 (14) and 1980 (6).</li>
<li>The Banking Laws (Amendment) Act 2025 allows up to four nominees per account.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2009-150", year: 2009, no: 150, subject: "ca", caYear: "2008–09", topic: "Automobile makers' headquarters",
  q: `Consider the following pairs:
<table><tr><th>Automobile Manufacturer</th><th>Headquarters</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. BMW AG</td><td>USA</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Daimler AG</td><td>Sweden</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Renault S.A.</td><td>France</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Volkswagen AG</td><td>Germany</td></tr></table>
Which of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "3 and 4", "4 only", "1, 2 and 4"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3 and 4</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Pair 1 (wrong):</b> BMW is headquartered in <b>Munich, Germany</b>.</li>
<li><b>Pair 2 (wrong):</b> Daimler (now Mercedes-Benz Group) is based in <b>Stuttgart, Germany</b>.</li>
<li><b>Pair 3 (correct):</b> Renault is French (Boulogne-Billancourt).</li>
<li><b>Pair 4 (correct):</b> Volkswagen is German (Wolfsburg).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Volvo Cars is Swedish (owned by China's Geely). Renault took full ownership of the Renault–Nissan Chennai plant by buying Nissan's stake (2025).</li>
<li>India is the third-largest car market. The EV policy (2024) cut import duty for firms investing $500 million; Tesla entered India in July 2025.</li>
</ul>` }

);
;
