window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(

{ id: "2006-52", year: 2006, no: 52, subject: "modern", topic: "Interim Government 1946",
  q: `Which portfolio was held by Dr. Rajendra Prasad in the Interim Government formed in the year 1946?`,
  opts: ["Defence", "External Affairs and Commonwealth", "Food and Agriculture", "None"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Food and Agriculture</h3>
<p>In the Interim Government (2 September 1946), <b>Rajendra Prasad</b> held <b>Food and Agriculture</b>. He was soon elected President of the Constituent Assembly (11 December 1946).</p>
<table><tr><th>Member</th><th>Portfolio</th></tr>
<tr><td>Jawaharlal Nehru</td><td>Vice-President of the Executive Council; External Affairs and Commonwealth Relations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vallabhbhai Patel</td><td>Home, Information and Broadcasting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Baldev Singh</td><td>Defence</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Rajagopalachari</td><td>Education</td></tr>
<tr><td>Liaquat Ali Khan (League, from October)</td><td>Finance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jagjivan Ram</td><td>Labour</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Defence was held by Baldev Singh.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> External Affairs was Nehru's.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Prasad did hold a portfolio.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Viceroy (Wavell, then Mountbatten) remained the President of the Executive Council. The League's Liaquat Ali Khan presented the "poor man's budget" (1947).</li>
<li>Prasad: first President of India (1950–62, the only one elected twice), Bharat Ratna 1962.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-53", year: 2006, no: 53, subject: "culture", topic: "Chandralekha and the SNA Ratna",
  q: `Who among the following was presented with the Sangeet Natak Akademi Ratna Award, 2005 for her lifetime achievement in dance?`,
  opts: ["Chandralekha", "Tanjavur Balasaraswati", "Uma Sharma", "Yamini Krishnamurthy"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Chandralekha</h3>
<p><b>Chandralekha</b> (1928–2006), the Chennai-based choreographer who reinterpreted Bharatanatyam and yoga and kalaripayattu into contemporary dance (<i>Angika</i>, <i>Sharira</i>), was chosen for the <b>Sangeet Natak Akademi Ratna</b> (Fellowship), announced in early 2005.</p>
<div class="note">The fellowship belongs to the 2004 cycle (announced January 2005), which is why sources give either year.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> T. Balasaraswati (1918–84), legendary Bharatanatyam dancer; she had died long before 2005.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Uma Sharma, Kathak dancer (Delhi); Padma Bhushan.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Yamini Krishnamurthy, Bharatanatyam and Kuchipudi dancer; she died in 2024.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The SNA Fellowship (Akademi Ratna) is limited to 40 living persons; the Akademi Puraskar is given yearly. Sangeet Natak Akademi (1952) is India's national academy for music, dance and drama.</li>
<li>Other national academies: Sahitya Akademi (letters, 1954), Lalit Kala Akademi (fine arts, 1954).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-54", year: 2006, no: 54, subject: "environment", topic: "Carbon tax (New Zealand)",
  q: `Which one of the following countries is the first country in the world to propose a carbon tax for its people to address global warming?`,
  opts: ["Australia", "Germany", "Japan", "New Zealand"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) New Zealand</h3>
<p>The key refers to <b>New Zealand</b>'s 2005 announcement of a general carbon tax (to start in 2007) as part of its Kyoto commitments. It was abandoned in late 2005 and replaced by an <b>emissions trading scheme</b> (2008).</p>
<div class="note">Debated: <b>Finland</b> introduced the world's first carbon tax in <b>1990</b>, followed by Sweden, Norway and Denmark (1991–92). New Zealand's proposal was what was "in the news" in 2005–06. The key is followed.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Australia introduced a carbon price in 2012 and repealed it in 2014.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Germany had an ecological tax reform (1999) on energy, and a national CO<sub>2</sub> price on heating and transport fuels from 2021.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Japan introduced a modest "tax for climate change mitigation" in 2012.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Carbon pricing: tax (fixed price) vs cap-and-trade (fixed quantity). EU ETS (2005) is the biggest; its <b>CBAM</b> (carbon border tax) starts charging in 2026, which affects Indian steel and aluminium exports.</li>
<li>India's equivalents: Clean Energy Cess on coal (Rs 400/tonne, subsumed in GST compensation cess in 2017); Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (2023).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-55", year: 2006, no: 55, subject: "polity", topic: "High Court judges: practice and qualification",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>A person who has held office as a permanent Judge of a High Court cannot plead or act in any court or before any authority in India except the Supreme Court.</li><li>A person is not qualified for appointment as a Judge of a High Court in India unless he has for at least five years held a judicial office in the territory of India.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Article 220: a retired permanent HC judge may practise before the <b>Supreme Court and other High Courts</b> (only not in the court where he/she served, and courts below it). So "except the Supreme Court" is too narrow.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Article 217(2): a citizen who has held a judicial office in India for <b>10 years</b>, or has been an advocate of a High Court for 10 years. Not five.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Supreme Court judge (Article 124(3)): 5 years as HC judge, or 10 years as HC advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President's opinion.</li>
<li>Retirement: HC 62, SC 65. Appointments via the collegium (Second and Third Judges cases); the NJAC (99th Amendment) was struck down in 2015.</li>
<li>See 2007 Q77 for a similar question.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-57", year: 2006, no: 57, subject: "geography", topic: "Capitals",
  q: `Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?`,
  opts: ["Slovenia : Bratislava", "Seychelles : Victoria", "Sierra Leone : Freetown", "Uzbekistan : Tashkent"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Slovenia : Bratislava</h3>
<p><b>Bratislava</b> is the capital of <b>Slovakia</b>. Slovenia's capital is <b>Ljubljana</b>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Correct: Victoria is the capital of Seychelles (on Mahé). India is developing facilities on Assumption Island (stalled).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Correct: Freetown is the capital of Sierra Leone (founded for freed slaves, 1792).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Correct: Tashkent is the capital of Uzbekistan (Tashkent Declaration, 1966; Shastri died there).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Easily confused: Slovakia (Bratislava, on the Danube) vs Slovenia (Ljubljana); Austria (Vienna) also on the Danube, only about 60 km from Bratislava, the closest pair of European capitals.</li>
<li>Other capital pairs: Niger (Niamey) vs Nigeria (Abuja); Guinea (Conakry) vs Guinea-Bissau (Bissau); Congo (Brazzaville) vs DR Congo (Kinshasa), the closest capitals in the world, across the Congo river.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-58", year: 2006, no: 58, subject: "culture", topic: "Wole Soyinka",
  q: `Who is Wole Soyinka?`,
  opts: ["A well-known economist", "A well-known football player", "A well-known industrialist owning steel plants in many parts of the world", "A Nobel Prize winner for literature"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) A Nobel Prize winner for literature</h3>
<p><b>Wole Soyinka</b> (born 1934), Nigerian playwright, poet and essayist, won the <b>1986 Nobel Prize in Literature</b>, the first African laureate. Works: <i>Death and the King's Horseman</i>, <i>The Lion and the Jewel</i>, prison memoir <i>The Man Died</i>.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Not an economist (African economics laureate: W. Arthur Lewis, 1979).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Not a footballer.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> The steel magnate is Lakshmi Mittal (ArcelorMittal).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other African literature laureates: Naguib Mahfouz (Egypt, 1988), Nadine Gordimer (1991), J.M. Coetzee (2003), Doris Lessing (2007), Abdulrazak Gurnah (Tanzania, 2021).</li>
<li>Chinua Achebe (<i>Things Fall Apart</i>) is Nigeria's other literary giant; he never won the Nobel.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-59", year: 2006, no: 59, subject: "geography", topic: "Miles, Harmattan, Iberia",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Length of a terrestrial mile is lesser than that of a nautical mile.</li><li>Harmattan is a dusty land wind of the East African Coast.</li><li>Greece and Albania form a part of the Iberian Peninsula.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1 only"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (correct):</b> a statute (land) mile = 1.609 km; a <b>nautical mile = 1.852 km</b> (one minute of latitude). A knot is one nautical mile per hour.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> the <b>Harmattan</b> is a dry, dusty north-easterly wind from the Sahara blowing over <b>West Africa</b> (Gulf of Guinea) in winter; locally called "the doctor" because it brings relief from humid heat.</li>
<li><b>3 (wrong):</b> Greece and Albania are in the <b>Balkan</b> Peninsula. The Iberian Peninsula has Spain, Portugal, Andorra and Gibraltar.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<table><tr><th>Local wind</th><th>Where</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chinook (warm, "snow eater")</td><td>Rockies, USA/Canada</td></tr>
<tr><td>Foehn</td><td>Alps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sirocco</td><td>Sahara to Mediterranean (Italy)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mistral, Bora (cold)</td><td>France (Rhône), Adriatic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loo (hot)</td><td>North Indian plains</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kal Baisakhi (Nor'westers)</td><td>West Bengal, Assam</td></tr></table>` },

{ id: "2006-63", year: 2006, no: 63, subject: "ir", topic: "ASEAN members",
  q: `Which one of the following countries is not a member of ASEAN?`,
  opts: ["Vietnam", "Brunei Darussalam", "Bangladesh", "Myanmar"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Bangladesh</h3>
<p><b>Bangladesh</b> is a South Asian country (SAARC, BIMSTEC member); it is not in ASEAN. It is a member of the ASEAN Regional Forum (security dialogue).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Vietnam joined ASEAN in 1995.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Brunei joined in 1984.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Myanmar joined in 1997 (with Laos); since the 2021 coup its junta leaders are barred from summits.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>ASEAN (Bangkok Declaration, 1967; secretariat Jakarta): founders Indonesia, Malaysia, Philippines, Singapore, Thailand; then Brunei, Vietnam, Laos, Myanmar, Cambodia; <b>Timor-Leste</b> became the 11th member in October 2025.</li>
<li>India: Look East (1991) → Act East (2014); Comprehensive Strategic Partnership (2022); ASEAN–India Trade in Goods Agreement (2009) under review. 2025 was the ASEAN–India Year of Tourism.</li>
<li>BIMSTEC links Bangladesh with Myanmar and Thailand.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-64", year: 2006, no: 64, subject: "science", topic: "INSAT-4A",
  q: `Consider the following statements in respect of India's advanced satellite INSAT-4A:
<ol><li>INSAT-4A was launched in December, 2005 from New Mexico.</li><li>The European commercial launch service provider Arianespace was associated with the launch of INSAT-4A.</li><li>Tata Sky, a digital cable service provider, is associated with DTH television broadcasting services from INSAT-4A.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above are correct?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> INSAT-4A was launched on 22 December 2005 by an Ariane-5 from <b>Kourou, French Guiana</b>, not New Mexico.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> <b>Arianespace</b> launched it.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> <b>Tata Sky</b> (now Tata Play, launched August 2006) used INSAT-4A's Ku-band transponders for direct-to-home TV.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>INSAT-4A had 12 Ku-band and 12 C-band transponders. DTH in India: DD Free Dish (public, free), Tata Play, Airtel Digital TV, Dish TV, Sun Direct.</li>
<li>ISRO's commercial arm <b>NSIL</b> now owns and leases communication satellites (GSAT-24 for Tata Play, 2022). India's satellite communication policy now allows private players (IN-SPACe).</li>
<li>India still uses Ariane for heavy satellites (GSAT-20 went on SpaceX Falcon 9 in 2024; LVM3 launched CMS-03, the heaviest from India, in 2025).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-65", year: 2006, no: 65, subject: "economy", topic: "APTEL and APDRP",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Appellate Tribunal for Electricity has been established by each State Government in India.</li><li>One of the components of the Accelerated Power Development and Reforms Programme (APDRP) is upgradation of sub-transmission and distribution system for electricity in India.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> there is a single <b>Appellate Tribunal for Electricity (APTEL)</b>, set up by the <b>Centre</b> under Section 110 of the Electricity Act, 2003, in New Delhi (2005). It hears appeals against CERC and State Electricity Regulatory Commissions. Appeals from APTEL go to the Supreme Court.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> APDRP (2002–03) funded upgrading of sub-transmission and distribution networks and cutting transmission and distribution (AT&amp;C) losses.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Distribution reform schemes: APDRP → R-APDRP (2008) → IPDS and UDAY (2015, discom debt) → <b>RDSS</b> (2021, smart prepaid meters, loss reduction). AT&amp;C losses fell from about 22% (2021) to about 16% (2024).</li>
<li>Electricity (Amendment) Bill (2022, and a 2025 draft) proposes multiple distribution licensees in an area.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-66", year: 2006, no: 66, subject: "culture", topic: "Buddhist monasteries",
  q: `Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?
<table><tr><th>Monastery</th><th>State</th></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["Dhankar Monastery : Himachal Pradesh", "Rumtek Monastery : Sikkim", "Tabo Monastery : Himachal Pradesh", "Kye Monastery : Arunachal Pradesh"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Kye Monastery : Arunachal Pradesh</h3>
<p><b>Key (Kye/Ki) Monastery</b> (about 4,100 m) is in the <b>Spiti valley, Himachal Pradesh</b>, the largest monastery of Spiti (Gelugpa sect). Arunachal's famous monastery is <b>Tawang</b> (largest in India).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Correct: Dhankar (Spiti, HP), a cliff-top fort monastery.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Correct: Rumtek (near Gangtok, Sikkim), seat of the Karmapa (Kagyu sect).</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Correct: Tabo (Spiti, HP), founded in 996 CE by Rinchen Zangpo; called the "Ajanta of the Himalayas" for its murals.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other monasteries: Hemis, Thiksey, Diskit, Lamayuru (Ladakh); Tawang (Arunachal); Enchey, Pemayangtse (Sikkim); Ghoom (Darjeeling); Namdroling (Bylakuppe, Karnataka).</li>
<li>The Dalai Lama (90 in July 2025) said his succession will be decided by his Gaden Phodrang Trust, rejecting a Chinese role.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-67", year: 2006, no: 67, subject: "geography", topic: "Himalayan and NE valleys",
  q: `Match List-I with List-II and select the correct answer using the code given below the lists:
<table><tr><th>List-I (Valley)</th><th>List-II (State)</th></tr>
<tr><td>A. Markha Valley</td><td>1. Sikkim</td></tr>
<tr><td>B. Dzukou Valley</td><td>2. Himachal Pradesh</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Sangla Valley</td><td>3. Jammu and Kashmir</td></tr>
<tr><td>D. Yumthang Valley</td><td>4. Nagaland</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1", "A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4", "A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4", "A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1</h3>
<table><tr><th>Valley</th><th>Where</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Markha</td><td>Ladakh (then J&amp;K)</td><td>Popular trek in Hemis National Park</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dzukou</td><td>Nagaland–Manipur border</td><td>Dzukou lily; frequent forest fires and a boundary dispute</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sangla (Baspa valley)</td><td>Kinnaur, Himachal Pradesh</td><td>Baspa river; Chitkul is the last village near the Tibet border</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yumthang</td><td>North Sikkim</td><td>"Valley of Flowers" of Sikkim; Shingba Rhododendron Sanctuary</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Puts Markha in Himachal and Sangla in J&amp;K; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Puts Dzukou in Sikkim and Yumthang in Nagaland; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Wrong on all four.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Valley of Flowers (Uttarakhand) is a World Heritage Site with Nanda Devi NP.</li>
<li>North Sikkim was hit by the South Lhonak GLOF (October 2023) that destroyed the Teesta-III dam at Chungthang.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-70", year: 2006, no: 70, subject: "geography", topic: "Sources of peninsular rivers",
  q: `Which one of the following statements is not correct?`,
  opts: ["Mahanadi River rises in Chhattisgarh", "Godavari River rises in Maharashtra", "Cauvery River rises in Andhra Pradesh", "Tapti River rises in Madhya Pradesh"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Cauvery River rises in Andhra Pradesh</h3>
<p>The <b>Kaveri (Cauvery)</b> rises at <b>Talakaveri</b> in the Brahmagiri hills, <b>Kodagu (Coorg), Karnataka</b>, and flows through Karnataka and Tamil Nadu to the Bay of Bengal. It does not touch Andhra Pradesh.</p>
<div class="note">This item is missing from the Education Province copy (and so has no circulated key); the text is from iasexamportal's copy and the answer is by analysis.</div>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Correct: the Mahanadi rises near <b>Sihawa</b> (Dhamtari, Chhattisgarh).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Correct: the Godavari rises at <b>Trimbakeshwar</b> (Nashik, Maharashtra); India's longest peninsular river ("Dakshin Ganga").</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Correct: the Tapi rises at <b>Multai</b> (Betul, Madhya Pradesh).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Krishna rises at Mahabaleshwar (Maharashtra); the Narmada and Son at Amarkantak (MP); the Pennar in Karnataka (Nandi hills).</li>
<li>Kaveri water dispute: Tribunal award (2007), Supreme Court verdict (2018), Cauvery Water Management Authority. Mekedatu dam (Karnataka) is opposed by Tamil Nadu.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-71", year: 2006, no: 71, subject: "environment", topic: "Bundala Biosphere Reserve",
  q: `Where is Bundala Biosphere Reserve which has been recently added to the UNESCO's Man and Biosphere (MAB) network, located?`,
  opts: ["Russia", "India", "Sri Lanka", "Bangladesh"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Sri Lanka</h3>
<p><b>Bundala</b> (Hambantota district, southern <b>Sri Lanka</b>) was added to UNESCO's World Network of Biosphere Reserves in 2005. It was Sri Lanka's first Ramsar site (1990), famous for greater flamingos and migratory waders.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Russia has many biosphere reserves (Baikal etc.) but Bundala is not one.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> India's reserves on the network then: Nilgiri, Gulf of Mannar, Sundarbans, Nanda Devi (2008 Q90).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Bangladesh's Sundarbans is a World Heritage Site and Ramsar site; not Bundala.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sri Lanka's other biosphere reserves: Hurulu, Sinharaja (also a World Heritage Site), Kanneliya–Dediyagala–Nakiyadeniya, Knuckles.</li>
<li>India now has 13 reserves on the network (Cold Desert added 2025) and over 90 Ramsar sites.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-72", year: 2006, no: 72, subject: "geography", topic: "Literacy, Census 2001",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>According to the Census 2001, Kerala has the smallest gap in male and female literacy rates among the 28 States of India (Delhi and Pondicherry not included).</li><li>According to the Census 2001, Rajasthan has literacy rate above the national average literacy rate.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> in 2001 the smallest male–female literacy gap was in <b>Mizoram</b> (about 4.6 points: 90.7% vs 86.1%), followed by Meghalaya; Kerala's gap was about 6.3 points (94.2% vs 87.9%).</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> Rajasthan's literacy (about 60.4%) was <b>below</b> the national average (64.8%), though it had the fastest rise in the 1990s.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Census 2011: national literacy 74%; male 82%, female 65%. Highest: Kerala (94%), Lakshadweep; lowest: Bihar (61.8%). Smallest gender gap: Meghalaya; largest: Rajasthan (about 27 points).</li>
<li>PLFS 2023–24 puts literacy (age 7+) at about 80%. ULLAS (New India Literacy Programme, 2022–27) targets adult literacy.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-73", year: 2006, no: 73, subject: "economy", topic: "NREGA provisions",
  q: `Consider the following statements in respect of the National Rural Employment Guarantee Act, 2005:
<ol><li>Under the provisions of the Act, 100 days of employment in a year to every household whose adult members volunteer to do unskilled manual work has become a fundamental right.</li><li>Under the provisions of the Act, women are to get priority to the extent that one-half of persons who are given employment are women who have asked for work.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Neither 1 nor 2</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> NREGA created a <b>legal (statutory) right</b> to work, not a Fundamental Right under Part III. If work is not given within 15 days, an unemployment allowance is due.</li>
<li><b>2 (wrong):</b> priority to women is such that <b>at least one-third</b> of beneficiaries are women (Schedule II), not one-half. (In practice women's share has been about 55–59%.)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Other features: work within 5 km, worksite facilities (crèche, water, shade), social audits by Gram Sabhas, wages paid through bank/post accounts (Aadhaar-based payments since 2024).</li>
<li>In December 2025 MGNREGA was replaced by a new law (Viksit Bharat – G RAM G) with a 125-day guarantee.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Unverified: the provisions of the 2025 replacement law; check the official text.</div>` },

{ id: "2006-74", year: 2006, no: 74, subject: "medieval", topic: "Afghan rulers of Delhi", provKey: "A",
  q: `Which one of the following is the correct chronological order of the Afghan rulers to the throne of Delhi?`,
  opts: ["Sikandar Shah—Ibrahim Lodi—Bahlol Khan Lodi", "Sikandar Shah—Bahlol Khan Lodi—Ibrahim Lodi", "Bahlol Khan Lodi—Sikandar Shah—Ibrahim Lodi", "Bahlol Khan Lodi—Ibrahim Lodi—Sikandar Shah"], ans: 3,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Bahlol Khan Lodi—Ibrahim Lodi—Sikandar Shah</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (a), which puts Bahlol Lodi, the founder of the Lodi dynasty, last; that cannot be right. "Sikandar Shah" here is <b>Sikandar Shah Suri</b> (1555), the last Afghan ruler of Delhi, as distinct from Sikandar <b>Lodi</b>.</div>
<table><tr><th>Ruler</th><th>Reign</th><th>Note</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bahlol Khan Lodi</td><td>1451–89</td><td>Founded the Lodi (first Afghan) dynasty</td></tr>
<tr><td>(Sikandar Lodi)</td><td>1489–1517</td><td>Founded Agra (1504); not named "Shah" here</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ibrahim Lodi</td><td>1517–26</td><td>Killed by Babur at Panipat (1526)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sikandar Shah Suri</td><td>1555</td><td>Last Sur ruler; defeated by Akbar's forces at Sirhind, before Humayun regained Delhi</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Puts Bahlol last; impossible.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Puts Sikandar Shah first; impossible either way.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Fits only if "Sikandar Shah" means Sikandar Lodi; the question's naming points to Sikandar Shah Suri.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Afghan dynasties: Lodis (1451–1526) and Surs (Sher Shah 1540–45, Islam Shah, …, Sikandar Shah 1555).</li>
<li>Sher Shah: Grand Trunk Road, rupiya coin, land measurement, sarais.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">Debated: some solutions read "Sikandar Shah" as Sikandar Lodi and choose (c). (d) is preferred because the paper names the Lodis with the surname and Sikandar Shah without it.</div>` },

{ id: "2006-75", year: 2006, no: 75, subject: "medieval", topic: "Sant Tukaram",
  q: `Bhakta Tukaram was a contemporary of which Mughal Emperor?`,
  opts: ["Babar", "Akbar", "Jahangir", "Aurangzeb"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Jahangir</h3>
<p><b>Sant Tukaram</b> (about 1608–1650), the Marathi Varkari saint-poet of Dehu (near Pune), lived during the reigns of <b>Jahangir</b> (1605–27) and Shah Jahan (1628–58). His abhangas praise Vitthala of Pandharpur. Shivaji is said to have met him.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Babur died in 1530, long before Tukaram's birth.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Akbar died in 1605, before Tukaram was born.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Aurangzeb came to the throne in 1658, after Tukaram's death.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Maharashtra's Bhakti saints: Jnaneshwar (13th century, Jnaneshwari), Namdev (14th), Eknath (16th), Tukaram (17th), Ramdas (Shivaji's guru).</li>
<li>Pandharpur Wari pilgrimage (Ashadhi Ekadashi) carries the palkhis of Jnaneshwar (Alandi) and Tukaram (Dehu).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-76", year: 2006, no: 76, subject: "medieval", topic: "Abdul Hamid Lahori",
  q: `In Indian history, who was Abdul Hamid Lahori?`,
  opts: ["An important military commander during Akbar's reign", "An official historian of the reign of Shah Jahan", "An important noble and confidant of Aurangzeb", "A chronicler and poet during the reign of Muhammad Shah"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) An official historian of the reign of Shah Jahan</h3>
<p><b>Abdul Hamid Lahori</b> wrote the <b><i>Padshahnama</i></b> (<i>Badshahnama</i>), the official history of the first twenty years of <b>Shah Jahan</b>'s reign. Its illustrated manuscript is in the Royal Collection at Windsor.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Akbar's chronicler was Abul Fazl (<i>Akbarnama</i>, <i>Ain-i-Akbari</i>); commanders included Man Singh, Bairam Khan.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Aurangzeb's period: Khafi Khan wrote <i>Muntakhab-ul-Lubab</i>; Saqi Mustaid Khan wrote <i>Maasir-i-Alamgiri</i>.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Muhammad Shah's reign (1719–48) saw Nadir Shah's invasion; not Lahori.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mughal chronicles: <i>Baburnama</i> (Babur, Turki), <i>Humayunnama</i> (Gulbadan Begum), <i>Tuzuk-i-Jahangiri</i>, <i>Shahjahannama</i> (Inayat Khan), <i>Alamgirnama</i> (Muhammad Kazim).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-78", year: 2006, no: 78, subject: "modern", topic: "Governor-General during 1857",
  q: `Who was the Governor-General of India during the Sepoy Mutiny?`,
  opts: ["Lord Canning", "Lord Dalhousie", "Lord Hardinge", "Lord Lytton"], ans: 0,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Lord Canning</h3>
<p><b>Lord Canning</b> was Governor-General (1856–58) during the revolt of 1857 and became the first <b>Viceroy</b> under the Government of India Act 1858. He read out Queen Victoria's Proclamation at Allahabad (1 November 1858). Called "Clemency Canning" for opposing indiscriminate revenge.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(b)</b> Dalhousie (1848–56) left just before the revolt; his Doctrine of Lapse and annexations (Awadh) were among its causes.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Hardinge I (1844–48) fought the First Anglo-Sikh War; Hardinge II (1910–16) moved the capital to Delhi.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Lytton (1876–80): Delhi Durbar, Vernacular Press Act.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Also under Canning: Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act (1856), General Service Enlistment Act (1856), universities of Calcutta, Bombay and Madras (1857), Indian Councils Act (1861), Indian Penal Code (1860).</li>
<li>1857 revolt began at Meerut (10 May 1857); Mangal Pandey at Barrackpore (March 1857).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-79", year: 2006, no: 79, subject: "modern", topic: "Madame Bhikaji Cama", provKey: "D",
  q: `Consider the following statements about Madam Bhikaji Cama:
<ol><li>Madam Cama unfurled the National Flag at the International Socialist Conference in Paris in the year 1907.</li><li>Madam Cama served as private secretary to Dadabhai Naoroji.</li><li>Madam Cama was born to Parsi parents.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "3 only"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (d). But Bhikaji Cama did work as <b>private secretary to Dadabhai Naoroji</b> in London (from about 1905), as standard biographies record, so statement 2 is correct.</div>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> she unfurled the "Flag of Indian Independence" at the International Socialist Congress at <b>Stuttgart</b> (Germany), 22 August 1907, not Paris (although she was based in Paris).</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> she worked as Naoroji's private secretary in London.</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> she was born in Bombay (1861) to a wealthy <b>Parsi</b> family (Patel).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Cama co-founded the Paris Indian Society and published <i>Bande Mataram</i> and <i>Madan's Talwar</i>; worked with Shyamji Krishna Varma and S.R. Rana. She returned to India only in 1935, dying in 1936.</li>
<li>Her 1907 flag (green, saffron, red with "Vande Mataram") is preserved at the Maratha and Kesari Library, Pune.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-80", year: 2006, no: 80, subject: "ca", caYear: 2006, topic: "Davis Cup 2005",
  q: `Whom did Croatia defeat in the Davis Cup-2005 Finals to win the Davis Cup-2005?`,
  opts: ["United States of America", "Slovak Republic", "Argentina", "Spain"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Slovak Republic</h3>
<p><b>Croatia</b> won its first Davis Cup in December 2005, beating the <b>Slovak Republic</b> 3–2 in Bratislava (Ivan Ljubičić, Mario Ančić).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> The USA has the most titles (32), last in 2007.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Argentina won only in 2016 (beating Croatia).</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Spain won in 2000, 2004, 2008, 2009, 2011 and 2019.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Davis Cup (men's team tennis, 1900); Billie Jean King Cup for women. Since 2019 the final stage is a single-venue "Finals" event.</li>
<li><b>Italy</b> won three in a row: 2023, 2024 and 2025.</li>
<li>India reached the final three times (1966, 1974 (forfeited due to apartheid), 1987).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-81", year: 2006, no: 81, subject: "culture", topic: "Angkor Wat",
  q: `The initial design and construction of which massive temple took place during the reign of Suryavarman II?`,
  opts: ["Sri Mariamman Temple", "Angkor Vat", "Batu Caves Temple", "Kamakhya Temple"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Angkor Vat</h3>
<p><b>Angkor Wat</b> (Cambodia), the world's largest religious monument, was built in the early 12th century under the Khmer king <b>Suryavarman II</b> as a Vishnu temple; it later became Buddhist. It appears on Cambodia's flag.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Sri Mariamman Temple is Singapore's oldest Hindu temple (1827), built by Tamil migrants.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Batu Caves (near Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia): Murugan shrine, Thaipusam festival; 19th century.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Kamakhya Temple (Guwahati, Assam): Shakti peetha; rebuilt by Koch king Naranarayana (1565).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The Archaeological Survey of India has restored Angkor Wat (1986–93) and is restoring <b>Ta Prohm</b> and Preah Vihear.</li>
<li>Other Indic sites abroad: Borobudur and Prambanan (Indonesia), My Son (Vietnam), Wat Phou (Laos; ASI restoration).</li>
<li>Thailand–Cambodia border clashes (2025) flared near disputed Khmer temples (Preah Vihear area, Ta Muen Thom).</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-82", year: 2006, no: 82, subject: "culture", topic: "Konark wheel logo",
  q: `Indian Airlines (New name: Indian) have redesigned their logo which is a graphic wheel. This logo has been inspired from which one of the following?`,
  opts: ["Hampi Temples", "Mamallapuram Temples", "Sun Temple, Konark", "Khajuraho Temples"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Sun Temple, Konark</h3>
<p>Indian Airlines' 2005 rebranding as "Indian" used an orange wheel inspired by the <b>wheels of the Konark Sun Temple</b> (Odisha), built as the chariot of Surya by Narasimhadeva I of the Eastern Ganga dynasty (13th century).</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Hampi's famous chariot is the stone chariot of the Vittala temple (2007 Q126), not the logo's inspiration.</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Mamallapuram (Pallava, 7th–8th century): Shore Temple, Pancha Rathas, Arjuna's Penance.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Khajuraho (Chandela, 10th–11th century): Nagara temples with sculpture.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Konark: World Heritage Site (1984), "Black Pagoda"; 24 wheels (sundials) and 7 horses. The Konark wheel appears on the Rs 10 note and was the backdrop at the G20 summit's Bharat Mandapam (2023).</li>
<li>Indian Airlines merged into Air India (2007), which Tata acquired in 2022.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-83", year: 2006, no: 83, subject: "ca", caYear: 2006, topic: "Personalities and fields", provKey: "C",
  q: `Match List-I with List-II and select the correct answer using the code given below the lists:
<table><tr><th>List-I</th><th>List-II</th></tr>
<tr><td>A. E.C.G. Sudarshan</td><td>1. Theatre</td></tr>
<tr><td>B. V. Shanta</td><td>2. Violin</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. T.N. Krishnan</td><td>3. Research in Physics</td></tr>
<tr><td>D. Mahesh Dattani</td><td>4. Cancer treatment</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td>5. Water harvesting</td></tr></table>`,
  opts: ["A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4", "A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1", "A-2, B-4, C-5, D-1", "A-2, B-1, C-5, D-4"], ans: 1,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1</h3>
<div class="note">The circulated (Education Province) key says (c), which makes E.C.G. Sudarshan a violinist and T.N. Krishnan a water-harvester. That is plainly wrong.</div>
<table><tr><th>Person</th><th>Field</th></tr>
<tr><td>E.C.G. Sudarshan (1931–2018)</td><td>Theoretical physics: V–A theory of weak interaction, quantum optics (Glauber–Sudarshan representation), tachyons</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dr V. Shanta (1927–2021)</td><td>Cancer treatment: chairperson of the Adyar Cancer Institute, Chennai; Magsaysay Award 2005</td></tr>
<tr><td>T.N. Krishnan (1928–2020)</td><td>Carnatic violin maestro</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mahesh Dattani</td><td>Playwright and theatre director; first English-language playwright to win the Sahitya Akademi Award (1998, <i>Final Solutions</i>)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Makes Dr Shanta a theatre person and Dattani a cancer doctor; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(c)</b> Makes Sudarshan a violinist and Krishnan a water-harvester; wrong.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> Wrong on all four.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sudarshan was overlooked for the Nobel (Glauber's 2005 prize drew controversy). Water harvesting (option 5, unmatched): Rajendra Singh, Anupam Mishra.</li>
<li>Cancer care: National Cancer Grid; Budget 2025 announced day-care cancer centres in all district hospitals.</li>
</ul>` }
);
;
