{ id: "2006-140", year: 2006, no: 140, subject: "science", topic: "CMYK in printing",
  q: `To which one of the following processes is the term CMYK related?`,
  opts: ["Railway signalling", "Navigation", "Offset printing", "Electronic voting machine"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Offset printing</h3>
<p><b>CMYK</b> (Cyan, Magenta, Yellow and Key = black) is the four-colour process used in <b>offset</b> and other colour printing. Overlapping tiny dots of these inks (subtractive mixing) reproduce the full range of colours; black adds depth and saves ink.</p>
<h3>Why not the others</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>(a)</b> Railway signalling uses red, yellow, green aspects (and Kavach for automatic protection).</li>
<li><b>(b)</b> Navigation uses GPS/NavIC, compasses, charts; not CMYK.</li>
<li><b>(d)</b> EVMs (with VVPAT since 2013–19) use ballot units and control units; not CMYK.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Screens use additive <b>RGB</b>; print uses subtractive CMYK (see 2007 Q138).</li>
<li>Offset printing: ink is transferred ("offset") from a plate to a rubber blanket and then to paper; currency notes use offset and intaglio printing.</li>
</ul>` },

{ id: "2006-141", year: 2006, no: 141, subject: "ir", topic: "Kofi Annan",
  q: `Consider the following statements:
<ol><li>Kofi Annan, the UN Secretary General, is from Nigeria.</li><li>Kofi Annan is the first UN Secretary General to be appointed from the ranks of the United Nations staff.</li><li>Kofi Annan was appointed for a second term to the office of the UN Secretary General.</li></ol>
Which of the statements given above are correct?`,
  opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
  analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3>
<h3>Statement by statement</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>1 (wrong):</b> Kofi Annan (1938–2018) was from <b>Ghana</b>.</li>
<li><b>2 (correct):</b> he was the first Secretary-General to rise from the ranks of UN staff (he had headed peacekeeping).</li>
<li><b>3 (correct):</b> he served two terms (1997–2006); he and the UN shared the <b>2001 Nobel Peace Prize</b>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Holistic notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Secretaries-General: Trygve Lie, Dag Hammarskjöld, U Thant, Kurt Waldheim, Pérez de Cuéllar, Boutros-Ghali, Kofi Annan, Ban Ki-moon, <b>António Guterres</b> (2017–2026). Selection of his successor (term from January 2027) is under way in 2026; there has never been a woman Secretary-General.</li>
<li>Annan's initiatives: Millennium Development Goals (2000), the "Responsibility to Protect" idea, Global Fund (AIDS, TB, malaria).</li>
<li>Shashi Tharoor (India) was runner-up to Ban Ki-moon in 2006.</li>
</ul>` }
